window.LING285_MODULE1 = {"moduleId": "module1", "title": "Module I", "study": {"title": "Study this first · Module I", "lead": "If you study only one page of Module I, study this one. Material that repeated across Lectures 01 to 10 is combined into one topic each, with what to know, the slides and videos beside the notes, and every review and Exam 1 question with its answer.", "askLead": "Every Exam 1 review question and every question from Exam 1 itself, in the way he asks them, plus predicted versions that change one thing, all with answers.", "focusLabel": "Module I focus (review and Exam 1 questions, with answers)"}, "versions": {"module1-v1": {"label": "Module I · Study this first (Lectures 01–10, notes with slides and videos beside them)", "range": "Lectures 01 to 10", "split": true, "notes": true, "howToAnswer": [], "scope": {"label": "On the final", "covers": "Lectures 01 to 10 were Exam 1. The final is cumulative, so all of this comes back, and the questions below are the ones he already asked about it.", "final": "", "evidence": "On Exam 1, about 15 of the 25 questions came almost word for word from the review sheet, with only the sounds, numbers or positions changed. The topics marked Asked on Exam 1 had a question on the exam. The topics marked On the review sheet were on the review but not the exam. Questions marked Predicted change one thing in a real review or exam question. They are predictions, not confirmed exam questions."}, "flags": ["The Exam 1 key is the professor's own. Where an answer here says more than the key, the extra sentences are the slides' explanation of the same answer, not a different answer.", "The uploaded answer companion for the review sheet has unverified authorship. Its answers agree with the slides, and nothing here comes only from it.", "The deck labeled Lectures 05–06 has the internal title Lectures 4+5. Both labels mean the same deck.", "Lecture 08 page 10 and page 9 show two Euler methods. The explicit method uses the old position and the old velocity for both updates, and its swings grow. The position-first method uses the new position for the velocity update, and its swings stay the same size. The slides show both so that you can see the order of the updates matters."], "topics": [{"id": "m1-consonants", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 01 and 02", "title": "Consonants: voicing, place, manner and nasality", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**A consonant is made by narrowing or blocking the air somewhere in the mouth, and voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate while you do it.** Say f as in fat and hold it, then say v as in vat and hold it. The lips and teeth stay in the same place for both, so the narrowing is the same. The difference is that the vocal folds vibrate for v and not for f, so v is **voiced** and f is **voiceless**.", "sub": ["The same pair happens for th in thing and th in that, for s and z, and for sh and the j sound in beige. In each pair the place stays the same and only the voicing changes.", "These sounds are **fricatives** (narrowing sounds), because the air squeezes through a narrow gap and makes a hissing noise."], "end": "So voicing is one dimension of a consonant, and it can change while everything else stays the same.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p9 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**Place of articulation is where in the mouth the narrowing happens, and the fricatives line up from the front of the mouth to the back.** The f and v are **labial** (made with the lower lip against the upper teeth). The two th sounds are **dental** (tongue tip at the teeth). The s and z are **alveolar** (tongue at the ridge just behind the upper teeth). The sh and the beige j sound are **postalveolar** (a little farther back than the ridge).", "sub": ["In the MRI images the narrowing moves from the front of the mouth toward the back as you go across the table."], "end": "So place is the second dimension, and each fricative is one place combined with one voicing.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p10 · Lecture 02 · p5", "main": "**A stop blocks the air completely somewhere in the mouth, and the stops come in groups of three by place.** The p, b and m are made at the lips (labial). The t, d and n are made at the alveolar ridge. The k, g and ng are made with the back of the tongue raised to the **velum** (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth).", "sub": ["Within each group, p, t and k are voiceless and b, d and g are voiced, so p and b are a voicing pair with the same place, and so are t and d, and k and g.", "The m, n and ng are **nasal stops**. The mouth is blocked in the same place as for b, d and g, but the velum is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose. They are all voiced."], "end": "So m and b share place and voicing and differ only in manner, because m lets the air out through the nose and b does not.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p6", "main": "**Every English consonant is one combination of manner, voicing and place.** **Manner** is how the air is restricted, as a fricative (narrowed), an oral stop (blocked and then released through the mouth) or a nasal stop (blocked in the mouth and let out through the nose). **Voicing** is whether the vocal folds vibrate. **Place** is where the restriction is.", "sub": ["Nasality can be counted as a fourth way to tell consonants apart, because it is whether the velum is lowered to let air through the nose.", "A speaker knows this whole system without ever naming its parts, and that is part of what makes knowing a language a remarkable skill."], "end": "So to say how two consonants are similar and different, compare them one dimension at a time, manner, voicing and place.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p6"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 1", "q": "What is the similarity between m in mad and b in bad, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both m and b are voiced, and both are made at the lips, so they share voicing and place of articulation. The difference is manner (how the air is let out). For m the velum (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth) is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose, and m is a nasal stop. For b the velum is raised, so the air is blocked completely at the lips and then released through the mouth, and b is an oral stop (a plosive)."}, {"source": "Review 6", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish between the consonants at the beginning of the words Pam, Bam, Dam, Tam, Kam and Gam?", "a": "All six are oral stops, so they are distinguished by place of articulation and by voicing. Place is where the air is blocked. The p and b are made at the lips (labial), the t and d at the alveolar ridge behind the upper teeth (alveolar), and the k and g with the back of the tongue at the velum (velar). Voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate. The p, t and k are voiceless, and the b, d and g are voiced. So each sound is one place combined with one voicing."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 2", "q": "Name three ways in which we can distinguish consonants from each other.", "a": "Any three of these four. Voicing, which is whether the vocal folds vibrate. Place of articulation, which is where in the mouth the sound is made, such as labial, dental, alveolar, postalveolar or velar. Manner of articulation, which is how the air is constricted, such as fricative, oral stop or nasal stop. Nasality, which is whether the velum is lowered so that the air escapes through the nose."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 6", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the tongue tip is up at the teeth. In (b) the lower lip is against the upper teeth. Which of these is f as in family, and which is th as in thing?", "a": "The f is (b), and the th is (a). This is because f is a labial sound, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth, which is what (b) shows. The th is a dental sound, made with the tongue tip up at the teeth, which is what (a) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q06.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 8", "q": "What is the similarity between n in neck and d in deck, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both are voiced alveolar stops, so they share voicing, place and a complete closure. The difference is that n is a nasal stop, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose. The d is an oral stop, so the air is blocked completely and then released through the mouth."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 19", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the back of the tongue is raised. In (b) the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which of these is k as in Ken, and which is m as in men?", "a": "The k is (a), and the m is (b). This is because k is a velar sound, made with the back of the tongue raised up to the velum, which is what (a) shows. The m is a labial nasal, made with the lips closed and the velum lowered so that air goes through the nose, which is what (b) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q19.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between s in sing and z in zing, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are fricatives made at the alveolar ridge, so they share manner and place. The difference is voicing. For z the vocal folds vibrate, so z is voiced. For s they do not, so s is voiceless."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between t in tap and n in nap, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are stops made at the alveolar ridge, so they share place and a complete closure in the mouth. They differ in voicing and in nasality. The t is voiceless and oral, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth. The n is voiced and nasal, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish the consonants at the beginning of fat, vat, sing and zing?", "a": "All four are fricatives, so they are distinguished by place and by voicing. The f and v are labial, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth. The s and z are alveolar, made with the tongue at the ridge behind the upper teeth. The f and s are voiceless, and the v and z are voiced."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In one the lips are closed and the velum is raised. In the other the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which is b and which is m?", "a": "The b is the one with the velum raised, and the m is the one with the velum lowered. This is because both are made with the lips closed, so the lips do not tell them apart. For m the lowered velum opens the way to the nose, so the air comes out through the nose and m is nasal. For b the raised velum closes that way, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth."}]}, {"id": "m1-vowels", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "title": "Vowels: tongue height, tongue frontness, and more than one frequency", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p12 · Lecture 07 · p10", "main": "**Vowels are told apart by where the tongue is, by its height and by its frontness.** Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth.", "sub": ["The vowel in dean is high and front. The vowel in dune is high and back. The vowel in Dan is low and front. The vowel in Don is low and back. The vowel in done is in the middle.", "Moving the tongue changes where the air space above the vocal folds is narrow and where it is wide."], "end": "So each vowel is a different shape of the air space, made by a different tongue position.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p13 · Lecture 07 · p12", "main": "**The air in a vowel does not vibrate at a single frequency, it vibrates at a combination of frequencies at once.** The illustrative /i/ model adds a slow vibration and a fast one, 1 cycle per second plus 4 cycles per second. The illustrative /a/ model adds 2 cycles per second plus 3 cycles per second. Both start at the same value, but inside each second they move very differently.", "sub": ["Two air slices can be at different points at the same moment, so one can be compressed while the other is spread out.", "The frequencies in the model are made slow on purpose so that the combination can be seen. Real vowel frequencies are hundreds of cycles per second."], "end": "So what makes /i/ sound different from /a/ is which frequencies are combined, not a single frequency.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**The tongue shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and that is why one vowel contains several frequencies.** Where the tongue narrows the air space, that part of the air behaves differently from the part where there is more room. More mass makes a part vibrate more slowly, and more stiffness makes a part vibrate faster.", "sub": ["So the vibrating air has a mix of lower, middle and higher frequencies, and the mix depends on the vowel.", "To hear speech, the ear has to pick that mix apart and tell how strong each frequency is. That is how a listener tells scene from son."], "end": "So vowels connect the tongue, the physics of mass and stiffness, and hearing in one chain.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p4"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 13", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at a combination of frequencies at the same time. This is because the tongue shape changes where the air in the vocal tract is constricted (narrowed), so different parts of the air have different mass and stiffness. Different mass and stiffness means different frequencies, and they all sound together. The combination is what makes one vowel sound different from another."}, {"source": "Review 14", "q": "What are the 2 basic ways we distinguish vowels?", "a": "Vowels are distinguished by tongue height and by tongue frontness. Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth. For example, the vowel in dean is high and front, the vowel in dune is high and back, the vowel in Dan is low and front, and the vowel in Don is low and back."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 9", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in mean and moon?", "a": "Both are high vowels, so the tongue is high in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in mean is a front vowel, and the vowel in moon is a back vowel. For moon the lips are also rounded."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 16", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at several frequencies at once, because the tongue shape gives different parts of the air in the vocal tract different mass and stiffness, so they vibrate at different frequencies together."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in Dan and Don?", "a": "Both are low vowels, so the tongue is low in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in Dan is a front vowel and the vowel in Don is a back vowel."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in dean and Dan?", "a": "Both are front vowels, so the raised part of the tongue is toward the front of the mouth for both. The difference is height. The vowel in dean is high, and the vowel in Dan is low."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does one vowel sound different from another, if both are just air vibrating in the vocal tract?", "a": "The two vowels have different combinations of frequencies. This is because the tongue is in a different place for each vowel, so the air space has a different shape. A different shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and different mass and stiffness mean different frequencies. So the air vibrates at a different mix of frequencies for each vowel, and the ear hears the difference in the mix."}]}, {"id": "m1-system", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 02 and 03", "title": "Speech, the octopus, and what a dynamical system is", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 03 · p3", "main": "**A dynamical system is a set of elements with relationships between them, and their organized interaction produces behavior over time toward a goal.** To understand a behavior, name the elements, name the relationships among them, and name the goal their coordinated activity serves.", "sub": ["A list of parts on its own does not explain the behavior. The relationships are what make the parts act together."], "end": "So the question to ask about any system is what the elements are, how they are related, and what they are for.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p4 · Lecture 03 · p7", "main": "**An octopus keeping its den is a dynamical system made of eight arms, the ground and the fish it slaps at.** The arms interact with the ground and with the fish, so the environment is part of the system. A nervous system spread through the head and the body coordinates the arms.", "sub": ["A study of 19 arm slaps found that the octopus combines simple movements, rotation (turning an arm) and translation (moving it along), one after another and at the same time, to make the complex slap.", "The simple movements are planned ahead and then adjusted by feedback about where the moving fish is."], "end": "So a complex action is built from a few simple movements organized together, and the organization, not any one arm, explains the behavior.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p5 · Lecture 01 · p2 · Lecture 02 · p3", "main": "**Speech is a dynamical system whose elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat.** The brain coordinates the relationships among these parts so that the shape of the vocal tract changes in the right way. The goal is to convey a thought.", "sub": ["In the MRI videos the tongue reshapes while the openings in the mouth and throat change, and almost none of this can be seen from outside.", "Listing the parts does not explain speaking. Their changing relationships produce the movement, the same way the arms and the ground together produce the octopus slap."], "end": "So speech, like the octopus, is explained by organized interaction, not by a list of parts.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p6", "main": "**Very different systems can share the same organization, and that is why studying a simple one helps with a complicated one.** The heart, the pancreas, neurons and voluntary movements can all be studied as dynamical systems, with elements that are organs, cells or chemicals.", "sub": ["Seeing the deep similarity between two systems that look different is creative reasoning, as opposed to treating things that merely look alike as the same."], "end": "So the same questions about elements, relationships and goals work for speech, for the body, and for AI.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p6"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 25", "q": "There are systems all around us. True or false?", "a": "True. A system is a set of elements with relationships between them that together serve a goal, and that describes speech, an octopus, a thermostat, a population, an economy and a neural network."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the elements, the relationships and the goal of the speech system?", "a": "The elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat. The relationships are the coordinated movements among those parts that change the shape of the vocal tract, which the brain organizes. The goal is to convey a thought."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is a list of the speech organs not a complete explanation of speaking?", "a": "A list of the parts leaves out the relationships between them, and the relationships are what produce the behavior. Speaking happens because the tongue, lips, velum and larynx move together in an organized way toward the goal of conveying a thought. So the explanation has to say how the parts interact, not just what they are."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What makes the octopus arm slap a dynamical system, and what does it have in common with speech?", "a": "The arm slap has elements, the eight arms, the ground and the fish, that interact through relationships coordinated by the nervous system, toward the goal of keeping the den. Speech has the same organization, with the tongue, lips, velum and larynx as elements, coordinated by the brain, toward the goal of conveying a thought. In both, the behavior comes from the organized interaction of the parts, not from any one part on its own."}]}, {"id": "m1-closure", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02 and 03", "title": "Closure, the cloze task and how GPT learns", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p9", "main": "**Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar pattern that is unfinished.** A broken circle is seen as a whole circle, because familiarity with the shape lets the mind close the gap. Three black disks with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, even though no triangle is drawn.", "sub": ["Cloze is pronounced like close and is named after closure. It takes the same idea from shapes to language. The reader uses the words that remain to fill in the word that is missing."], "end": "So closure means filling in what is missing from what is already known.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p10", "main": "**The cloze procedure deletes words from a message and measures how well people can restore them.** In \"Chickens cackle and ___ quack,\" the missing word is ducks. Each correct restoration counts as one cloze unit.", "sub": ["Filling the gap takes more than knowing one word. It takes the meanings of the words, the way chickens goes with cackle and ducks with quack, and the parallel shape of the sentence.", "Wilson Taylor introduced the procedure in 1953 as a way to measure how readable a text is."], "end": "So knowledge can be tested through the ability to complete a sequence, and that is how a language model shows what it knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p10", "main": "**GPT learns by doing the cloze task billions of times.** It takes a sentence, hides part of it, and makes the network guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of completions, the weights hold the knowledge of the language.", "sub": ["Completing a sequence is predicting what fits the context, and people do the same thing when they expect what comes next.", "Whether this counts as knowledge is a debate. One view is that the system's knowledge is not so different from ours, and a skeptical view, associated with Yann LeCun, says it is."], "end": "So closure is the principle, the cloze task is the method, and prediction is what the system learns to do.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 7", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, and how it is used in the GPT system.", "a": "Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar but unfinished pattern. A broken circle is still seen as a circle, and three black circles with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, because the mind fills in what is missing. GPT uses the same idea as its way of learning, which is called the cloze task. A sentence is taken, part of it is hidden, and the network has to guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of these completions the network ends up knowing the language."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 11", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, illustrated by the figure of three black circles with wedges cut out, and how it is used in the GPT system as a principle for learning.", "a": "Humans are very good at filling in missing information using what we already know. In the figure no triangle is actually drawn, but we see one anyway because the brain completes the shape. GPT learns the same way, with the cloze task. It takes a sentence, pretends that part of the sentence is missing, and asks the network to complete what is missing. If the guess is wrong, the weights get adjusted. After billions of these completions the system ends up knowing the language.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q11.png", "alt": "Three black circles with wedges cut out that make a triangle appear"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A sentence has a word hidden, and a language model guesses the wrong word. What happens next, and why does this count as learning?", "a": "The wrong guess gets compared with the real missing word, and the difference is the mistake. The weights are then changed a little in the direction that would have made the right word more likely. This counts as learning because the weights hold the knowledge, so changing them changes what the network knows. After billions of these corrections the guesses become right."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a broken circle and the sentence Chickens cackle and ___ quack have in common?", "a": "In both, something is missing and the mind fills it in from what is already there. The circle is completed from familiarity with its shape, and the sentence is completed with ducks from the meanings of the other words and the parallel pattern of the sentence. Both are closure, which is completing a familiar but unfinished pattern."}]}, {"id": "m1-output", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 09 and 10", "title": "What a neural network outputs: a probability distribution and a decision", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10 · Lecture 10 · p3", "main": "**A neural network takes in a signal as a list of numbers and gives out a probability distribution over the possible identities.** A face image can be 48 million pixel numbers, and the question is which of 1000 people it is. A speech wave is a list of pressure numbers, and the question is which of 25 sounds it is.", "sub": ["For five vowels in the order read, rad, rude, Rud, rod, the output [0.2, 0.2, 0.4, 0.2, 0] gives 20 percent to read, 20 percent to rad, 40 percent to rude, 20 percent to Rud and 0 to rod.", "The decision is the category with the largest number, so here the decision is rude."], "end": "So the output is not one answer but a set of probabilities, and the decision is the biggest one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10", "main": "**A probability distribution is a list where every number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1.** A list that adds up to 0, or to 1.5, is not a distribution, and neither is a list with a negative number in it.", "sub": ["The list only means something together with the agreed order of the categories. The same numbers in a different order would be a different decision.", "For example, [.4, 0, .5, .2] is not a good output for four categories, because the numbers add up to 1.1."], "end": "So to check an output, check that each number is between 0 and 1 and that the total is exactly 1.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p11 · Lecture 10 · p12", "main": "**The true answer is given to the network as a distribution with all of the probability on the correct category.** If the correct vowel is Rud, the fourth of five, the target is [0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. The network's own output might be [0.4, 0.1, 0.2, 0.2, 0.1], which puts the most probability on read.", "sub": ["Comparing the output with the target gives the error, which is also called the loss. Training changes the weights to make that error smaller.", "The input numbers can be positive or negative and are not probabilities. Only the output is a probability distribution."], "end": "So the target is the same kind of list as the output, with a 1 in the right place and 0 everywhere else.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p11 · Lecture 03 · p9", "main": "**For a language model the categories are the possible next words, so the output is a probability for each word.** Each input word is a vector (a list of numbers), for example 1,024 numbers per word. The model uses the whole sequence, chickens, cackle, and, ducks, to predict the next word.", "sub": ["The output is another vector, for example 10,000 numbers, one for each possible next word such as a, cat, quack and Zulu. The number for quack should be the biggest."], "end": "So a language model's decision is the next word with the highest probability.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 11", "q": "What is the output of an LLM or a neural network in general, and how does it express a decision?", "a": "The output is a probability distribution over the possible categories. That is a list with one number per category, where each number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1. The decision is the category with the highest probability. For an LLM the categories are the possible next words, so the output says how likely each word is to come next."}, {"source": "Review 12", "q": "Is this a good output of a neural network, if there are 4 categories, [.4, 0, .5, .2]? Why or why not?", "a": "No, it is not a good output. This is because the output has to be a probability distribution, so the numbers have to add up to 1. These numbers add up to 1.1. Each number is between 0 and 1, so that part is fine, but the total is wrong."}, {"source": "Review 20", "q": "The decision by a neural network is [.1, .1, .1, .2, .5], but the true category is the one in the second position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, as a probability distribution, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is written as [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]. This is because the correct category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and every other category gets 0. The 1 goes in the second position because that is where the true category is. The list still adds up to 1, so it is a probability distribution, and the network can compare its own output with it to find its mistake."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 1", "q": "Which of these four could be the probability distribution decision of a neural network? (a) [0, 0, 0, 0], (b) [0, .4, 0, .6], (c) [-.5, 0, .5, 0], (d) [.3, .3, .4, .5].", "a": "Only (b) is possible. This is because the numbers in a probability distribution have to be between 0 and 1 and have to add up to 1. List (a) adds up to 0. List (c) has a negative number. List (d) adds up to 1.5. List (b) has numbers between 0 and 1 that add up to 1."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 23", "q": "The decision made by a neural network is [.4, .1, .1, .3, .1], but the true category is the one in the last position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, in a probability distribution format, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 0, 0, 1]. This is because the true category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and the other four categories get 0. The 1 goes in the last position because that is where the true category is."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Is [.2, .3, .5] a good output of a neural network with three categories? Is [.5, .5, .5]?", "a": "The first one is good, because each number is between 0 and 1 and they add up to 1. The second one is not good, because the numbers add up to 1.5. A probability distribution has to add up to exactly 1."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A network's output is [.1, .6, .2, .1] and the true category is the third one. Write the true answer as a probability distribution. Is the network's decision right?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 1, 0], with all the probability on the third category. The network's decision is wrong, because its biggest number, .6, is in the second position, so it decided on the second category. The gap between its output and the target is the error that training uses to fix the weights."}]}, {"id": "m1-weights", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 09 and 10", "title": "Knowledge in the weights: inner products and forward propagation", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p12 · Lecture 02 · p13", "main": "**A network's knowledge is stored in its weights, which are the numbers that determine how the inputs get turned into output probabilities.** The input words are vectors called x's, and the weights are called w's. The computation that combines them uses only adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing, but a very large interconnected system of these operations can do something complex.", "sub": ["Some large AI systems have trillions of weights. The brain stores the same kind of knowledge in its synapses (the connections between neurons), about 150 trillion of them.", "Changing the weights changes which next words get high or low probability for the same input."], "end": "So learning means changing the weights, because that is where the knowledge is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p6", "main": "**Each neuron multiplies every incoming activation by its own weight and adds the products, and that sum is the inner product.** An **activation** is the number coming in from a neuron in the layer before. A **weight** is the number on one connection, and it can be positive or negative.", "sub": ["With inputs of minus 1, 1 and 3 and weights of minus 2, 3 and 1, the products are 2, 3 and 3, and the sum is 8.", "A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight changes it. For example, an input of 3 with a weight of 2 gives 6, and the same input with a weight of minus 2 gives minus 6.", "The inner product is also called the dot product or the scalar product. It is the core calculation of all neural networks."], "end": "So a neuron with 5 inputs has 5 weights, and its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w5 times x5.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p15 · Lecture 09 · p13", "main": "**The inner product is a way of comparing two lists of numbers, so the weights act as templates that the input is matched against.** With an input of [minus 1, 3, minus 2], the template [minus 2, 0, 3] gives 2 plus 0 minus 6, which is minus 4. The template [minus 2, 2, minus 1] gives 2 plus 6 plus 2, which is 10.", "sub": ["A big positive inner product means a strong match, and a value near zero means little match, so the second template matches better.", "In the templatic theory of face perception, the brain compares an incoming face with stored templates, and the most similar template decides who it is. The weights play the part of the templates."], "end": "So before learning the templates are random and the matches are poor, and training makes the templates useful.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p9 · Lecture 10 · p10", "main": "**Forward propagation is the calculation that runs from the input layer to the output layer using the current weights.** A network with 5 inputs, hidden layers of 3, 4 and 3 neurons, and 7 outputs has every neuron connected to every neuron in the next layer, which is 60 connections, each with its own weight.", "sub": ["Each neuron in the first hidden layer takes the inner product of the 5 inputs with its 5 weights. Those 3 activations become the inputs to the next layer, which does the same, and so on through the layers.", "With inputs [2, 1, 3, 4, 2] and weights [2, minus 1, 1, 3, minus 2], the middle hidden neuron gets 4 minus 1 plus 3 plus 12 minus 4, which is 14. With inputs [minus 2, 1, 3, minus 4, 2] and weights [3, minus 2, 1, 2, 5] the sum is minus 6 minus 2 plus 3 minus 8 plus 10, which is minus 3. Hidden activations can be negative.", "The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution, for example [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08], and the fourth category, at 0.30, is the decision."], "end": "So the output depends on the inputs and on every weight along the way, and the signs matter at every step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p14 · Lecture 09 · p16 · Lecture 10 · p5", "main": "**Perception works in stages, and a network copies that by turning each layer's output into the next layer's input.** In the visual system, areas V1, V2, V3 and V4 and the areas after them each transform the information and pass it on, with connections running forward and back.", "sub": ["**Inference** is using the current weights to compute an output for an input. **Learning** is changing the weights. A training step has to run inference first, to get the output it will compare with the correct answer, so inference is part of learning.", "Like Euler's method, a network builds a complex result out of many repeated simple calculations."], "end": "So a hierarchy of template comparisons, layer after layer, is what a neural network is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 8", "q": "How is knowledge represented in neural networks?", "a": "Knowledge is represented in the weights, which are the numbers on the connections between neurons. The weights determine how the input numbers get turned into the output probabilities. Changing the weights changes what the network knows, so learning means changing the weights. In the brain the same kind of knowledge sits in the synapses (the connections between neurons), and a large AI system can have trillions of weights."}, {"source": "Review 19", "q": "What is the math of forward propagation, from the signal input to a neural network all the way to a decision at the end? Describe it.", "a": "The input signal is a vector, which is a list of numbers, such as pressure values from a sound wave. Each neuron in the first layer multiplies each input number by its own weight and adds the products up. That sum is the inner product, and it is the neuron's activation. The activations of that layer become the inputs to the next layer, where every neuron does the same multiply-and-add with its own weights. This repeats layer by layer until the last layer. The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution over the categories, so the numbers are between 0 and 1 and add up to 1. The decision is the category with the largest probability."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 21", "q": "Imagine a neural network with many neurons in many layers. One neuron, call it y, in layer 4 is connected to 5 neurons from the previous layer. Every neuron is connected to all the neurons in the previous layer. How many weights does y have?", "a": "Neuron y has 5 weights. This is because there is one weight on each connection coming into a neuron, and y has 5 connections coming in, one from each of the 5 neurons in the previous layer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 22", "q": "Continuing from the previous question, y's weights are w1, w2, w3, w4 and w5, and the activations of the 5 neurons feeding it are x1, x2, x3, x4 and x5. How do we write the activation of neuron y?", "a": "The activation of y is w1 times x1, plus w2 times x2, plus w3 times x3, plus w4 times x4, plus w5 times x5. This is because each incoming activation is multiplied by its own weight, and then all five products are added up. That sum is the inner product."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron in layer 3 is connected to 7 neurons in layer 2. How many weights does it have, and how is its activation written?", "a": "It has 7 weights, one on each incoming connection. Its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w7 times x7, where the x's are the 7 activations coming in and the w's are the 7 weights. Each input is multiplied by its own weight and the products are added."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron has inputs 2, minus 1 and 3 with weights 1, 4 and minus 2. What is its activation, and what is this calculation called?", "a": "Its activation is minus 8. This is because the products are 2 times 1, which is 2, then minus 1 times 4, which is minus 4, then 3 times minus 2, which is minus 6, and 2 minus 4 minus 6 is minus 8. The calculation is the inner product, which multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between inference and learning in a neural network?", "a": "Inference uses the weights the network has now to compute an output for an input. Learning changes the weights. The two are linked, because a learning step first runs inference to get the current output, then compares it with the correct answer, then changes the weights to shrink the difference. So inference is part of every learning step, but only the weight change is learning."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can an input of 3 end up pushing a neuron's activation down?", "a": "It pushes the activation down when its weight is negative. This is because the input is multiplied by its weight, so 3 times a negative weight such as minus 2 gives minus 6, which lowers the sum. The sign of the weight decides whether an input adds to the activation or subtracts from it."}]}, {"id": "m1-sgd", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03 and 10", "title": "Stochastic gradient descent: guess, mistake, correct", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p16 · Lecture 03 · p12", "main": "**Stochastic gradient descent learns a quantity, such as the average of a list, by starting with a guess and correcting it from one observation at a time.** Adding up three numbers and dividing by three gives their average directly, but speech, language and scenes cannot be learned by adding a few numbers. So a different way of learning the average is used as a model of learning in general.", "sub": ["Start with a guess. Compare it with one value from the list. Move the guess part of the way toward that value. Repeat with another value.", "Each correction happens because a new observation shows a discrepancy (a gap) between the guess and the data."], "end": "So learning here means being corrected by observations, not being told the answer.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p17 · Lecture 03 · p13 · Lecture 03 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p15", "main": "**Each step subtracts a fraction of the mistake from the current estimate, and the fraction is the learning rate.** The data are [78, 67, 98, 85, 76, 68, 98, 72]. The estimate starts at 130, which is an arbitrary guess. A random observation, 98, is drawn. The mistake, or **loss**, is the estimate minus the observation, 130 minus 98, which is 32.", "sub": ["With a learning rate of 10 percent, the estimate moves down by a tenth of 32, so it goes from 130 to 126.8. With a learning rate of 25 percent it moves down by a quarter of 32, to 122. Either way it moves toward 98 without jumping all the way to it.", "**Stochastic** means the observation is picked at random. **Gradient** means the direction that reduces the mistake. **Descent** means the mistake goes down, not that the estimate always goes down."], "end": "So a partial correction lets each observation count without throwing away what the estimate already knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/017.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p17"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p23 · Lecture 03 · p27", "main": "**The first minus in the update makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the estimate.** When the estimate is 94.672 and the observation is 98, the difference is minus 3.328. Subtracting a negative number raises the estimate, so it goes up to 95.504.", "sub": ["When the estimate is above the observation, the difference is positive, so subtracting it lowers the estimate. When the estimate is below, the difference is negative, so subtracting it raises the estimate.", "That is why the path of the estimate jitters. It mostly falls from 130 toward 80, but it rises whenever a high observation such as 98 is drawn."], "end": "So one step can move away from the true average, and learning is judged by the whole run, not by one step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p28 · Lecture 03 · p29", "main": "**The loss goes down whenever the gap between the estimate and the target shrinks, whether the estimate itself is going up or going down.** The loss is half of the squared gap, so it is never negative and a big gap counts much more than a small one.", "sub": ["Starting at 140 with a target of 80, the estimate falls and the loss falls. Starting at minus 100, the estimate rises and the loss still falls, because the gap is shrinking.", "Descent describes the loss, not the estimate."], "end": "So the estimate can rise while the learning is still going downhill.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/028.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p28"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p10 · Lecture 03 · p11", "main": "**Fake it till you make it means the network starts with random weights, so it knows nothing, and gets its knowledge by being corrected.** For chickens cackle and ducks, random weights can give the highest probability to the wrong word. The target puts all the probability on quack, and the gap between them tells training which way to change the weights.", "sub": ["The two principles are to start with a guess and to improve the guess by learning from mistakes.", "Random guessing on its own creates no knowledge. The repeated correction is what turns the guess into knowledge."], "end": "So the system fakes it at the start, and after enough corrections it makes it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p12 · Lecture 10 · p13", "main": "**Training a network is the same loop run with a labelled input. Compute the output, compare it with the target, and change the weights to shrink the error.** For an input whose correct category is the sixth of seven, the target is [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. An output of [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08] puts only 0.12 on the right category, and the gap is the error.", "sub": ["**Backpropagation** carries the output error back through the layers so that every weight can be adjusted with SGD. Repeating this over billions of labelled pairs builds the network's knowledge.", "A mistake in the data gets learned too. In a list of grades with a typo of 400, the estimate is pulled toward 400 and the learned average comes out near 100 instead of near 80. Correct arithmetic does not fix a wrong observation."], "end": "So the loop is guess, measure the mistake, correct a fraction, and repeat, and it learns whatever the data contains, errors included.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p12"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 18", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula? Consider the case where the data point is above the current x and the case where it is below.", "a": "The first minus makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the current estimate. If the current estimate is above the data point, the difference in the parentheses is positive, so subtracting it makes the estimate go down, toward the data point. If the current estimate is below the data point, the difference is negative, so subtracting a negative number makes the estimate go up, again toward the data point. For example, with an estimate of 130 and a data point of 98, the difference is 32, so the estimate goes down. With an estimate of 94 and a data point of 98, the difference is minus 4, so the estimate goes up."}, {"source": "Review 10", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I describe it this way.", "a": "The system starts with completely random weights, so at first it does not know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, gets feedback on how wrong the guess was, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this many times it makes it, which means it ends up with the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 7", "q": "Describe how the stochastic gradient descent algorithm works in your own words.", "a": "We want to estimate a quantity from some existing data, like the average of a list of numbers. We start with a random estimate. Then we pick a random data point and compute the loss, which is the difference between our guess and that data point. We subtract a small fraction of the loss from the guess, and that fraction is the learning rate. That gives a new, slightly better guess. We repeat this with new random data points over and over, and the guess gets closer and closer to the real answer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 15", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I put it this way.", "a": "The system starts out with completely random weights, so it does not actually know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, receives feedback, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this a lot of times, it makes it, which means it has the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 20", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula?", "a": "The minus makes the estimate move toward the data point, whether the data point is above or below the estimate. If the estimate is above the data point, the loss is positive, so subtracting it pushes the estimate down. If the estimate is below the data point, the loss is negative, so subtracting it pushes the estimate up."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The current estimate is 60 and the next data point is 90, with a learning rate of 10 percent. Which way does the estimate move, and by how much?", "a": "The estimate moves up, to 63. This is because the difference, 60 minus 90, is minus 30. Subtracting 10 percent of minus 30, which is minus 3, raises the estimate by 3. The minus in the formula makes the estimate move toward the data point, which is above it."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Can the SGD estimate move away from the true average on a single step? Why?", "a": "Yes. This is because each step moves the estimate toward the one data point that was drawn, not toward the average. If the estimate is 94 and the data point drawn is 98, the estimate goes up even though the average is 80. Over many steps the moves average out and the estimate settles near 80."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A list of exam grades has one typo, 400, in it. What happens when SGD learns the average from this list?", "a": "The learned average comes out too high, near 100 instead of near 80. This is because the step that draws 400 pulls the estimate strongly toward 400, and SGD has no way to know that the observation is wrong. The arithmetic is correct, but it learns the mistake in the data along with everything else."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the words stochastic, gradient and descent each mean in the name of the algorithm?", "a": "Stochastic means the data point is picked at random each step. Gradient means the direction of change that reduces the mistake. Descent means the mistake goes down over the run. Descent does not mean the estimate itself always goes down, because the estimate rises whenever it is below the data point."}]}, {"id": "m1-rate", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 04 and 07", "title": "Learning rate and stiffness: the speed and accuracy tradeoff", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p18 · Lecture 03 · p30 · Lecture 07 · p15", "main": "**A bigger learning rate makes the estimate reach the right region faster but keeps it jumping around once it is there.** Three runs start at 130, at 80 and at 10, and all head toward the average near 80. At 1 percent the runs are smooth but slow, and after 300 steps the run from 10 is still below 80. At 10 percent they arrive fairly fast and then wobble a little.", "sub": ["At 50 percent the runs arrive quickly but make big jumps around 80. At 80 percent each observation throws the estimate a long way, so it keeps bouncing over a wide range.", "A big rate gives each observation a lot of influence, so an estimate near the average is easily pulled away by one data point. A small rate gives little jitter but needs many observations to travel far from a bad start."], "end": "So the run that starts near the answer does fine with a small rate, and a run that starts far away needs a bigger rate to get there in time.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p18"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p31", "main": "**This is the speed and accuracy tradeoff.** Raising the learning rate raises the speed and lowers the accuracy, because the estimate reacts more to each observation and keeps fluctuating. Lowering the learning rate lowers the speed and raises the accuracy, because each correction is small and the fluctuations near the average are small.", "sub": ["Optimization research looks for methods that get both speed and accuracy. The tradeoff describes the problem those methods try to solve."], "end": "So no single learning rate is both the fastest and the most accurate, and the choice is a compromise.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18 · Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**Stiffness does for a spring what the learning rate does for SGD, because both set how strongly the system corrects a discrepancy.** A viscous spring with no mass follows the rule that the change in position is a negative number times the position, so a positive position falls toward zero and a negative one rises toward zero. A bigger number in that rule, which is more stiffness, means a faster return.", "sub": ["Hot coffee cools toward room temperature and iced coffee warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is big and slows as the gap shrinks, so different starts reach the same equilibrium from opposite sides.", "The SGD estimate does the same, except its path is noisy because each observation is random, while the coffee curve is smooth."], "end": "So a high learning rate and a high stiffness both mean a fast correction, and a low learning rate and a low stiffness both mean a slow one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 2", "q": "What is the role of the learning rate in stochastic gradient descent and the role of stiffness in a viscous springy system? What happens when the learning rate is high versus low, and when stiffness is high versus low?", "a": "The learning rate determines how big a fraction of each mistake gets corrected, and stiffness determines how strongly the spring pulls back toward equilibrium (its resting position). With a high learning rate, each correction is large, so the estimate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, so it is less accurate. With a low learning rate, each correction is small, so the estimate moves slowly but settles steadily, so it is more accurate but slow. With high stiffness, the spring pulls back hard, so it returns to equilibrium fast. With low stiffness, it pulls back weakly, so it returns slowly. In both systems a bigger setting means a faster response."}, {"source": "Review 9", "q": "What is the basic tradeoff that we see in both the shortest path problem and the stochastic gradient descent algorithm?", "a": "The tradeoff is speed against accuracy. In the shortest path problem, the greedy algorithm is fast but can pick the wrong path, and the exhaustive algorithm always finds the right path but is slow. Dynamic programming balances the two. In stochastic gradient descent, a high learning rate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, and a low learning rate settles accurately but slowly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 10", "q": "Imagine a stochastic gradient descent algorithm with a very low learning rate, and a springy system with no mass and a low stiffness. (a) Describe how the SGD estimate and the spring's position evolve with time. (b) How would the evolution in time differ if the learning rate and the stiffness increased? For SGD, ignore accuracy and oscillation.", "a": "Part (a). Both move toward their goal slowly. With a low learning rate, the SGD estimate only takes a small correction toward the average each time, so it takes many steps to get there. With low stiffness, the spring only pulls weakly, so it drifts back to its equilibrium position slowly.\n\nPart (b). Both would get there faster. A higher learning rate makes bigger corrections toward the average, so the estimate arrives in fewer steps. A stiffer spring pulls harder, so it snaps back to equilibrium more quickly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two SGD runs use the same data. One has a learning rate of 1 percent and the other 80 percent. Describe how each estimate moves over time.", "a": "The 1 percent run moves slowly and smoothly toward the average, and if it starts far away it may not have arrived after hundreds of steps. The 80 percent run reaches the region of the average very fast, but then each observation throws it a long way, so it keeps bouncing around instead of settling. The small rate is accurate but slow, and the big rate is fast but inaccurate."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A spring with no mass is released from a stretched position. Compare a stiff spring with a loose one.", "a": "Both return to equilibrium and stop there without crossing it. The stiff spring returns fast, because it pulls back hard, so the position drops toward zero quickly. The loose spring returns slowly, because it pulls back weakly. Neither one overshoots, because there is no mass to carry the motion past equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a cooling cup of coffee and an SGD estimate have in common?", "a": "Both move toward a goal by correcting a discrepancy, and the correction is big when the gap is big and small when the gap is small. The coffee's temperature moves toward room temperature, and the estimate moves toward the data. The difference is that the coffee curve is smooth, while the estimate's path jitters because each observation is random."}]}, {"id": "m1-paths", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "title": "Shortest paths: greedy, exhaustive and dynamic programming", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p3", "main": "**A shortest path problem asks for the cheapest way from the start state to the goal state through a series of stages.** The circles are states, the arrows are the allowed moves, and the number on an arrow is the cost of that move. From A you can go to B for 12 or to C for 7, and the stages continue through D, E and F, then G, H and I, then J.", "sub": ["A route's total is the sum of every arrow it follows. A cheap first arrow does not make a route cheap, because later arrows can undo its advantage."], "end": "So the quantity that matters is the total cost of the whole route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p4", "main": "**The greedy algorithm takes the cheapest arrow out of whatever state it is in, and it can lose.** From A it takes C, because 7 is less than 12. From C it takes E, because 10 is the smallest. From E it takes H, because 7 is the smallest. Then H to J costs 9, and the route totals 33.", "sub": ["Greedy is fast, because it only looks at the next arrow. It is inaccurate, because it ignores the costs that are still ahead, and an expensive first step can lead to a cheaper whole route."], "end": "So greedy is fast but can pick the wrong route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p6", "main": "**The exhaustive algorithm works out the total of every complete route and picks the cheapest, so it is always right and very slow.** For this problem it finds the true minimum, because it compares every route.", "sub": ["Every added state or stage multiplies the number of complete routes, so the work grows very fast. The routes overlap, so the same additions get done over and over."], "end": "So exhaustive search is accurate but inefficient.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p7 · Lecture 04 · p8", "main": "**Dynamic programming breaks the big problem into smaller problems that it contains, solves the smallest first, and reuses their answers.** Call the best cost of finishing from B the value of B, and the same for C. If those two values are known, the decision at A is the smaller of 12 plus the value of B and 7 plus the value of C.", "sub": ["A **value** is the cost of the whole remaining problem from a state, not the cost of one arrow. The values of B and C are unknown at first, so each one becomes a smaller problem, the cheapest way to finish from that state.", "Breaking problems into smaller problems is Bellman's idea, and it keeps going until the remaining problem is trivial."], "end": "So a hard problem is solved by solving the easy problems inside it and building back up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p9 · Lecture 04 · p10 · Lecture 04 · p13 · Lecture 04 · p16", "main": "**The values are worked out backward from the goal, and then the route is followed forward from the start.** The last states have values that are just their final arrow, so G is 5, H is 9 and I is 8. For D, compare 8 plus 5, 7 plus 9 and 10 plus 8, and the smallest, 13, is the value of D. The same rule gives E the value 14 and F the value 15.", "sub": ["Then B is 18 through D and C is 24 through E. At A the choice is 12 plus 18 through B, which is 30, or 7 plus 24 through C, which is 31. So the best route is A to B to D to G to J, costing 30, even though its first arrow is the expensive one.", "The rule at every state is the same. For each possible next state, add the cost of getting there to that state's value, and keep the smallest sum. Remember which next state gave it, so the route can be read off forward afterward."], "end": "So the calculation runs backward from the goal, and the route is lived forward from the start.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p11 · Lecture 04 · p12", "main": "**Dynamic programming is more efficient than exhaustive search because the routes overlap, and it solves each overlapping part only once.** Exhaustive search redoes the same additions for every route through a state. Dynamic programming finds the best way to finish from each state once and reuses it for every route that passes through.", "sub": ["A costly arrow far ahead, like the 9 from H to J, is carried backward into the values, so the choice at A already knows about it.", "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, exhaustive is accurate but slow, and dynamic programming is the balance."], "end": "So the value of a state summarizes the whole future, and that is what lets a local choice be a good one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 5", "q": "Why is the exhaustive algorithm so inefficient for solving the shortest path problem?", "a": "The exhaustive algorithm is inefficient because it works out the total cost of every complete path separately. The paths overlap, so the same additions get done over and over. Every time a stage is added, the number of complete paths multiplies, so the work grows very fast. It does find the true shortest path, but for a big problem it takes far too long."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 4", "q": "Describe how dynamic programming works in terms of the size of problems, and problems containing other problems.", "a": "You break the big problem into smaller subproblems, and keep breaking those down until you reach small problems that are trivial to solve. You solve those smallest problems first. Then you use their answers to solve the larger problems that contain them, and keep going up until you reach the hardest problem, which is the original one."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 5", "q": "Describe why dynamic programming is more efficient than the exhaustive algorithm.", "a": "The paths in a shortest path problem overlap. The exhaustive algorithm solves every path separately, so it redoes the same additions over and over. Dynamic programming solves only the non-overlapping parts. It finds the best solution from each state only once and reuses it for every path through that state, so it never repeats work."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 12", "q": "In the shortest path problem shown, what is the greedy forward solution? Write out the numbers with plus signs.", "a": "The greedy forward solution is 3 plus 3 plus 4 plus 2, which is 12, along the path A to B to E to F to H. This is because greedy takes the cheapest arrow out of each state as it goes. From A the cheapest arrow is 3 to B. From B it is 3 to E. From E it is 4 to F. From F the only arrow is 2 to H. The greedy total is 12, and it is not the best path. The cheapest complete path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 13", "q": "In the same shortest path problem, we call the best cost from any state x to state H V x H. Imagine we have already solved for V D H and V E H. How can I calculate V C H?", "a": "V C H is the smaller of two sums, 2 plus V D H and 1 plus V E H. This is because from C you can only go to D, which costs 2, or to E, which costs 1. Each sum adds the cost of that first step to the best cost of finishing from the state you land in. Taking the minimum is the important part, because the best cost from C is the cheaper of the two complete ways of finishing.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In a shortest path problem, what is the difference between the cost of an arrow and the value of a state?", "a": "The cost of an arrow is the price of one move. The value of a state is the cheapest total cost of finishing from that state all the way to the goal, which adds up several arrows. Dynamic programming uses values, because a value already includes every cost that is still ahead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does dynamic programming work backward from the goal?", "a": "It works backward because a state's value can only be computed once the values of the states after it are known. The last states are easy, because their value is just their final arrow. Each earlier state then adds one arrow to a known value and keeps the smallest sum. Once every value is known, the route is read off forward from the start."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, the greedy path from A to H cost 12. What is the cheapest path, and why does greedy miss it?", "a": "The cheapest path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11. Greedy misses it because the first arrow to C costs 5, which is more than the 3 to B, so greedy never goes to C. The cheap arrows after C, 2 and 1, are what make the whole route cheaper, and greedy cannot see them from A.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, we know the values V F H and V G H. How do we calculate V D H?", "a": "V D H is the smaller of 3 plus V F H and 1 plus V G H. This is because from D you can go to F for 3 or to G for 1, and each sum adds that first step to the best cost of finishing from where you land. The minimum of the two is the best cost from D.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}]}, {"id": "m1-least-time", "tier": "Possible", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "title": "Least distance is not least time", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p14", "main": "**The fastest route is not always the shortest route, because the cost of a stretch depends on the conditions on it.** On uniform sand, where speed is the same everywhere, the straight line is both shortest and fastest. A rescuer who runs faster on sand than they swim in water should run farther along the sand before entering the water, so the fastest route bends at the shore.", "sub": ["The extra distance on the fast sand saves more time than it costs, because it shortens the slow swim.", "This is the same idea as paying the expensive 12 to B in the shortest path problem, because the cheaper continuation more than pays it back."], "end": "So the thing to minimize is the total time or total cost of the whole route, not the length of its first stretch.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p14"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p15", "main": "**Light takes the least-time route too, and that is why it bends at the boundary between air and water.** Light moves faster in air than in water, so its path changes direction at the surface, the way the rescuer's path changes direction at the shore. A pencil in a glass of water looks bent because the light reaching the eye followed a bent path.", "sub": ["The pencil itself is straight. The bend is in the path of the light."], "end": "So a locally shorter stretch is not always best when the rest of the route runs under different conditions.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can the fastest route be longer than the shortest route?", "a": "Because the route crosses regions where the speed is different. Spending extra distance in the fast region shortens the distance in the slow region, and the time saved there is bigger than the time added. So the route with the least total time bends toward the fast region and is longer than the straight line."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a lifeguard running to a swimmer, a ray of light entering water, and the shortest path problem have in common?", "a": "In all three, the best choice for the whole route is not the cheapest first step. The lifeguard runs farther on sand because swimming is slow. Light bends at the water because it travels slower in water. In the path problem the expensive first arrow leads to the cheaper total. What is minimized is the total time or total cost over the whole route."}]}, {"id": "m1-oscillator", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "title": "Position and velocity: why a mass on a spring oscillates", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p3 · Lecture 07 · p2", "main": "**A harmonic oscillator has two quantities that change each other, position x and velocity v.** Position is where the mass is relative to its resting position. Velocity is how fast the position is changing. A dot over a quantity means its rate of change, so x dot is how position changes and v dot is how velocity changes.", "sub": ["The first rule is that x dot equals v. Velocity determines how position changes, and the sign stays the same, so positive velocity makes position go up.", "The second rule is that v dot equals minus x. Position determines how velocity changes, with the sign reversed, so positive position makes velocity go down and negative position makes velocity go up."], "end": "So position and velocity keep changing each other through a feedback loop.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p4 · Lecture 05–06 · p5", "main": "**The rules say which way a quantity is changing, not whether the quantity is positive right now.** When v is plus 5, position rises. When v is minus 5, position falls. When x is plus 5, velocity goes down. When x is minus 5, velocity goes up.", "sub": ["Velocity going up can mean going from a big negative number toward zero, which is slowing down, not speeding up in the same direction.", "Keeping the quantity and its rate of change separate is what makes the back and forth motion understandable."], "end": "So read each rule as a direction of change.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p6 · Lecture 05–06 · p7 · Lecture 07 · p3", "main": "**Following the two rules around the loop gives repeated motion, not a single return to the middle.** Start with a positive position and zero velocity. Positive x makes velocity become negative. Negative velocity makes x fall. As x nears zero the push on velocity weakens, but the velocity is still negative, so it carries x through zero to the negative side.", "sub": ["Now negative x makes velocity rise. Velocity passes through zero and becomes positive, so x turns around and rises back through zero. Then positive x starts pushing velocity down again, and the cycle repeats.", "Position and velocity are coordinated but do not peak together. Position is at a turning point when velocity is zero, and velocity is biggest when position passes through zero.", "The two arrows, from x to v and from v to x, close the loop, so one change leads to another and back again. That is what oscillation is."], "end": "So two simple rules, each the cause of the other, produce vibration.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p4 · Lecture 07 · p7", "main": "**A spring and a mass make vibration because the spring wants to return to equilibrium and the moving mass wants to keep moving.** The spring's goal is to get back to its resting position, and **stiffness** is how strongly it pulls back. A mass in motion stays in motion unless something changes it, which is **inertia**. The spring can pull the mass back to the middle, but the mass arrives moving, so it goes through, and the spring has to slow it and turn it around from the other side.", "sub": ["The same back and forth shows up in a car after a bump, in music, in boom and bust, in the SGD estimate swinging around the average, in the air in the vocal tract and in the vocal folds."], "end": "So the goal of the spring plus the inertia of the mass is the whole explanation of oscillation.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p8 · Lecture 08 · p3", "main": "**A spring on its own returns to equilibrium and stops, and a spring with a mass overshoots and keeps going.** Pull a spring alone to plus 1 and let go, and it comes back toward 0 and flattens out without crossing. Push it to minus 1 and it rises toward 0 without crossing. Attach a mass, release it from plus 1, and it moves to 0 and keeps going, because it is already moving. The spring is then compressed, pulls back, and turns the mass around, and the mass carries it through the next crossing too.", "sub": ["The trace of the mass on the spring crosses zero again and again, with each swing a little smaller when there is light damping.", "The resting position is the same in both cases. What differs is whether the motion stops there or passes through."], "end": "So the mass is the difference between settling and oscillating.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 16", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If it starts with x negative and v equal to 0, will it oscillate? Say in a couple of sentences what will happen.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium (x equal to 0). The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it goes past 0 and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. So the position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Review 15", "q": "Some people say that everything is a harmonic oscillator. Give 5 examples of oscillatory systems we discussed in class or in the Meadows book.", "a": "A mass on a spring swinging back and forth. The air in the vocal tract vibrating during speech, with its particles moving closer together and farther apart. The vocal folds opening and closing hundreds of times a second. The estimate in stochastic gradient descent swinging around the average when the learning rate is high. The economy going through boom and bust around its growth trend. A car bouncing after it goes over a bump. In the Meadows book, a room temperature swinging above and below the setting when the thermostat is far from the heater, and a car dealership's inventory swinging up and down because orders arrive after a delay. Any five of these work."}, {"source": "Review 4", "q": "Two temperature control systems are set to 70. In the first, the thermostat is right next to the heating and cooling unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, the temperature drops to 70, and the thermostat detects that and turns the unit off. Below 70, the heat turns on until 70 and then stops. In the second, the thermostat is far from the unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, but the thermostat does not detect 70, so the temperature keeps dropping to 60. Then the heat turns on, the temperature rises past 70 to 80 before the thermostat notices, and so on. Which system is like a spring by itself, and which is like a mass-spring system?", "a": "The first system, with the thermostat next to the unit, is like a spring by itself. This is because it moves toward its goal of 70 and stops there, the way a spring alone returns to equilibrium and stays. The second system, with the far thermostat, is like a mass-spring system. This is because it keeps going past 70, to 60 and then to 80, and swings back and forth around the goal. A moving mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so a mass-spring system overshoots and oscillates in the same way. The delay in detecting the temperature plays the part of the mass."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 17", "q": "What is the goal of a spring, if it is extended or squeezed? What is the primary physical property of a mass that is already moving?", "a": "The goal of a spring is to go back to equilibrium, its resting position. The primary property of a mass that is already moving is that it keeps on moving. Together these two facts are why a mass on a spring oscillates, because the spring keeps pulling the mass back and the mass keeps going past the middle."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 18", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If x starts as negative and v is 0, will the system oscillate? How will the position of the mass evolve with time?", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts out negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium at x equal to 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A mass on a spring starts with x positive and v equal to 0. Will it oscillate? Describe what happens.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position starts positive, so the spring is stretched and pulls the mass back toward equilibrium at 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots to the negative side and compresses the spring. The spring then pushes it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth as a cosine-shaped wave, starting from its high point."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the oscillator rules, x dot equals v and v dot equals minus x. If x is plus 5 right now, is the mass moving up or down?", "a": "You cannot tell from x alone. The rules only say that a positive x makes the velocity go down. Whether the mass is moving up or down right now depends on the current velocity. If v is still positive the mass is moving up while slowing, and if v is already negative it is moving down."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a mass on a spring keep passing through the middle instead of stopping there?", "a": "Because at the middle the spring is at its resting position and pulls with no force, but the mass is moving as fast as it ever does. A moving mass keeps moving unless something changes its motion, so it goes through. Only after it is past the middle does the spring start pulling it back."}]}, {"id": "m1-balancing", "tier": "On the review sheet", "lectures": "Lectures 04, 05–06, 07 and 09", "title": "A spring alone, a cooling cup and balancing feedback", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**A viscous spring with no mass obeys one rule, the change in x is minus one third of x, so it returns to equilibrium and stops.** If x is positive, its change is negative, so x falls. If x is negative, its change is positive, so x rises. Both lead to the same resting position at 0.", "sub": ["Starting from 1, the first return is big, because one third of 1 is a big step. As x gets smaller, its rate of change gets smaller too, so the curve drops steeply at first and then flattens. From below zero it rises and flattens.", "This is **balancing feedback** (negative feedback), because the system acts against the discrepancy from equilibrium. The motion reduces the very displacement that causes it, and when x is 0 nothing drives it any more.", "A bigger number in the rule, such as minus 0.9 instead of minus 0.1, means a faster return. A smaller number means a slower return."], "end": "So without a mass there is no overshoot, only a return that slows as it gets close.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p3 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**A differential equation says how a system changes from the state it is in now, and together with a starting state it predicts the whole future.** The rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, with x starting at 100, gives a change of about minus 33, so the next value is about 67. The same rule on 67 gives a smaller step, and the value heads toward 0 while the curve flattens.", "sub": ["The same rule describes a spring, a cup of coffee and a learning system, because all three correct a gap, and the correction shrinks as the gap shrinks.", "Repeating the arithmetic step by step is Euler's method."], "end": "So knowing the rule of change and the starting point is enough to predict the time course.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18", "main": "**Learning and a cooling cup of coffee share the same shape of approach to equilibrium.** Hot coffee at 60, 80 or 100 degrees cools toward room temperature at 18, and iced coffee at 0, 5 or 10 degrees warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is large and slows as the gap shrinks, so every cup ends at the same temperature from either side.", "sub": ["The SGD estimate behaves the same way from 130 above or 10 below, except that its path is noisy, because each observation is random."], "end": "So a correction that opposes a discrepancy is the shared organization of both.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 17", "q": "If x dot equals minus 0.1 times x describes a system, and x starts negative, what will happen? What happens if the number minus 0.1 becomes minus 0.9?", "a": "The position x rises toward 0 and stops there. This is because x is negative, so minus 0.1 times x is positive, so x goes up. As x gets closer to 0 the change gets smaller, so it rises fast at first and then more and more slowly, and it never crosses 0. With minus 0.9 the same thing happens much faster, because each step changes x by 90 percent of the gap instead of 10 percent. There is no mass in this system, so there is no overshoot and no oscillation."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A system follows the rule that the change in x is minus 0.1 times x, and x starts positive. What happens? What if it starts at exactly 0?", "a": "If x starts positive it falls toward 0 and stops there. This is because minus 0.1 times a positive x is negative, so x goes down, and the step gets smaller as x gets smaller, so it never crosses 0.\n\nIf x starts at exactly 0, nothing happens, because minus 0.1 times 0 is 0, so there is no change. The system is already at equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a spring with no mass never overshoot its resting position?", "a": "Because the only thing driving the motion is the discrepancy from equilibrium. As the spring gets close to its resting position the discrepancy shrinks, so the motion shrinks, and at the resting position the motion is zero. There is no mass to carry the motion past that point. With a mass, the mass arrives moving and keeps moving, and that is what makes the overshoot."}]}, {"id": "m1-mass-stiffness", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "title": "Mass, stiffness and frequency, from springs to vocal folds", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p13 · Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**More mass lowers the frequency of a mass-spring system, and more stiffness raises it.** **Perturbation theory** asks how the behavior changes when one setting of the system is changed. When the mass gets heavier, the same spring has more to slow down and turn around, so each cycle takes longer and fewer cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes down.", "sub": ["When the spring gets stiffer, it pulls the mass back more strongly for the same displacement, so the turns happen sooner and more cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes up.", "Mass and stiffness therefore have opposite effects on frequency."], "end": "So to predict a frequency change, ask whether mass or stiffness changed, and in which direction.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p8", "main": "**The vocal folds are small muscles, about 1 centimeter long and 1 millimeter high, and their mass and stiffness set the pitch of the voice.** About ten other muscles act on them through the surrounding cartilages. Stretching the folds makes them stiffer, so they vibrate faster and the pitch goes up. Bunching them makes them effectively heavier, so they vibrate more slowly and the pitch goes down.", "sub": ["The rate of vibration is called **F0**, the fundamental frequency. Changing it is how we sing, ask a question, show surprise or doubt, and in tone languages tell one word from another.", "The folds vibrate because air pressure from the lungs builds up below the closed folds and pushes them apart, the fast air between them sucks them back together (the Bernoulli force, like a shower curtain pulled inward), pressure builds again, and this repeats hundreds of times a second."], "end": "So the vocal folds are a mass-spring system made of tissue, and the air above them is a separate vibrating system.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 24", "q": "If I raise the stiffness of a mass-spring system, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall? And if I raise the mass, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall?", "a": "Raising the stiffness makes the frequency rise, because a stiffer spring pulls the mass back faster, so each cycle takes less time. Raising the mass makes the frequency fall, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around, so each cycle takes more time."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A singer stretches the vocal folds tighter. What happens to the pitch, and why?", "a": "The pitch goes up. This is because stretching the folds makes them stiffer, and a stiffer system springs back faster, so it vibrates more times per second. More vibrations per second is a higher fundamental frequency, which is heard as a higher pitch."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If the mass of a mass-spring system is doubled and the stiffness is also doubled, what can you say about the frequency?", "a": "The two changes work against each other. Doubling the mass lowers the frequency, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around. Doubling the stiffness raises it, because a stiffer spring pulls back harder. For a single mass on a single spring the two effects cancel, so the frequency stays the same."}]}, {"id": "m1-air", "tier": "On the review sheet", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 05–06 and 07", "title": "Air vibration: compression, rarefaction and frequency", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p4", "main": "**The air between the tongue and the walls of the vocal tract can vibrate, and that vibration is the sound of speech.** The curved vocal tract can be straightened into a tube to understand the motion of its air. In the tube the rows of air particles move back and forth, so the air is more crowded in some places and less crowded in others.", "sub": ["Moving the tongue changes the shape of the air space, which is **articulatory phonetics**. The sound the vibrating air makes is **acoustic phonetics**."], "end": "So the visible space above the tongue and the physical vibration that carries sound are the same thing seen two ways.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p10 · Lecture 07 · p5", "main": "**A vibration in air is a repeated change between compression and rarefaction.** A slice of air keeps the same particles the whole time. In **compression** the particles move closer together and the pressure is high. In **rarefaction** they move farther apart and the pressure is low. The particles do not change size or number, only their spacing.", "sub": ["At equilibrium the spacing is 100 percent. In the compressed phase it drops to about 60 percent, and in the spread-out phase it rises to about 140 percent, exaggerated so it can be seen.", "The slice contracts, expands back through equilibrium, reaches rarefaction, and contracts again, over and over."], "end": "So sound in air is particles swinging closer and farther apart around their resting spacing, like a mass on a spring.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p11 · Lecture 07 · p9", "main": "**The expansion of the air slice follows a cosine, and one full cycle is one round trip from spread out to compressed and back.** At the start the value is plus 1 and the particles are farthest apart. The value falls through 0, the equilibrium spacing, to minus 1, the closest spacing, and rises back to plus 1. Three cycles fit in the graph.", "sub": ["The moving point on the curve shows the spacing at that moment. The graph is a record of expansion over time, not a path that the particles travel along."], "end": "So positive values mean rarefaction, negative values mean compression, and the curve repeats.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p11", "main": "**Frequency is how many complete cycles fit into a fixed amount of time, measured in cycles per second, or hertz.** Three air slices swing by the same amount. One completes 3 cycles in 12 seconds, one completes 6, and one completes 9. The one with 9 cycles has three times the frequency of the one with 3.", "sub": ["Equal swings do not mean equal frequencies. The particles can spread and compress by the same amount at very different rates."], "end": "So frequency is about how fast the cycle repeats, not how big the swing is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p15", "main": "**Speaking involves two different vibrating things, the vocal folds and the air in the vocal tract.** The folds are moving tissue. The air is changing compression and rarefaction. An explanation of speech has to account for both.", "end": "So the voice has a source, the folds, and a vibrating air column above it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between compression and rarefaction, and what stays the same during both?", "a": "In compression the particles of air move closer together and the pressure goes up. In rarefaction they move farther apart and the pressure goes down. What stays the same is the particles themselves. The slice keeps the same particles, with the same size and number, and only their spacing changes."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vibrations have the same amplitude, but one completes 4 cycles in 12 seconds and the other completes 8. Which has the higher frequency, and by how much?", "a": "The one with 8 cycles has the higher frequency, twice as high. This is because frequency is the number of cycles in a fixed time, and 8 cycles in 12 seconds is twice 4 cycles in 12 seconds. The amplitude, how far the particles spread and compress, does not change the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is the cosine graph of the air slice not a drawing of where the particles go?", "a": "The graph shows how spread out the slice is over time. The height of the curve is the spacing at that moment, plus 1 for farthest apart and minus 1 for closest together. The particles themselves only move back and forth along the tube. They do not travel along a wavy path."}]}, {"id": "m1-hearing", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "title": "Hearing: the cochlea and the basilar membrane", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p5 · Lecture 08 · p6", "main": "**Sound travels from the air into the ear and reaches the cochlea, the coiled structure of the inner ear that sends frequency information to the brain.** The vibration moves the eardrum (the tympanic membrane), passes through three small bones, the malleus, incus and stapes, and enters the cochlea.", "sub": ["Inside the cochlea runs the **basilar membrane**, a long strip from the entrance to the far end at the helicotrema. Thinking of it uncoiled makes it easier to see how different places along one strip can respond differently.", "The word to memorize is cochlea. The other labels on the diagram show where things are and do not need to be memorized."], "end": "So the path is air, eardrum, three bones, cochlea, basilar membrane.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p14 · Lecture 08 · p7", "main": "**The basilar membrane sorts a sound into its frequencies because its mass and stiffness change along its length.** The wide end is heavy and loose, so it has a lot of mass and little stiffness, and both of those favor low frequencies. The narrow end is light and stiff, and both of those favor high frequencies. Places in between respond to frequencies in between.", "sub": ["Extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. Extra stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. That is the same rule as for a mass on a spring.", "Nerve fibers leave every place along the membrane, so which fibers are active tells the brain where the vibration is strongest, and that tells the brain which frequencies are present. Georg von Békésy won the 1961 Nobel Prize for this work."], "end": "So a mixture of frequencies makes several places vibrate at once, and the brain reads the mixture off the pattern of active nerves.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 3", "q": "What is the physical principle behind how your auditory system hears different frequencies? Describe the basic physics of the basilar membrane to show how the system works.", "a": "The principle is that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The basilar membrane (the long strip inside the cochlea) is heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other end. The heavy, loose end vibrates most for low frequencies, because extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. The light, stiff end vibrates most for high frequencies, because stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. Each place along the membrane is connected to its own neurons. So which neurons fire tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells the brain which frequencies are in the sound."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 3", "q": "One side of the basilar membrane is heavy and not stiff, while the other side is light and very stiff. How do these facts enable hearing?", "a": "Frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. So the heavy, non-stiff end of the membrane vibrates at low frequencies, and the light, stiff end vibrates at high frequencies. Each part of the membrane is hooked to neurons. Those neurons tell the brain which frequencies were heard, based on which location is vibrating."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which end of the basilar membrane responds to a high-pitched sound, and why?", "a": "The light, stiff end responds to a high-pitched sound. This is because a high pitch is a high frequency, and a part with little mass is easy to move while a stiff part springs back fast, so that part vibrates quickly. The heavy, loose end vibrates slowly, so it responds to low frequencies instead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does the brain know which frequencies are in a sound, if all it gets is nerve signals?", "a": "Each place along the basilar membrane is connected to its own nerve fibers, and each place vibrates most for one frequency. So the set of nerve fibers that is active tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells it which frequencies are present. The brain reads frequency from location."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the basilar membrane and a mass-spring system have in common?", "a": "Both follow the rule that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The membrane is like a row of mass-spring systems, heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other, so each place has its own natural frequency. That is what lets it separate a sound into its frequencies."}]}, {"id": "m1-euler", "tier": "Possible", "lectures": "Lectures 08 and 09", "title": "Euler's method: arithmetic that approximates continuous change", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p11 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**Euler's method turns a rule about rates of change into a sequence of numbers by taking small time steps.** The new value is the old value plus the rate of change times the size of the time step. Repeating that arithmetic gives an approximate record of how the system moves through time.", "sub": ["The result is approximate, so the size of the time step and the order of the updates both affect what comes out.", "For the rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, starting at 100 gives about 67, then a smaller step, and so on toward 0."], "end": "So a computer simulates a dynamical system by adding up many small changes.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p10", "main": "**Explicit Euler updates position and velocity from the old pair, and with a big time step its swings grow.** With a time step of 0.5, starting at x equal to 1 and v equal to 0, the new position is the old velocity times 0.5 plus the old position, and the new velocity is minus the old position times 0.5 plus the old velocity. The first step gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second gives x of 0.75 and v of minus 1.", "sub": ["The values keep changing sign, but each swing gets bigger, reaching past 2 and past 3 within twelve steps.", "That growth comes from the approximation with this time step, not from the real oscillator gaining energy."], "end": "So the same physics simulated two ways can look different, and this way looks like it is blowing up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p9", "main": "**Position-first Euler updates the position first and then uses the new position to update the velocity, and its swings stay the same size.** The first step again gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second step gives x of 0.75, and then the velocity uses that new 0.75, so v is minus 0.875 instead of minus 1.", "sub": ["The sequence stays between about minus 1 and plus 1, crossing zero again and again, which is what the real oscillator does.", "The order matters because the second calculation receives information from the first."], "end": "So the way the arithmetic is arranged changes the answer, and the position-first order keeps the oscillation bounded.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p9"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does Euler's method do, and why is its answer only approximate?", "a": "Euler's method takes a rule for how fast something changes and turns it into a list of values, by adding the rate of change times a small time step to the current value, over and over. It is only approximate because the rate is treated as constant during each step, when really it keeps changing. A smaller time step makes the approximation closer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two simulations of the same mass-spring system use the same time step, but one keeps growing and the other stays bounded. What is the difference?", "a": "The difference is the order of the updates. The simulation that grows is explicit Euler, which computes the new position and the new velocity both from the old pair. The one that stays bounded updates the position first and then uses that new position to update the velocity. The physics is the same, so the growth is a mistake of the arithmetic, not a property of the oscillator."}]}]}, "module1-v2": {"label": "Module I · Study this first (Lectures 01–10, slides with the videos played on them)", "range": "Lectures 01 to 10", "split": true, "notes": true, "howToAnswer": [], "scope": {"label": "On the final", "covers": "Lectures 01 to 10 were Exam 1. The final is cumulative, so all of this comes back, and the questions below are the ones he already asked about it.", "final": "", "evidence": "On Exam 1, about 15 of the 25 questions came almost word for word from the review sheet, with only the sounds, numbers or positions changed. The topics marked Asked on Exam 1 had a question on the exam. The topics marked On the review sheet were on the review but not the exam. Questions marked Predicted change one thing in a real review or exam question. They are predictions, not confirmed exam questions."}, "flags": ["The Exam 1 key is the professor's own. Where an answer here says more than the key, the extra sentences are the slides' explanation of the same answer, not a different answer.", "The uploaded answer companion for the review sheet has unverified authorship. Its answers agree with the slides, and nothing here comes only from it.", "The deck labeled Lectures 05–06 has the internal title Lectures 4+5. Both labels mean the same deck.", "Lecture 08 page 10 and page 9 show two Euler methods. The explicit method uses the old position and the old velocity for both updates, and its swings grow. The position-first method uses the new position for the velocity update, and its swings stay the same size. The slides show both so that you can see the order of the updates matters."], "topics": [{"id": "m1-consonants", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 01 and 02", "title": "Consonants: voicing, place, manner and nasality", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**A consonant is made by narrowing or blocking the air somewhere in the mouth, and voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate while you do it.** Say f as in fat and hold it, then say v as in vat and hold it. The lips and teeth stay in the same place for both, so the narrowing is the same. The difference is that the vocal folds vibrate for v and not for f, so v is **voiced** and f is **voiceless**.", "sub": ["The same pair happens for th in thing and th in that, for s and z, and for sh and the j sound in beige. In each pair the place stays the same and only the voicing changes.", "These sounds are **fricatives** (narrowing sounds), because the air squeezes through a narrow gap and makes a hissing noise."], "end": "So voicing is one dimension of a consonant, and it can change while everything else stays the same.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p9 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**Place of articulation is where in the mouth the narrowing happens, and the fricatives line up from the front of the mouth to the back.** The f and v are **labial** (made with the lower lip against the upper teeth). The two th sounds are **dental** (tongue tip at the teeth). The s and z are **alveolar** (tongue at the ridge just behind the upper teeth). The sh and the beige j sound are **postalveolar** (a little farther back than the ridge).", "sub": ["In the MRI images the narrowing moves from the front of the mouth toward the back as you go across the table."], "end": "So place is the second dimension, and each fricative is one place combined with one voicing.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p10 · Lecture 02 · p5", "main": "**A stop blocks the air completely somewhere in the mouth, and the stops come in groups of three by place.** The p, b and m are made at the lips (labial). The t, d and n are made at the alveolar ridge. The k, g and ng are made with the back of the tongue raised to the **velum** (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth).", "sub": ["Within each group, p, t and k are voiceless and b, d and g are voiced, so p and b are a voicing pair with the same place, and so are t and d, and k and g.", "The m, n and ng are **nasal stops**. The mouth is blocked in the same place as for b, d and g, but the velum is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose. They are all voiced."], "end": "So m and b share place and voicing and differ only in manner, because m lets the air out through the nose and b does not.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/010.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/af1596660e42d3892bf1.mp4", "box": [39.74, 36.2, 18.05, 27.93], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/af1596660e42d3892bf1.jpg"}, {"video": "assets/original/f6f97d126c32a6a771b6.mp4", "box": [39.71, 67.69, 18.05, 30.06], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/f6f97d126c32a6a771b6.jpg"}, {"video": "assets/original/948f00d8877b60e2367c.mp4", "box": [61.42, 68.76, 18.34, 25.92], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/948f00d8877b60e2367c.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/af1596660e42d3892bf1.mp4", "box": [39.74, 36.2, 18.05, 27.93], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/af1596660e42d3892bf1.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p6", "main": "**Every English consonant is one combination of manner, voicing and place.** **Manner** is how the air is restricted, as a fricative (narrowed), an oral stop (blocked and then released through the mouth) or a nasal stop (blocked in the mouth and let out through the nose). **Voicing** is whether the vocal folds vibrate. **Place** is where the restriction is.", "sub": ["Nasality can be counted as a fourth way to tell consonants apart, because it is whether the velum is lowered to let air through the nose.", "A speaker knows this whole system without ever naming its parts, and that is part of what makes knowing a language a remarkable skill."], "end": "So to say how two consonants are similar and different, compare them one dimension at a time, manner, voicing and place.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p6"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 1", "q": "What is the similarity between m in mad and b in bad, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both m and b are voiced, and both are made at the lips, so they share voicing and place of articulation. The difference is manner (how the air is let out). For m the velum (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth) is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose, and m is a nasal stop. For b the velum is raised, so the air is blocked completely at the lips and then released through the mouth, and b is an oral stop (a plosive)."}, {"source": "Review 6", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish between the consonants at the beginning of the words Pam, Bam, Dam, Tam, Kam and Gam?", "a": "All six are oral stops, so they are distinguished by place of articulation and by voicing. Place is where the air is blocked. The p and b are made at the lips (labial), the t and d at the alveolar ridge behind the upper teeth (alveolar), and the k and g with the back of the tongue at the velum (velar). Voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate. The p, t and k are voiceless, and the b, d and g are voiced. So each sound is one place combined with one voicing."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 2", "q": "Name three ways in which we can distinguish consonants from each other.", "a": "Any three of these four. Voicing, which is whether the vocal folds vibrate. Place of articulation, which is where in the mouth the sound is made, such as labial, dental, alveolar, postalveolar or velar. Manner of articulation, which is how the air is constricted, such as fricative, oral stop or nasal stop. Nasality, which is whether the velum is lowered so that the air escapes through the nose."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 6", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the tongue tip is up at the teeth. In (b) the lower lip is against the upper teeth. Which of these is f as in family, and which is th as in thing?", "a": "The f is (b), and the th is (a). This is because f is a labial sound, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth, which is what (b) shows. The th is a dental sound, made with the tongue tip up at the teeth, which is what (a) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q06.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 8", "q": "What is the similarity between n in neck and d in deck, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both are voiced alveolar stops, so they share voicing, place and a complete closure. The difference is that n is a nasal stop, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose. The d is an oral stop, so the air is blocked completely and then released through the mouth."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 19", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the back of the tongue is raised. In (b) the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which of these is k as in Ken, and which is m as in men?", "a": "The k is (a), and the m is (b). This is because k is a velar sound, made with the back of the tongue raised up to the velum, which is what (a) shows. The m is a labial nasal, made with the lips closed and the velum lowered so that air goes through the nose, which is what (b) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q19.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between s in sing and z in zing, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are fricatives made at the alveolar ridge, so they share manner and place. The difference is voicing. For z the vocal folds vibrate, so z is voiced. For s they do not, so s is voiceless."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between t in tap and n in nap, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are stops made at the alveolar ridge, so they share place and a complete closure in the mouth. They differ in voicing and in nasality. The t is voiceless and oral, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth. The n is voiced and nasal, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish the consonants at the beginning of fat, vat, sing and zing?", "a": "All four are fricatives, so they are distinguished by place and by voicing. The f and v are labial, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth. The s and z are alveolar, made with the tongue at the ridge behind the upper teeth. The f and s are voiceless, and the v and z are voiced."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In one the lips are closed and the velum is raised. In the other the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which is b and which is m?", "a": "The b is the one with the velum raised, and the m is the one with the velum lowered. This is because both are made with the lips closed, so the lips do not tell them apart. For m the lowered velum opens the way to the nose, so the air comes out through the nose and m is nasal. For b the raised velum closes that way, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth."}]}, {"id": "m1-vowels", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "title": "Vowels: tongue height, tongue frontness, and more than one frequency", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p12 · Lecture 07 · p10", "main": "**Vowels are told apart by where the tongue is, by its height and by its frontness.** Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth.", "sub": ["The vowel in dean is high and front. The vowel in dune is high and back. The vowel in Dan is low and front. The vowel in Don is low and back. The vowel in done is in the middle.", "Moving the tongue changes where the air space above the vocal folds is narrow and where it is wide."], "end": "So each vowel is a different shape of the air space, made by a different tongue position.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p13 · Lecture 07 · p12", "main": "**The air in a vowel does not vibrate at a single frequency, it vibrates at a combination of frequencies at once.** The illustrative /i/ model adds a slow vibration and a fast one, 1 cycle per second plus 4 cycles per second. The illustrative /a/ model adds 2 cycles per second plus 3 cycles per second. Both start at the same value, but inside each second they move very differently.", "sub": ["Two air slices can be at different points at the same moment, so one can be compressed while the other is spread out.", "The frequencies in the model are made slow on purpose so that the combination can be seen. Real vowel frequencies are hundreds of cycles per second."], "end": "So what makes /i/ sound different from /a/ is which frequencies are combined, not a single frequency.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/013.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/3d37757b637ee16552ee.mp4", "box": [8.58, 8.16, 88.05, 88.05], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3d37757b637ee16552ee.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/3d37757b637ee16552ee.mp4", "box": [8.58, 8.16, 88.05, 88.05], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3d37757b637ee16552ee.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p13 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**The tongue shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and that is why one vowel contains several frequencies.** Where the tongue narrows the air space, that part of the air behaves differently from the part where there is more room. More mass makes a part vibrate more slowly, and more stiffness makes a part vibrate faster.", "sub": ["So the vibrating air has a mix of lower, middle and higher frequencies, and the mix depends on the vowel.", "To hear speech, the ear has to pick that mix apart and tell how strong each frequency is. That is how a listener tells scene from son."], "end": "So vowels connect the tongue, the physics of mass and stiffness, and hearing in one chain.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p4"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 13", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at a combination of frequencies at the same time. This is because the tongue shape changes where the air in the vocal tract is constricted (narrowed), so different parts of the air have different mass and stiffness. Different mass and stiffness means different frequencies, and they all sound together. The combination is what makes one vowel sound different from another."}, {"source": "Review 14", "q": "What are the 2 basic ways we distinguish vowels?", "a": "Vowels are distinguished by tongue height and by tongue frontness. Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth. For example, the vowel in dean is high and front, the vowel in dune is high and back, the vowel in Dan is low and front, and the vowel in Don is low and back."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 9", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in mean and moon?", "a": "Both are high vowels, so the tongue is high in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in mean is a front vowel, and the vowel in moon is a back vowel. For moon the lips are also rounded."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 16", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at several frequencies at once, because the tongue shape gives different parts of the air in the vocal tract different mass and stiffness, so they vibrate at different frequencies together."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in Dan and Don?", "a": "Both are low vowels, so the tongue is low in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in Dan is a front vowel and the vowel in Don is a back vowel."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in dean and Dan?", "a": "Both are front vowels, so the raised part of the tongue is toward the front of the mouth for both. The difference is height. The vowel in dean is high, and the vowel in Dan is low."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does one vowel sound different from another, if both are just air vibrating in the vocal tract?", "a": "The two vowels have different combinations of frequencies. This is because the tongue is in a different place for each vowel, so the air space has a different shape. A different shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and different mass and stiffness mean different frequencies. So the air vibrates at a different mix of frequencies for each vowel, and the ear hears the difference in the mix."}]}, {"id": "m1-system", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 02 and 03", "title": "Speech, the octopus, and what a dynamical system is", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 03 · p3", "main": "**A dynamical system is a set of elements with relationships between them, and their organized interaction produces behavior over time toward a goal.** To understand a behavior, name the elements, name the relationships among them, and name the goal their coordinated activity serves.", "sub": ["A list of parts on its own does not explain the behavior. The relationships are what make the parts act together."], "end": "So the question to ask about any system is what the elements are, how they are related, and what they are for.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p4 · Lecture 03 · p7", "main": "**An octopus keeping its den is a dynamical system made of eight arms, the ground and the fish it slaps at.** The arms interact with the ground and with the fish, so the environment is part of the system. A nervous system spread through the head and the body coordinates the arms.", "sub": ["A study of 19 arm slaps found that the octopus combines simple movements, rotation (turning an arm) and translation (moving it along), one after another and at the same time, to make the complex slap.", "The simple movements are planned ahead and then adjusted by feedback about where the moving fish is."], "end": "So a complex action is built from a few simple movements organized together, and the organization, not any one arm, explains the behavior.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p5 · Lecture 01 · p2 · Lecture 02 · p3", "main": "**Speech is a dynamical system whose elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat.** The brain coordinates the relationships among these parts so that the shape of the vocal tract changes in the right way. The goal is to convey a thought.", "sub": ["In the MRI videos the tongue reshapes while the openings in the mouth and throat change, and almost none of this can be seen from outside.", "Listing the parts does not explain speaking. Their changing relationships produce the movement, the same way the arms and the ground together produce the octopus slap."], "end": "So speech, like the octopus, is explained by organized interaction, not by a list of parts.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/005.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.mp4", "box": [58.11, 21.24, 35.42, 62.96], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.mp4", "box": [58.11, 21.24, 35.42, 62.96], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p5 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p6", "main": "**Very different systems can share the same organization, and that is why studying a simple one helps with a complicated one.** The heart, the pancreas, neurons and voluntary movements can all be studied as dynamical systems, with elements that are organs, cells or chemicals.", "sub": ["Seeing the deep similarity between two systems that look different is creative reasoning, as opposed to treating things that merely look alike as the same."], "end": "So the same questions about elements, relationships and goals work for speech, for the body, and for AI.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p6"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 25", "q": "There are systems all around us. True or false?", "a": "True. A system is a set of elements with relationships between them that together serve a goal, and that describes speech, an octopus, a thermostat, a population, an economy and a neural network."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the elements, the relationships and the goal of the speech system?", "a": "The elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat. The relationships are the coordinated movements among those parts that change the shape of the vocal tract, which the brain organizes. The goal is to convey a thought."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is a list of the speech organs not a complete explanation of speaking?", "a": "A list of the parts leaves out the relationships between them, and the relationships are what produce the behavior. Speaking happens because the tongue, lips, velum and larynx move together in an organized way toward the goal of conveying a thought. So the explanation has to say how the parts interact, not just what they are."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What makes the octopus arm slap a dynamical system, and what does it have in common with speech?", "a": "The arm slap has elements, the eight arms, the ground and the fish, that interact through relationships coordinated by the nervous system, toward the goal of keeping the den. Speech has the same organization, with the tongue, lips, velum and larynx as elements, coordinated by the brain, toward the goal of conveying a thought. In both, the behavior comes from the organized interaction of the parts, not from any one part on its own."}]}, {"id": "m1-closure", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02 and 03", "title": "Closure, the cloze task and how GPT learns", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p9", "main": "**Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar pattern that is unfinished.** A broken circle is seen as a whole circle, because familiarity with the shape lets the mind close the gap. Three black disks with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, even though no triangle is drawn.", "sub": ["Cloze is pronounced like close and is named after closure. It takes the same idea from shapes to language. The reader uses the words that remain to fill in the word that is missing."], "end": "So closure means filling in what is missing from what is already known.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p10", "main": "**The cloze procedure deletes words from a message and measures how well people can restore them.** In \"Chickens cackle and ___ quack,\" the missing word is ducks. Each correct restoration counts as one cloze unit.", "sub": ["Filling the gap takes more than knowing one word. It takes the meanings of the words, the way chickens goes with cackle and ducks with quack, and the parallel shape of the sentence.", "Wilson Taylor introduced the procedure in 1953 as a way to measure how readable a text is."], "end": "So knowledge can be tested through the ability to complete a sequence, and that is how a language model shows what it knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p10", "main": "**GPT learns by doing the cloze task billions of times.** It takes a sentence, hides part of it, and makes the network guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of completions, the weights hold the knowledge of the language.", "sub": ["Completing a sequence is predicting what fits the context, and people do the same thing when they expect what comes next.", "Whether this counts as knowledge is a debate. One view is that the system's knowledge is not so different from ours, and a skeptical view, associated with Yann LeCun, says it is."], "end": "So closure is the principle, the cloze task is the method, and prediction is what the system learns to do.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 7", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, and how it is used in the GPT system.", "a": "Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar but unfinished pattern. A broken circle is still seen as a circle, and three black circles with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, because the mind fills in what is missing. GPT uses the same idea as its way of learning, which is called the cloze task. A sentence is taken, part of it is hidden, and the network has to guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of these completions the network ends up knowing the language."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 11", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, illustrated by the figure of three black circles with wedges cut out, and how it is used in the GPT system as a principle for learning.", "a": "Humans are very good at filling in missing information using what we already know. In the figure no triangle is actually drawn, but we see one anyway because the brain completes the shape. GPT learns the same way, with the cloze task. It takes a sentence, pretends that part of the sentence is missing, and asks the network to complete what is missing. If the guess is wrong, the weights get adjusted. After billions of these completions the system ends up knowing the language.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q11.png", "alt": "Three black circles with wedges cut out that make a triangle appear"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A sentence has a word hidden, and a language model guesses the wrong word. What happens next, and why does this count as learning?", "a": "The wrong guess gets compared with the real missing word, and the difference is the mistake. The weights are then changed a little in the direction that would have made the right word more likely. This counts as learning because the weights hold the knowledge, so changing them changes what the network knows. After billions of these corrections the guesses become right."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a broken circle and the sentence Chickens cackle and ___ quack have in common?", "a": "In both, something is missing and the mind fills it in from what is already there. The circle is completed from familiarity with its shape, and the sentence is completed with ducks from the meanings of the other words and the parallel pattern of the sentence. Both are closure, which is completing a familiar but unfinished pattern."}]}, {"id": "m1-output", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 09 and 10", "title": "What a neural network outputs: a probability distribution and a decision", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10 · Lecture 10 · p3", "main": "**A neural network takes in a signal as a list of numbers and gives out a probability distribution over the possible identities.** A face image can be 48 million pixel numbers, and the question is which of 1000 people it is. A speech wave is a list of pressure numbers, and the question is which of 25 sounds it is.", "sub": ["For five vowels in the order read, rad, rude, Rud, rod, the output [0.2, 0.2, 0.4, 0.2, 0] gives 20 percent to read, 20 percent to rad, 40 percent to rude, 20 percent to Rud and 0 to rod.", "The decision is the category with the largest number, so here the decision is rude."], "end": "So the output is not one answer but a set of probabilities, and the decision is the biggest one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10", "main": "**A probability distribution is a list where every number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1.** A list that adds up to 0, or to 1.5, is not a distribution, and neither is a list with a negative number in it.", "sub": ["The list only means something together with the agreed order of the categories. The same numbers in a different order would be a different decision.", "For example, [.4, 0, .5, .2] is not a good output for four categories, because the numbers add up to 1.1."], "end": "So to check an output, check that each number is between 0 and 1 and that the total is exactly 1.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p11 · Lecture 10 · p12", "main": "**The true answer is given to the network as a distribution with all of the probability on the correct category.** If the correct vowel is Rud, the fourth of five, the target is [0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. The network's own output might be [0.4, 0.1, 0.2, 0.2, 0.1], which puts the most probability on read.", "sub": ["Comparing the output with the target gives the error, which is also called the loss. Training changes the weights to make that error smaller.", "The input numbers can be positive or negative and are not probabilities. Only the output is a probability distribution."], "end": "So the target is the same kind of list as the output, with a 1 in the right place and 0 everywhere else.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p11 · Lecture 03 · p9", "main": "**For a language model the categories are the possible next words, so the output is a probability for each word.** Each input word is a vector (a list of numbers), for example 1,024 numbers per word. The model uses the whole sequence, chickens, cackle, and, ducks, to predict the next word.", "sub": ["The output is another vector, for example 10,000 numbers, one for each possible next word such as a, cat, quack and Zulu. The number for quack should be the biggest."], "end": "So a language model's decision is the next word with the highest probability.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 11", "q": "What is the output of an LLM or a neural network in general, and how does it express a decision?", "a": "The output is a probability distribution over the possible categories. That is a list with one number per category, where each number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1. The decision is the category with the highest probability. For an LLM the categories are the possible next words, so the output says how likely each word is to come next."}, {"source": "Review 12", "q": "Is this a good output of a neural network, if there are 4 categories, [.4, 0, .5, .2]? Why or why not?", "a": "No, it is not a good output. This is because the output has to be a probability distribution, so the numbers have to add up to 1. These numbers add up to 1.1. Each number is between 0 and 1, so that part is fine, but the total is wrong."}, {"source": "Review 20", "q": "The decision by a neural network is [.1, .1, .1, .2, .5], but the true category is the one in the second position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, as a probability distribution, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is written as [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]. This is because the correct category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and every other category gets 0. The 1 goes in the second position because that is where the true category is. The list still adds up to 1, so it is a probability distribution, and the network can compare its own output with it to find its mistake."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 1", "q": "Which of these four could be the probability distribution decision of a neural network? (a) [0, 0, 0, 0], (b) [0, .4, 0, .6], (c) [-.5, 0, .5, 0], (d) [.3, .3, .4, .5].", "a": "Only (b) is possible. This is because the numbers in a probability distribution have to be between 0 and 1 and have to add up to 1. List (a) adds up to 0. List (c) has a negative number. List (d) adds up to 1.5. List (b) has numbers between 0 and 1 that add up to 1."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 23", "q": "The decision made by a neural network is [.4, .1, .1, .3, .1], but the true category is the one in the last position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, in a probability distribution format, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 0, 0, 1]. This is because the true category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and the other four categories get 0. The 1 goes in the last position because that is where the true category is."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Is [.2, .3, .5] a good output of a neural network with three categories? Is [.5, .5, .5]?", "a": "The first one is good, because each number is between 0 and 1 and they add up to 1. The second one is not good, because the numbers add up to 1.5. A probability distribution has to add up to exactly 1."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A network's output is [.1, .6, .2, .1] and the true category is the third one. Write the true answer as a probability distribution. Is the network's decision right?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 1, 0], with all the probability on the third category. The network's decision is wrong, because its biggest number, .6, is in the second position, so it decided on the second category. The gap between its output and the target is the error that training uses to fix the weights."}]}, {"id": "m1-weights", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 09 and 10", "title": "Knowledge in the weights: inner products and forward propagation", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p12 · Lecture 02 · p13", "main": "**A network's knowledge is stored in its weights, which are the numbers that determine how the inputs get turned into output probabilities.** The input words are vectors called x's, and the weights are called w's. The computation that combines them uses only adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing, but a very large interconnected system of these operations can do something complex.", "sub": ["Some large AI systems have trillions of weights. The brain stores the same kind of knowledge in its synapses (the connections between neurons), about 150 trillion of them.", "Changing the weights changes which next words get high or low probability for the same input."], "end": "So learning means changing the weights, because that is where the knowledge is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p6", "main": "**Each neuron multiplies every incoming activation by its own weight and adds the products, and that sum is the inner product.** An **activation** is the number coming in from a neuron in the layer before. A **weight** is the number on one connection, and it can be positive or negative.", "sub": ["With inputs of minus 1, 1 and 3 and weights of minus 2, 3 and 1, the products are 2, 3 and 3, and the sum is 8.", "A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight changes it. For example, an input of 3 with a weight of 2 gives 6, and the same input with a weight of minus 2 gives minus 6.", "The inner product is also called the dot product or the scalar product. It is the core calculation of all neural networks."], "end": "So a neuron with 5 inputs has 5 weights, and its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w5 times x5.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p15 · Lecture 09 · p13", "main": "**The inner product is a way of comparing two lists of numbers, so the weights act as templates that the input is matched against.** With an input of [minus 1, 3, minus 2], the template [minus 2, 0, 3] gives 2 plus 0 minus 6, which is minus 4. The template [minus 2, 2, minus 1] gives 2 plus 6 plus 2, which is 10.", "sub": ["A big positive inner product means a strong match, and a value near zero means little match, so the second template matches better.", "In the templatic theory of face perception, the brain compares an incoming face with stored templates, and the most similar template decides who it is. The weights play the part of the templates."], "end": "So before learning the templates are random and the matches are poor, and training makes the templates useful.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p9 · Lecture 10 · p10", "main": "**Forward propagation is the calculation that runs from the input layer to the output layer using the current weights.** A network with 5 inputs, hidden layers of 3, 4 and 3 neurons, and 7 outputs has every neuron connected to every neuron in the next layer, which is 60 connections, each with its own weight.", "sub": ["Each neuron in the first hidden layer takes the inner product of the 5 inputs with its 5 weights. Those 3 activations become the inputs to the next layer, which does the same, and so on through the layers.", "With inputs [2, 1, 3, 4, 2] and weights [2, minus 1, 1, 3, minus 2], the middle hidden neuron gets 4 minus 1 plus 3 plus 12 minus 4, which is 14. With inputs [minus 2, 1, 3, minus 4, 2] and weights [3, minus 2, 1, 2, 5] the sum is minus 6 minus 2 plus 3 minus 8 plus 10, which is minus 3. Hidden activations can be negative.", "The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution, for example [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08], and the fourth category, at 0.30, is the decision."], "end": "So the output depends on the inputs and on every weight along the way, and the signs matter at every step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p14 · Lecture 09 · p16 · Lecture 10 · p5", "main": "**Perception works in stages, and a network copies that by turning each layer's output into the next layer's input.** In the visual system, areas V1, V2, V3 and V4 and the areas after them each transform the information and pass it on, with connections running forward and back.", "sub": ["**Inference** is using the current weights to compute an output for an input. **Learning** is changing the weights. A training step has to run inference first, to get the output it will compare with the correct answer, so inference is part of learning.", "Like Euler's method, a network builds a complex result out of many repeated simple calculations."], "end": "So a hierarchy of template comparisons, layer after layer, is what a neural network is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 8", "q": "How is knowledge represented in neural networks?", "a": "Knowledge is represented in the weights, which are the numbers on the connections between neurons. The weights determine how the input numbers get turned into the output probabilities. Changing the weights changes what the network knows, so learning means changing the weights. In the brain the same kind of knowledge sits in the synapses (the connections between neurons), and a large AI system can have trillions of weights."}, {"source": "Review 19", "q": "What is the math of forward propagation, from the signal input to a neural network all the way to a decision at the end? Describe it.", "a": "The input signal is a vector, which is a list of numbers, such as pressure values from a sound wave. Each neuron in the first layer multiplies each input number by its own weight and adds the products up. That sum is the inner product, and it is the neuron's activation. The activations of that layer become the inputs to the next layer, where every neuron does the same multiply-and-add with its own weights. This repeats layer by layer until the last layer. The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution over the categories, so the numbers are between 0 and 1 and add up to 1. The decision is the category with the largest probability."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 21", "q": "Imagine a neural network with many neurons in many layers. One neuron, call it y, in layer 4 is connected to 5 neurons from the previous layer. Every neuron is connected to all the neurons in the previous layer. How many weights does y have?", "a": "Neuron y has 5 weights. This is because there is one weight on each connection coming into a neuron, and y has 5 connections coming in, one from each of the 5 neurons in the previous layer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 22", "q": "Continuing from the previous question, y's weights are w1, w2, w3, w4 and w5, and the activations of the 5 neurons feeding it are x1, x2, x3, x4 and x5. How do we write the activation of neuron y?", "a": "The activation of y is w1 times x1, plus w2 times x2, plus w3 times x3, plus w4 times x4, plus w5 times x5. This is because each incoming activation is multiplied by its own weight, and then all five products are added up. That sum is the inner product."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron in layer 3 is connected to 7 neurons in layer 2. How many weights does it have, and how is its activation written?", "a": "It has 7 weights, one on each incoming connection. Its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w7 times x7, where the x's are the 7 activations coming in and the w's are the 7 weights. Each input is multiplied by its own weight and the products are added."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron has inputs 2, minus 1 and 3 with weights 1, 4 and minus 2. What is its activation, and what is this calculation called?", "a": "Its activation is minus 8. This is because the products are 2 times 1, which is 2, then minus 1 times 4, which is minus 4, then 3 times minus 2, which is minus 6, and 2 minus 4 minus 6 is minus 8. The calculation is the inner product, which multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between inference and learning in a neural network?", "a": "Inference uses the weights the network has now to compute an output for an input. Learning changes the weights. The two are linked, because a learning step first runs inference to get the current output, then compares it with the correct answer, then changes the weights to shrink the difference. So inference is part of every learning step, but only the weight change is learning."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can an input of 3 end up pushing a neuron's activation down?", "a": "It pushes the activation down when its weight is negative. This is because the input is multiplied by its weight, so 3 times a negative weight such as minus 2 gives minus 6, which lowers the sum. The sign of the weight decides whether an input adds to the activation or subtracts from it."}]}, {"id": "m1-sgd", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03 and 10", "title": "Stochastic gradient descent: guess, mistake, correct", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p16 · Lecture 03 · p12", "main": "**Stochastic gradient descent learns a quantity, such as the average of a list, by starting with a guess and correcting it from one observation at a time.** Adding up three numbers and dividing by three gives their average directly, but speech, language and scenes cannot be learned by adding a few numbers. So a different way of learning the average is used as a model of learning in general.", "sub": ["Start with a guess. Compare it with one value from the list. Move the guess part of the way toward that value. Repeat with another value.", "Each correction happens because a new observation shows a discrepancy (a gap) between the guess and the data."], "end": "So learning here means being corrected by observations, not being told the answer.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p17 · Lecture 03 · p13 · Lecture 03 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p15", "main": "**Each step subtracts a fraction of the mistake from the current estimate, and the fraction is the learning rate.** The data are [78, 67, 98, 85, 76, 68, 98, 72]. The estimate starts at 130, which is an arbitrary guess. A random observation, 98, is drawn. The mistake, or **loss**, is the estimate minus the observation, 130 minus 98, which is 32.", "sub": ["With a learning rate of 10 percent, the estimate moves down by a tenth of 32, so it goes from 130 to 126.8. With a learning rate of 25 percent it moves down by a quarter of 32, to 122. Either way it moves toward 98 without jumping all the way to it.", "**Stochastic** means the observation is picked at random. **Gradient** means the direction that reduces the mistake. **Descent** means the mistake goes down, not that the estimate always goes down."], "end": "So a partial correction lets each observation count without throwing away what the estimate already knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/017.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p17"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p23 · Lecture 03 · p27", "main": "**The first minus in the update makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the estimate.** When the estimate is 94.672 and the observation is 98, the difference is minus 3.328. Subtracting a negative number raises the estimate, so it goes up to 95.504.", "sub": ["When the estimate is above the observation, the difference is positive, so subtracting it lowers the estimate. When the estimate is below, the difference is negative, so subtracting it raises the estimate.", "That is why the path of the estimate jitters. It mostly falls from 130 toward 80, but it rises whenever a high observation such as 98 is drawn."], "end": "So one step can move away from the true average, and learning is judged by the whole run, not by one step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p28 · Lecture 03 · p29", "main": "**The loss goes down whenever the gap between the estimate and the target shrinks, whether the estimate itself is going up or going down.** The loss is half of the squared gap, so it is never negative and a big gap counts much more than a small one.", "sub": ["Starting at 140 with a target of 80, the estimate falls and the loss falls. Starting at minus 100, the estimate rises and the loss still falls, because the gap is shrinking.", "Descent describes the loss, not the estimate."], "end": "So the estimate can rise while the learning is still going downhill.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/028.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p28"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p10 · Lecture 03 · p11", "main": "**Fake it till you make it means the network starts with random weights, so it knows nothing, and gets its knowledge by being corrected.** For chickens cackle and ducks, random weights can give the highest probability to the wrong word. The target puts all the probability on quack, and the gap between them tells training which way to change the weights.", "sub": ["The two principles are to start with a guess and to improve the guess by learning from mistakes.", "Random guessing on its own creates no knowledge. The repeated correction is what turns the guess into knowledge."], "end": "So the system fakes it at the start, and after enough corrections it makes it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p12 · Lecture 10 · p13", "main": "**Training a network is the same loop run with a labelled input. Compute the output, compare it with the target, and change the weights to shrink the error.** For an input whose correct category is the sixth of seven, the target is [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. An output of [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08] puts only 0.12 on the right category, and the gap is the error.", "sub": ["**Backpropagation** carries the output error back through the layers so that every weight can be adjusted with SGD. Repeating this over billions of labelled pairs builds the network's knowledge.", "A mistake in the data gets learned too. In a list of grades with a typo of 400, the estimate is pulled toward 400 and the learned average comes out near 100 instead of near 80. Correct arithmetic does not fix a wrong observation."], "end": "So the loop is guess, measure the mistake, correct a fraction, and repeat, and it learns whatever the data contains, errors included.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p12"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 18", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula? Consider the case where the data point is above the current x and the case where it is below.", "a": "The first minus makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the current estimate. If the current estimate is above the data point, the difference in the parentheses is positive, so subtracting it makes the estimate go down, toward the data point. If the current estimate is below the data point, the difference is negative, so subtracting a negative number makes the estimate go up, again toward the data point. For example, with an estimate of 130 and a data point of 98, the difference is 32, so the estimate goes down. With an estimate of 94 and a data point of 98, the difference is minus 4, so the estimate goes up."}, {"source": "Review 10", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I describe it this way.", "a": "The system starts with completely random weights, so at first it does not know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, gets feedback on how wrong the guess was, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this many times it makes it, which means it ends up with the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 7", "q": "Describe how the stochastic gradient descent algorithm works in your own words.", "a": "We want to estimate a quantity from some existing data, like the average of a list of numbers. We start with a random estimate. Then we pick a random data point and compute the loss, which is the difference between our guess and that data point. We subtract a small fraction of the loss from the guess, and that fraction is the learning rate. That gives a new, slightly better guess. We repeat this with new random data points over and over, and the guess gets closer and closer to the real answer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 15", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I put it this way.", "a": "The system starts out with completely random weights, so it does not actually know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, receives feedback, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this a lot of times, it makes it, which means it has the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 20", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula?", "a": "The minus makes the estimate move toward the data point, whether the data point is above or below the estimate. If the estimate is above the data point, the loss is positive, so subtracting it pushes the estimate down. If the estimate is below the data point, the loss is negative, so subtracting it pushes the estimate up."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The current estimate is 60 and the next data point is 90, with a learning rate of 10 percent. Which way does the estimate move, and by how much?", "a": "The estimate moves up, to 63. This is because the difference, 60 minus 90, is minus 30. Subtracting 10 percent of minus 30, which is minus 3, raises the estimate by 3. The minus in the formula makes the estimate move toward the data point, which is above it."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Can the SGD estimate move away from the true average on a single step? Why?", "a": "Yes. This is because each step moves the estimate toward the one data point that was drawn, not toward the average. If the estimate is 94 and the data point drawn is 98, the estimate goes up even though the average is 80. Over many steps the moves average out and the estimate settles near 80."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A list of exam grades has one typo, 400, in it. What happens when SGD learns the average from this list?", "a": "The learned average comes out too high, near 100 instead of near 80. This is because the step that draws 400 pulls the estimate strongly toward 400, and SGD has no way to know that the observation is wrong. The arithmetic is correct, but it learns the mistake in the data along with everything else."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the words stochastic, gradient and descent each mean in the name of the algorithm?", "a": "Stochastic means the data point is picked at random each step. Gradient means the direction of change that reduces the mistake. Descent means the mistake goes down over the run. Descent does not mean the estimate itself always goes down, because the estimate rises whenever it is below the data point."}]}, {"id": "m1-rate", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 04 and 07", "title": "Learning rate and stiffness: the speed and accuracy tradeoff", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p18 · Lecture 03 · p30 · Lecture 07 · p15", "main": "**A bigger learning rate makes the estimate reach the right region faster but keeps it jumping around once it is there.** Three runs start at 130, at 80 and at 10, and all head toward the average near 80. At 1 percent the runs are smooth but slow, and after 300 steps the run from 10 is still below 80. At 10 percent they arrive fairly fast and then wobble a little.", "sub": ["At 50 percent the runs arrive quickly but make big jumps around 80. At 80 percent each observation throws the estimate a long way, so it keeps bouncing over a wide range.", "A big rate gives each observation a lot of influence, so an estimate near the average is easily pulled away by one data point. A small rate gives little jitter but needs many observations to travel far from a bad start."], "end": "So the run that starts near the answer does fine with a small rate, and a run that starts far away needs a bigger rate to get there in time.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p18"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p31", "main": "**This is the speed and accuracy tradeoff.** Raising the learning rate raises the speed and lowers the accuracy, because the estimate reacts more to each observation and keeps fluctuating. Lowering the learning rate lowers the speed and raises the accuracy, because each correction is small and the fluctuations near the average are small.", "sub": ["Optimization research looks for methods that get both speed and accuracy. The tradeoff describes the problem those methods try to solve."], "end": "So no single learning rate is both the fastest and the most accurate, and the choice is a compromise.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18 · Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**Stiffness does for a spring what the learning rate does for SGD, because both set how strongly the system corrects a discrepancy.** A viscous spring with no mass follows the rule that the change in position is a negative number times the position, so a positive position falls toward zero and a negative one rises toward zero. A bigger number in that rule, which is more stiffness, means a faster return.", "sub": ["Hot coffee cools toward room temperature and iced coffee warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is big and slows as the gap shrinks, so different starts reach the same equilibrium from opposite sides.", "The SGD estimate does the same, except its path is noisy because each observation is random, while the coffee curve is smooth."], "end": "So a high learning rate and a high stiffness both mean a fast correction, and a low learning rate and a low stiffness both mean a slow one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 2", "q": "What is the role of the learning rate in stochastic gradient descent and the role of stiffness in a viscous springy system? What happens when the learning rate is high versus low, and when stiffness is high versus low?", "a": "The learning rate determines how big a fraction of each mistake gets corrected, and stiffness determines how strongly the spring pulls back toward equilibrium (its resting position). With a high learning rate, each correction is large, so the estimate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, so it is less accurate. With a low learning rate, each correction is small, so the estimate moves slowly but settles steadily, so it is more accurate but slow. With high stiffness, the spring pulls back hard, so it returns to equilibrium fast. With low stiffness, it pulls back weakly, so it returns slowly. In both systems a bigger setting means a faster response."}, {"source": "Review 9", "q": "What is the basic tradeoff that we see in both the shortest path problem and the stochastic gradient descent algorithm?", "a": "The tradeoff is speed against accuracy. In the shortest path problem, the greedy algorithm is fast but can pick the wrong path, and the exhaustive algorithm always finds the right path but is slow. Dynamic programming balances the two. In stochastic gradient descent, a high learning rate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, and a low learning rate settles accurately but slowly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 10", "q": "Imagine a stochastic gradient descent algorithm with a very low learning rate, and a springy system with no mass and a low stiffness. (a) Describe how the SGD estimate and the spring's position evolve with time. (b) How would the evolution in time differ if the learning rate and the stiffness increased? For SGD, ignore accuracy and oscillation.", "a": "Part (a). Both move toward their goal slowly. With a low learning rate, the SGD estimate only takes a small correction toward the average each time, so it takes many steps to get there. With low stiffness, the spring only pulls weakly, so it drifts back to its equilibrium position slowly.\n\nPart (b). Both would get there faster. A higher learning rate makes bigger corrections toward the average, so the estimate arrives in fewer steps. A stiffer spring pulls harder, so it snaps back to equilibrium more quickly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two SGD runs use the same data. One has a learning rate of 1 percent and the other 80 percent. Describe how each estimate moves over time.", "a": "The 1 percent run moves slowly and smoothly toward the average, and if it starts far away it may not have arrived after hundreds of steps. The 80 percent run reaches the region of the average very fast, but then each observation throws it a long way, so it keeps bouncing around instead of settling. The small rate is accurate but slow, and the big rate is fast but inaccurate."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A spring with no mass is released from a stretched position. Compare a stiff spring with a loose one.", "a": "Both return to equilibrium and stop there without crossing it. The stiff spring returns fast, because it pulls back hard, so the position drops toward zero quickly. The loose spring returns slowly, because it pulls back weakly. Neither one overshoots, because there is no mass to carry the motion past equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a cooling cup of coffee and an SGD estimate have in common?", "a": "Both move toward a goal by correcting a discrepancy, and the correction is big when the gap is big and small when the gap is small. The coffee's temperature moves toward room temperature, and the estimate moves toward the data. The difference is that the coffee curve is smooth, while the estimate's path jitters because each observation is random."}]}, {"id": "m1-paths", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "title": "Shortest paths: greedy, exhaustive and dynamic programming", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p3", "main": "**A shortest path problem asks for the cheapest way from the start state to the goal state through a series of stages.** The circles are states, the arrows are the allowed moves, and the number on an arrow is the cost of that move. From A you can go to B for 12 or to C for 7, and the stages continue through D, E and F, then G, H and I, then J.", "sub": ["A route's total is the sum of every arrow it follows. A cheap first arrow does not make a route cheap, because later arrows can undo its advantage."], "end": "So the quantity that matters is the total cost of the whole route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p4", "main": "**The greedy algorithm takes the cheapest arrow out of whatever state it is in, and it can lose.** From A it takes C, because 7 is less than 12. From C it takes E, because 10 is the smallest. From E it takes H, because 7 is the smallest. Then H to J costs 9, and the route totals 33.", "sub": ["Greedy is fast, because it only looks at the next arrow. It is inaccurate, because it ignores the costs that are still ahead, and an expensive first step can lead to a cheaper whole route."], "end": "So greedy is fast but can pick the wrong route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p6", "main": "**The exhaustive algorithm works out the total of every complete route and picks the cheapest, so it is always right and very slow.** For this problem it finds the true minimum, because it compares every route.", "sub": ["Every added state or stage multiplies the number of complete routes, so the work grows very fast. The routes overlap, so the same additions get done over and over."], "end": "So exhaustive search is accurate but inefficient.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p7 · Lecture 04 · p8", "main": "**Dynamic programming breaks the big problem into smaller problems that it contains, solves the smallest first, and reuses their answers.** Call the best cost of finishing from B the value of B, and the same for C. If those two values are known, the decision at A is the smaller of 12 plus the value of B and 7 plus the value of C.", "sub": ["A **value** is the cost of the whole remaining problem from a state, not the cost of one arrow. The values of B and C are unknown at first, so each one becomes a smaller problem, the cheapest way to finish from that state.", "Breaking problems into smaller problems is Bellman's idea, and it keeps going until the remaining problem is trivial."], "end": "So a hard problem is solved by solving the easy problems inside it and building back up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p9 · Lecture 04 · p10 · Lecture 04 · p13 · Lecture 04 · p16", "main": "**The values are worked out backward from the goal, and then the route is followed forward from the start.** The last states have values that are just their final arrow, so G is 5, H is 9 and I is 8. For D, compare 8 plus 5, 7 plus 9 and 10 plus 8, and the smallest, 13, is the value of D. The same rule gives E the value 14 and F the value 15.", "sub": ["Then B is 18 through D and C is 24 through E. At A the choice is 12 plus 18 through B, which is 30, or 7 plus 24 through C, which is 31. So the best route is A to B to D to G to J, costing 30, even though its first arrow is the expensive one.", "The rule at every state is the same. For each possible next state, add the cost of getting there to that state's value, and keep the smallest sum. Remember which next state gave it, so the route can be read off forward afterward."], "end": "So the calculation runs backward from the goal, and the route is lived forward from the start.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p11 · Lecture 04 · p12", "main": "**Dynamic programming is more efficient than exhaustive search because the routes overlap, and it solves each overlapping part only once.** Exhaustive search redoes the same additions for every route through a state. Dynamic programming finds the best way to finish from each state once and reuses it for every route that passes through.", "sub": ["A costly arrow far ahead, like the 9 from H to J, is carried backward into the values, so the choice at A already knows about it.", "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, exhaustive is accurate but slow, and dynamic programming is the balance."], "end": "So the value of a state summarizes the whole future, and that is what lets a local choice be a good one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 5", "q": "Why is the exhaustive algorithm so inefficient for solving the shortest path problem?", "a": "The exhaustive algorithm is inefficient because it works out the total cost of every complete path separately. The paths overlap, so the same additions get done over and over. Every time a stage is added, the number of complete paths multiplies, so the work grows very fast. It does find the true shortest path, but for a big problem it takes far too long."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 4", "q": "Describe how dynamic programming works in terms of the size of problems, and problems containing other problems.", "a": "You break the big problem into smaller subproblems, and keep breaking those down until you reach small problems that are trivial to solve. You solve those smallest problems first. Then you use their answers to solve the larger problems that contain them, and keep going up until you reach the hardest problem, which is the original one."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 5", "q": "Describe why dynamic programming is more efficient than the exhaustive algorithm.", "a": "The paths in a shortest path problem overlap. The exhaustive algorithm solves every path separately, so it redoes the same additions over and over. Dynamic programming solves only the non-overlapping parts. It finds the best solution from each state only once and reuses it for every path through that state, so it never repeats work."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 12", "q": "In the shortest path problem shown, what is the greedy forward solution? Write out the numbers with plus signs.", "a": "The greedy forward solution is 3 plus 3 plus 4 plus 2, which is 12, along the path A to B to E to F to H. This is because greedy takes the cheapest arrow out of each state as it goes. From A the cheapest arrow is 3 to B. From B it is 3 to E. From E it is 4 to F. From F the only arrow is 2 to H. The greedy total is 12, and it is not the best path. The cheapest complete path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 13", "q": "In the same shortest path problem, we call the best cost from any state x to state H V x H. Imagine we have already solved for V D H and V E H. How can I calculate V C H?", "a": "V C H is the smaller of two sums, 2 plus V D H and 1 plus V E H. This is because from C you can only go to D, which costs 2, or to E, which costs 1. Each sum adds the cost of that first step to the best cost of finishing from the state you land in. Taking the minimum is the important part, because the best cost from C is the cheaper of the two complete ways of finishing.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In a shortest path problem, what is the difference between the cost of an arrow and the value of a state?", "a": "The cost of an arrow is the price of one move. The value of a state is the cheapest total cost of finishing from that state all the way to the goal, which adds up several arrows. Dynamic programming uses values, because a value already includes every cost that is still ahead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does dynamic programming work backward from the goal?", "a": "It works backward because a state's value can only be computed once the values of the states after it are known. The last states are easy, because their value is just their final arrow. Each earlier state then adds one arrow to a known value and keeps the smallest sum. Once every value is known, the route is read off forward from the start."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, the greedy path from A to H cost 12. What is the cheapest path, and why does greedy miss it?", "a": "The cheapest path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11. Greedy misses it because the first arrow to C costs 5, which is more than the 3 to B, so greedy never goes to C. The cheap arrows after C, 2 and 1, are what make the whole route cheaper, and greedy cannot see them from A.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, we know the values V F H and V G H. How do we calculate V D H?", "a": "V D H is the smaller of 3 plus V F H and 1 plus V G H. This is because from D you can go to F for 3 or to G for 1, and each sum adds that first step to the best cost of finishing from where you land. The minimum of the two is the best cost from D.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}]}, {"id": "m1-least-time", "tier": "Possible", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "title": "Least distance is not least time", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p14", "main": "**The fastest route is not always the shortest route, because the cost of a stretch depends on the conditions on it.** On uniform sand, where speed is the same everywhere, the straight line is both shortest and fastest. A rescuer who runs faster on sand than they swim in water should run farther along the sand before entering the water, so the fastest route bends at the shore.", "sub": ["The extra distance on the fast sand saves more time than it costs, because it shortens the slow swim.", "This is the same idea as paying the expensive 12 to B in the shortest path problem, because the cheaper continuation more than pays it back."], "end": "So the thing to minimize is the total time or total cost of the whole route, not the length of its first stretch.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p14"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p15", "main": "**Light takes the least-time route too, and that is why it bends at the boundary between air and water.** Light moves faster in air than in water, so its path changes direction at the surface, the way the rescuer's path changes direction at the shore. A pencil in a glass of water looks bent because the light reaching the eye followed a bent path.", "sub": ["The pencil itself is straight. The bend is in the path of the light."], "end": "So a locally shorter stretch is not always best when the rest of the route runs under different conditions.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can the fastest route be longer than the shortest route?", "a": "Because the route crosses regions where the speed is different. Spending extra distance in the fast region shortens the distance in the slow region, and the time saved there is bigger than the time added. So the route with the least total time bends toward the fast region and is longer than the straight line."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a lifeguard running to a swimmer, a ray of light entering water, and the shortest path problem have in common?", "a": "In all three, the best choice for the whole route is not the cheapest first step. The lifeguard runs farther on sand because swimming is slow. Light bends at the water because it travels slower in water. In the path problem the expensive first arrow leads to the cheaper total. What is minimized is the total time or total cost over the whole route."}]}, {"id": "m1-oscillator", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "title": "Position and velocity: why a mass on a spring oscillates", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p3 · Lecture 07 · p2", "main": "**A harmonic oscillator has two quantities that change each other, position x and velocity v.** Position is where the mass is relative to its resting position. Velocity is how fast the position is changing. A dot over a quantity means its rate of change, so x dot is how position changes and v dot is how velocity changes.", "sub": ["The first rule is that x dot equals v. Velocity determines how position changes, and the sign stays the same, so positive velocity makes position go up.", "The second rule is that v dot equals minus x. Position determines how velocity changes, with the sign reversed, so positive position makes velocity go down and negative position makes velocity go up."], "end": "So position and velocity keep changing each other through a feedback loop.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p4 · Lecture 05–06 · p5", "main": "**The rules say which way a quantity is changing, not whether the quantity is positive right now.** When v is plus 5, position rises. When v is minus 5, position falls. When x is plus 5, velocity goes down. When x is minus 5, velocity goes up.", "sub": ["Velocity going up can mean going from a big negative number toward zero, which is slowing down, not speeding up in the same direction.", "Keeping the quantity and its rate of change separate is what makes the back and forth motion understandable."], "end": "So read each rule as a direction of change.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p6 · Lecture 05–06 · p7 · Lecture 07 · p3", "main": "**Following the two rules around the loop gives repeated motion, not a single return to the middle.** Start with a positive position and zero velocity. Positive x makes velocity become negative. Negative velocity makes x fall. As x nears zero the push on velocity weakens, but the velocity is still negative, so it carries x through zero to the negative side.", "sub": ["Now negative x makes velocity rise. Velocity passes through zero and becomes positive, so x turns around and rises back through zero. Then positive x starts pushing velocity down again, and the cycle repeats.", "Position and velocity are coordinated but do not peak together. Position is at a turning point when velocity is zero, and velocity is biggest when position passes through zero.", "The two arrows, from x to v and from v to x, close the loop, so one change leads to another and back again. That is what oscillation is."], "end": "So two simple rules, each the cause of the other, produce vibration.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p4 · Lecture 07 · p7", "main": "**A spring and a mass make vibration because the spring wants to return to equilibrium and the moving mass wants to keep moving.** The spring's goal is to get back to its resting position, and **stiffness** is how strongly it pulls back. A mass in motion stays in motion unless something changes it, which is **inertia**. The spring can pull the mass back to the middle, but the mass arrives moving, so it goes through, and the spring has to slow it and turn it around from the other side.", "sub": ["The same back and forth shows up in a car after a bump, in music, in boom and bust, in the SGD estimate swinging around the average, in the air in the vocal tract and in the vocal folds."], "end": "So the goal of the spring plus the inertia of the mass is the whole explanation of oscillation.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p8 · Lecture 08 · p3", "main": "**A spring on its own returns to equilibrium and stops, and a spring with a mass overshoots and keeps going.** Pull a spring alone to plus 1 and let go, and it comes back toward 0 and flattens out without crossing. Push it to minus 1 and it rises toward 0 without crossing. Attach a mass, release it from plus 1, and it moves to 0 and keeps going, because it is already moving. The spring is then compressed, pulls back, and turns the mass around, and the mass carries it through the next crossing too.", "sub": ["The trace of the mass on the spring crosses zero again and again, with each swing a little smaller when there is light damping.", "The resting position is the same in both cases. What differs is whether the motion stops there or passes through."], "end": "So the mass is the difference between settling and oscillating.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 16", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If it starts with x negative and v equal to 0, will it oscillate? Say in a couple of sentences what will happen.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium (x equal to 0). The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it goes past 0 and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. So the position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Review 15", "q": "Some people say that everything is a harmonic oscillator. Give 5 examples of oscillatory systems we discussed in class or in the Meadows book.", "a": "A mass on a spring swinging back and forth. The air in the vocal tract vibrating during speech, with its particles moving closer together and farther apart. The vocal folds opening and closing hundreds of times a second. The estimate in stochastic gradient descent swinging around the average when the learning rate is high. The economy going through boom and bust around its growth trend. A car bouncing after it goes over a bump. In the Meadows book, a room temperature swinging above and below the setting when the thermostat is far from the heater, and a car dealership's inventory swinging up and down because orders arrive after a delay. Any five of these work."}, {"source": "Review 4", "q": "Two temperature control systems are set to 70. In the first, the thermostat is right next to the heating and cooling unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, the temperature drops to 70, and the thermostat detects that and turns the unit off. Below 70, the heat turns on until 70 and then stops. In the second, the thermostat is far from the unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, but the thermostat does not detect 70, so the temperature keeps dropping to 60. Then the heat turns on, the temperature rises past 70 to 80 before the thermostat notices, and so on. Which system is like a spring by itself, and which is like a mass-spring system?", "a": "The first system, with the thermostat next to the unit, is like a spring by itself. This is because it moves toward its goal of 70 and stops there, the way a spring alone returns to equilibrium and stays. The second system, with the far thermostat, is like a mass-spring system. This is because it keeps going past 70, to 60 and then to 80, and swings back and forth around the goal. A moving mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so a mass-spring system overshoots and oscillates in the same way. The delay in detecting the temperature plays the part of the mass."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 17", "q": "What is the goal of a spring, if it is extended or squeezed? What is the primary physical property of a mass that is already moving?", "a": "The goal of a spring is to go back to equilibrium, its resting position. The primary property of a mass that is already moving is that it keeps on moving. Together these two facts are why a mass on a spring oscillates, because the spring keeps pulling the mass back and the mass keeps going past the middle."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 18", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If x starts as negative and v is 0, will the system oscillate? How will the position of the mass evolve with time?", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts out negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium at x equal to 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A mass on a spring starts with x positive and v equal to 0. Will it oscillate? Describe what happens.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position starts positive, so the spring is stretched and pulls the mass back toward equilibrium at 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots to the negative side and compresses the spring. The spring then pushes it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth as a cosine-shaped wave, starting from its high point."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the oscillator rules, x dot equals v and v dot equals minus x. If x is plus 5 right now, is the mass moving up or down?", "a": "You cannot tell from x alone. The rules only say that a positive x makes the velocity go down. Whether the mass is moving up or down right now depends on the current velocity. If v is still positive the mass is moving up while slowing, and if v is already negative it is moving down."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a mass on a spring keep passing through the middle instead of stopping there?", "a": "Because at the middle the spring is at its resting position and pulls with no force, but the mass is moving as fast as it ever does. A moving mass keeps moving unless something changes its motion, so it goes through. Only after it is past the middle does the spring start pulling it back."}]}, {"id": "m1-balancing", "tier": "On the review sheet", "lectures": "Lectures 04, 05–06, 07 and 09", "title": "A spring alone, a cooling cup and balancing feedback", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**A viscous spring with no mass obeys one rule, the change in x is minus one third of x, so it returns to equilibrium and stops.** If x is positive, its change is negative, so x falls. If x is negative, its change is positive, so x rises. Both lead to the same resting position at 0.", "sub": ["Starting from 1, the first return is big, because one third of 1 is a big step. As x gets smaller, its rate of change gets smaller too, so the curve drops steeply at first and then flattens. From below zero it rises and flattens.", "This is **balancing feedback** (negative feedback), because the system acts against the discrepancy from equilibrium. The motion reduces the very displacement that causes it, and when x is 0 nothing drives it any more.", "A bigger number in the rule, such as minus 0.9 instead of minus 0.1, means a faster return. A smaller number means a slower return."], "end": "So without a mass there is no overshoot, only a return that slows as it gets close.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p3 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**A differential equation says how a system changes from the state it is in now, and together with a starting state it predicts the whole future.** The rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, with x starting at 100, gives a change of about minus 33, so the next value is about 67. The same rule on 67 gives a smaller step, and the value heads toward 0 while the curve flattens.", "sub": ["The same rule describes a spring, a cup of coffee and a learning system, because all three correct a gap, and the correction shrinks as the gap shrinks.", "Repeating the arithmetic step by step is Euler's method."], "end": "So knowing the rule of change and the starting point is enough to predict the time course.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18", "main": "**Learning and a cooling cup of coffee share the same shape of approach to equilibrium.** Hot coffee at 60, 80 or 100 degrees cools toward room temperature at 18, and iced coffee at 0, 5 or 10 degrees warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is large and slows as the gap shrinks, so every cup ends at the same temperature from either side.", "sub": ["The SGD estimate behaves the same way from 130 above or 10 below, except that its path is noisy, because each observation is random."], "end": "So a correction that opposes a discrepancy is the shared organization of both.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 17", "q": "If x dot equals minus 0.1 times x describes a system, and x starts negative, what will happen? What happens if the number minus 0.1 becomes minus 0.9?", "a": "The position x rises toward 0 and stops there. This is because x is negative, so minus 0.1 times x is positive, so x goes up. As x gets closer to 0 the change gets smaller, so it rises fast at first and then more and more slowly, and it never crosses 0. With minus 0.9 the same thing happens much faster, because each step changes x by 90 percent of the gap instead of 10 percent. There is no mass in this system, so there is no overshoot and no oscillation."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A system follows the rule that the change in x is minus 0.1 times x, and x starts positive. What happens? What if it starts at exactly 0?", "a": "If x starts positive it falls toward 0 and stops there. This is because minus 0.1 times a positive x is negative, so x goes down, and the step gets smaller as x gets smaller, so it never crosses 0.\n\nIf x starts at exactly 0, nothing happens, because minus 0.1 times 0 is 0, so there is no change. The system is already at equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a spring with no mass never overshoot its resting position?", "a": "Because the only thing driving the motion is the discrepancy from equilibrium. As the spring gets close to its resting position the discrepancy shrinks, so the motion shrinks, and at the resting position the motion is zero. There is no mass to carry the motion past that point. With a mass, the mass arrives moving and keeps moving, and that is what makes the overshoot."}]}, {"id": "m1-mass-stiffness", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "title": "Mass, stiffness and frequency, from springs to vocal folds", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p13 · Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**More mass lowers the frequency of a mass-spring system, and more stiffness raises it.** **Perturbation theory** asks how the behavior changes when one setting of the system is changed. When the mass gets heavier, the same spring has more to slow down and turn around, so each cycle takes longer and fewer cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes down.", "sub": ["When the spring gets stiffer, it pulls the mass back more strongly for the same displacement, so the turns happen sooner and more cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes up.", "Mass and stiffness therefore have opposite effects on frequency."], "end": "So to predict a frequency change, ask whether mass or stiffness changed, and in which direction.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p8", "main": "**The vocal folds are small muscles, about 1 centimeter long and 1 millimeter high, and their mass and stiffness set the pitch of the voice.** About ten other muscles act on them through the surrounding cartilages. Stretching the folds makes them stiffer, so they vibrate faster and the pitch goes up. Bunching them makes them effectively heavier, so they vibrate more slowly and the pitch goes down.", "sub": ["The rate of vibration is called **F0**, the fundamental frequency. Changing it is how we sing, ask a question, show surprise or doubt, and in tone languages tell one word from another.", "The folds vibrate because air pressure from the lungs builds up below the closed folds and pushes them apart, the fast air between them sucks them back together (the Bernoulli force, like a shower curtain pulled inward), pressure builds again, and this repeats hundreds of times a second."], "end": "So the vocal folds are a mass-spring system made of tissue, and the air above them is a separate vibrating system.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 24", "q": "If I raise the stiffness of a mass-spring system, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall? And if I raise the mass, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall?", "a": "Raising the stiffness makes the frequency rise, because a stiffer spring pulls the mass back faster, so each cycle takes less time. Raising the mass makes the frequency fall, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around, so each cycle takes more time."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A singer stretches the vocal folds tighter. What happens to the pitch, and why?", "a": "The pitch goes up. This is because stretching the folds makes them stiffer, and a stiffer system springs back faster, so it vibrates more times per second. More vibrations per second is a higher fundamental frequency, which is heard as a higher pitch."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If the mass of a mass-spring system is doubled and the stiffness is also doubled, what can you say about the frequency?", "a": "The two changes work against each other. Doubling the mass lowers the frequency, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around. Doubling the stiffness raises it, because a stiffer spring pulls back harder. For a single mass on a single spring the two effects cancel, so the frequency stays the same."}]}, {"id": "m1-air", "tier": "On the review sheet", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 05–06 and 07", "title": "Air vibration: compression, rarefaction and frequency", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p4", "main": "**The air between the tongue and the walls of the vocal tract can vibrate, and that vibration is the sound of speech.** The curved vocal tract can be straightened into a tube to understand the motion of its air. In the tube the rows of air particles move back and forth, so the air is more crowded in some places and less crowded in others.", "sub": ["Moving the tongue changes the shape of the air space, which is **articulatory phonetics**. The sound the vibrating air makes is **acoustic phonetics**."], "end": "So the visible space above the tongue and the physical vibration that carries sound are the same thing seen two ways.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/004.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/1b8ee778658fd124057c.mp4", "box": [33.28, 42.3, 67.11, 22.08], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/1b8ee778658fd124057c.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/1b8ee778658fd124057c.mp4", "box": [33.28, 42.3, 67.11, 22.08], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/1b8ee778658fd124057c.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p10 · Lecture 07 · p5", "main": "**A vibration in air is a repeated change between compression and rarefaction.** A slice of air keeps the same particles the whole time. In **compression** the particles move closer together and the pressure is high. In **rarefaction** they move farther apart and the pressure is low. The particles do not change size or number, only their spacing.", "sub": ["At equilibrium the spacing is 100 percent. In the compressed phase it drops to about 60 percent, and in the spread-out phase it rises to about 140 percent, exaggerated so it can be seen.", "The slice contracts, expands back through equilibrium, reaches rarefaction, and contracts again, over and over."], "end": "So sound in air is particles swinging closer and farther apart around their resting spacing, like a mass on a spring.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/010.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/e8fcd6445835f8428280.mp4", "box": [22.55, 24.65, 50.92, 67.89], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e8fcd6445835f8428280.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/e8fcd6445835f8428280.mp4", "box": [22.55, 24.65, 50.92, 67.89], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e8fcd6445835f8428280.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p11 · Lecture 07 · p9", "main": "**The expansion of the air slice follows a cosine, and one full cycle is one round trip from spread out to compressed and back.** At the start the value is plus 1 and the particles are farthest apart. The value falls through 0, the equilibrium spacing, to minus 1, the closest spacing, and rises back to plus 1. Three cycles fit in the graph.", "sub": ["The moving point on the curve shows the spacing at that moment. The graph is a record of expansion over time, not a path that the particles travel along."], "end": "So positive values mean rarefaction, negative values mean compression, and the curve repeats.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/011.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.mp4", "box": [16.19, 21.29, 63.45, 63.45], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.mp4", "box": [16.19, 21.29, 63.45, 63.45], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p11", "main": "**Frequency is how many complete cycles fit into a fixed amount of time, measured in cycles per second, or hertz.** Three air slices swing by the same amount. One completes 3 cycles in 12 seconds, one completes 6, and one completes 9. The one with 9 cycles has three times the frequency of the one with 3.", "sub": ["Equal swings do not mean equal frequencies. The particles can spread and compress by the same amount at very different rates."], "end": "So frequency is about how fast the cycle repeats, not how big the swing is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/011.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.mp4", "box": [7.62, 9.19, 87.2, 87.2], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.mp4", "box": [7.62, 9.19, 87.2, 87.2], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p15", "main": "**Speaking involves two different vibrating things, the vocal folds and the air in the vocal tract.** The folds are moving tissue. The air is changing compression and rarefaction. An explanation of speech has to account for both.", "end": "So the voice has a source, the folds, and a vibrating air column above it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between compression and rarefaction, and what stays the same during both?", "a": "In compression the particles of air move closer together and the pressure goes up. In rarefaction they move farther apart and the pressure goes down. What stays the same is the particles themselves. The slice keeps the same particles, with the same size and number, and only their spacing changes."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vibrations have the same amplitude, but one completes 4 cycles in 12 seconds and the other completes 8. Which has the higher frequency, and by how much?", "a": "The one with 8 cycles has the higher frequency, twice as high. This is because frequency is the number of cycles in a fixed time, and 8 cycles in 12 seconds is twice 4 cycles in 12 seconds. The amplitude, how far the particles spread and compress, does not change the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is the cosine graph of the air slice not a drawing of where the particles go?", "a": "The graph shows how spread out the slice is over time. The height of the curve is the spacing at that moment, plus 1 for farthest apart and minus 1 for closest together. The particles themselves only move back and forth along the tube. They do not travel along a wavy path."}]}, {"id": "m1-hearing", "tier": "Asked on Exam 1", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "title": "Hearing: the cochlea and the basilar membrane", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p5 · Lecture 08 · p6", "main": "**Sound travels from the air into the ear and reaches the cochlea, the coiled structure of the inner ear that sends frequency information to the brain.** The vibration moves the eardrum (the tympanic membrane), passes through three small bones, the malleus, incus and stapes, and enters the cochlea.", "sub": ["Inside the cochlea runs the **basilar membrane**, a long strip from the entrance to the far end at the helicotrema. Thinking of it uncoiled makes it easier to see how different places along one strip can respond differently.", "The word to memorize is cochlea. The other labels on the diagram show where things are and do not need to be memorized."], "end": "So the path is air, eardrum, three bones, cochlea, basilar membrane.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p14 · Lecture 08 · p7", "main": "**The basilar membrane sorts a sound into its frequencies because its mass and stiffness change along its length.** The wide end is heavy and loose, so it has a lot of mass and little stiffness, and both of those favor low frequencies. The narrow end is light and stiff, and both of those favor high frequencies. Places in between respond to frequencies in between.", "sub": ["Extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. Extra stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. That is the same rule as for a mass on a spring.", "Nerve fibers leave every place along the membrane, so which fibers are active tells the brain where the vibration is strongest, and that tells the brain which frequencies are present. Georg von Békésy won the 1961 Nobel Prize for this work."], "end": "So a mixture of frequencies makes several places vibrate at once, and the brain reads the mixture off the pattern of active nerves.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 3", "q": "What is the physical principle behind how your auditory system hears different frequencies? Describe the basic physics of the basilar membrane to show how the system works.", "a": "The principle is that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The basilar membrane (the long strip inside the cochlea) is heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other end. The heavy, loose end vibrates most for low frequencies, because extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. The light, stiff end vibrates most for high frequencies, because stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. Each place along the membrane is connected to its own neurons. So which neurons fire tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells the brain which frequencies are in the sound."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 3", "q": "One side of the basilar membrane is heavy and not stiff, while the other side is light and very stiff. How do these facts enable hearing?", "a": "Frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. So the heavy, non-stiff end of the membrane vibrates at low frequencies, and the light, stiff end vibrates at high frequencies. Each part of the membrane is hooked to neurons. Those neurons tell the brain which frequencies were heard, based on which location is vibrating."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which end of the basilar membrane responds to a high-pitched sound, and why?", "a": "The light, stiff end responds to a high-pitched sound. This is because a high pitch is a high frequency, and a part with little mass is easy to move while a stiff part springs back fast, so that part vibrates quickly. The heavy, loose end vibrates slowly, so it responds to low frequencies instead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does the brain know which frequencies are in a sound, if all it gets is nerve signals?", "a": "Each place along the basilar membrane is connected to its own nerve fibers, and each place vibrates most for one frequency. So the set of nerve fibers that is active tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells it which frequencies are present. The brain reads frequency from location."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the basilar membrane and a mass-spring system have in common?", "a": "Both follow the rule that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The membrane is like a row of mass-spring systems, heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other, so each place has its own natural frequency. That is what lets it separate a sound into its frequencies."}]}, {"id": "m1-euler", "tier": "Possible", "lectures": "Lectures 08 and 09", "title": "Euler's method: arithmetic that approximates continuous change", "note": "", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p11 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**Euler's method turns a rule about rates of change into a sequence of numbers by taking small time steps.** The new value is the old value plus the rate of change times the size of the time step. Repeating that arithmetic gives an approximate record of how the system moves through time.", "sub": ["The result is approximate, so the size of the time step and the order of the updates both affect what comes out.", "For the rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, starting at 100 gives about 67, then a smaller step, and so on toward 0."], "end": "So a computer simulates a dynamical system by adding up many small changes.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p10", "main": "**Explicit Euler updates position and velocity from the old pair, and with a big time step its swings grow.** With a time step of 0.5, starting at x equal to 1 and v equal to 0, the new position is the old velocity times 0.5 plus the old position, and the new velocity is minus the old position times 0.5 plus the old velocity. The first step gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second gives x of 0.75 and v of minus 1.", "sub": ["The values keep changing sign, but each swing gets bigger, reaching past 2 and past 3 within twelve steps.", "That growth comes from the approximation with this time step, not from the real oscillator gaining energy."], "end": "So the same physics simulated two ways can look different, and this way looks like it is blowing up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p9", "main": "**Position-first Euler updates the position first and then uses the new position to update the velocity, and its swings stay the same size.** The first step again gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second step gives x of 0.75, and then the velocity uses that new 0.75, so v is minus 0.875 instead of minus 1.", "sub": ["The sequence stays between about minus 1 and plus 1, crossing zero again and again, which is what the real oscillator does.", "The order matters because the second calculation receives information from the first."], "end": "So the way the arithmetic is arranged changes the answer, and the position-first order keeps the oscillation bounded.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p9"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does Euler's method do, and why is its answer only approximate?", "a": "Euler's method takes a rule for how fast something changes and turns it into a list of values, by adding the rate of change times a small time step to the current value, over and over. It is only approximate because the rate is treated as constant during each step, when really it keeps changing. A smaller time step makes the approximation closer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two simulations of the same mass-spring system use the same time step, but one keeps growing and the other stays bounded. What is the difference?", "a": "The difference is the order of the updates. The simulation that grows is explicit Euler, which computes the new position and the new velocity both from the old pair. The one that stays bounded updates the position first and then uses that new position to update the velocity. The physics is the same, so the growth is a mistake of the arithmetic, not a property of the oscillator."}]}]}}, "bytopic": {"title": "Notes by topic · Lectures 01–10", "lead": "Each topic brings together every slide that teaches it, from every lecture in Module I, said once. Read the notes with their slide or video beside them, then answer the questions.", "topics": [{"id": "m1-consonants", "title": "Consonants: voicing, place, manner and nasality", "lectures": "Lectures 01 and 02", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**A consonant is made by narrowing or blocking the air somewhere in the mouth, and voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate while you do it.** Say f as in fat and hold it, then say v as in vat and hold it. The lips and teeth stay in the same place for both, so the narrowing is the same. The difference is that the vocal folds vibrate for v and not for f, so v is **voiced** and f is **voiceless**.", "sub": ["The same pair happens for th in thing and th in that, for s and z, and for sh and the j sound in beige. In each pair the place stays the same and only the voicing changes.", "These sounds are **fricatives** (narrowing sounds), because the air squeezes through a narrow gap and makes a hissing noise."], "end": "So voicing is one dimension of a consonant, and it can change while everything else stays the same.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p9 · Lecture 02 · p4", "main": "**Place of articulation is where in the mouth the narrowing happens, and the fricatives line up from the front of the mouth to the back.** The f and v are **labial** (made with the lower lip against the upper teeth). The two th sounds are **dental** (tongue tip at the teeth). The s and z are **alveolar** (tongue at the ridge just behind the upper teeth). The sh and the beige j sound are **postalveolar** (a little farther back than the ridge).", "sub": ["In the MRI images the narrowing moves from the front of the mouth toward the back as you go across the table."], "end": "So place is the second dimension, and each fricative is one place combined with one voicing.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p10 · Lecture 02 · p5", "main": "**A stop blocks the air completely somewhere in the mouth, and the stops come in groups of three by place.** The p, b and m are made at the lips (labial). The t, d and n are made at the alveolar ridge. The k, g and ng are made with the back of the tongue raised to the **velum** (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth).", "sub": ["Within each group, p, t and k are voiceless and b, d and g are voiced, so p and b are a voicing pair with the same place, and so are t and d, and k and g.", "The m, n and ng are **nasal stops**. The mouth is blocked in the same place as for b, d and g, but the velum is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose. They are all voiced."], "end": "So m and b share place and voicing and differ only in manner, because m lets the air out through the nose and b does not.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/010.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/af1596660e42d3892bf1.mp4", "box": [39.74, 36.2, 18.05, 27.93], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/af1596660e42d3892bf1.jpg"}, {"video": "assets/original/f6f97d126c32a6a771b6.mp4", "box": [39.71, 67.69, 18.05, 30.06], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/f6f97d126c32a6a771b6.jpg"}, {"video": "assets/original/948f00d8877b60e2367c.mp4", "box": [61.42, 68.76, 18.34, 25.92], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/948f00d8877b60e2367c.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/af1596660e42d3892bf1.mp4", "box": [39.74, 36.2, 18.05, 27.93], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/af1596660e42d3892bf1.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p6", "main": "**Every English consonant is one combination of manner, voicing and place.** **Manner** is how the air is restricted, as a fricative (narrowed), an oral stop (blocked and then released through the mouth) or a nasal stop (blocked in the mouth and let out through the nose). **Voicing** is whether the vocal folds vibrate. **Place** is where the restriction is.", "sub": ["Nasality can be counted as a fourth way to tell consonants apart, because it is whether the velum is lowered to let air through the nose.", "A speaker knows this whole system without ever naming its parts, and that is part of what makes knowing a language a remarkable skill."], "end": "So to say how two consonants are similar and different, compare them one dimension at a time, manner, voicing and place.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p6"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 1", "q": "What is the similarity between m in mad and b in bad, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both m and b are voiced, and both are made at the lips, so they share voicing and place of articulation. The difference is manner (how the air is let out). For m the velum (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth) is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose, and m is a nasal stop. For b the velum is raised, so the air is blocked completely at the lips and then released through the mouth, and b is an oral stop (a plosive)."}, {"source": "Review 6", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish between the consonants at the beginning of the words Pam, Bam, Dam, Tam, Kam and Gam?", "a": "All six are oral stops, so they are distinguished by place of articulation and by voicing. Place is where the air is blocked. The p and b are made at the lips (labial), the t and d at the alveolar ridge behind the upper teeth (alveolar), and the k and g with the back of the tongue at the velum (velar). Voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate. The p, t and k are voiceless, and the b, d and g are voiced. So each sound is one place combined with one voicing."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 2", "q": "Name three ways in which we can distinguish consonants from each other.", "a": "Any three of these four. Voicing, which is whether the vocal folds vibrate. Place of articulation, which is where in the mouth the sound is made, such as labial, dental, alveolar, postalveolar or velar. Manner of articulation, which is how the air is constricted, such as fricative, oral stop or nasal stop. Nasality, which is whether the velum is lowered so that the air escapes through the nose."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 6", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the tongue tip is up at the teeth. In (b) the lower lip is against the upper teeth. Which of these is f as in family, and which is th as in thing?", "a": "The f is (b), and the th is (a). This is because f is a labial sound, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth, which is what (b) shows. The th is a dental sound, made with the tongue tip up at the teeth, which is what (a) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q06.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 8", "q": "What is the similarity between n in neck and d in deck, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "a": "Both are voiced alveolar stops, so they share voicing, place and a complete closure. The difference is that n is a nasal stop, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose. The d is an oral stop, so the air is blocked completely and then released through the mouth."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 19", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the back of the tongue is raised. In (b) the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which of these is k as in Ken, and which is m as in men?", "a": "The k is (a), and the m is (b). This is because k is a velar sound, made with the back of the tongue raised up to the velum, which is what (a) shows. The m is a labial nasal, made with the lips closed and the velum lowered so that air goes through the nose, which is what (b) shows.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q19.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between s in sing and z in zing, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are fricatives made at the alveolar ridge, so they share manner and place. The difference is voicing. For z the vocal folds vibrate, so z is voiced. For s they do not, so s is voiceless."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity between t in tap and n in nap, and what is the difference?", "a": "Both are stops made at the alveolar ridge, so they share place and a complete closure in the mouth. They differ in voicing and in nasality. The t is voiceless and oral, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth. The n is voiced and nasal, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the different ways we distinguish the consonants at the beginning of fat, vat, sing and zing?", "a": "All four are fricatives, so they are distinguished by place and by voicing. The f and v are labial, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth. The s and z are alveolar, made with the tongue at the ridge behind the upper teeth. The f and s are voiceless, and the v and z are voiced."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In one the lips are closed and the velum is raised. In the other the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which is b and which is m?", "a": "The b is the one with the velum raised, and the m is the one with the velum lowered. This is because both are made with the lips closed, so the lips do not tell them apart. For m the lowered velum opens the way to the nose, so the air comes out through the nose and m is nasal. For b the raised velum closes that way, so the air is blocked and then released through the mouth."}]}, {"id": "m1-vowels", "title": "Vowels: tongue height, tongue frontness, and more than one frequency", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p12 · Lecture 07 · p10", "main": "**Vowels are told apart by where the tongue is, by its height and by its frontness.** Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth.", "sub": ["The vowel in dean is high and front. The vowel in dune is high and back. The vowel in Dan is low and front. The vowel in Don is low and back. The vowel in done is in the middle.", "Moving the tongue changes where the air space above the vocal folds is narrow and where it is wide."], "end": "So each vowel is a different shape of the air space, made by a different tongue position.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p13 · Lecture 07 · p12", "main": "**The air in a vowel does not vibrate at a single frequency, it vibrates at a combination of frequencies at once.** The illustrative /i/ model adds a slow vibration and a fast one, 1 cycle per second plus 4 cycles per second. The illustrative /a/ model adds 2 cycles per second plus 3 cycles per second. Both start at the same value, but inside each second they move very differently.", "sub": ["Two air slices can be at different points at the same moment, so one can be compressed while the other is spread out.", "The frequencies in the model are made slow on purpose so that the combination can be seen. Real vowel frequencies are hundreds of cycles per second."], "end": "So what makes /i/ sound different from /a/ is which frequencies are combined, not a single frequency.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/013.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/3d37757b637ee16552ee.mp4", "box": [8.58, 8.16, 88.05, 88.05], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3d37757b637ee16552ee.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/3d37757b637ee16552ee.mp4", "box": [8.58, 8.16, 88.05, 88.05], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/3d37757b637ee16552ee.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p13 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**The tongue shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and that is why one vowel contains several frequencies.** Where the tongue narrows the air space, that part of the air behaves differently from the part where there is more room. More mass makes a part vibrate more slowly, and more stiffness makes a part vibrate faster.", "sub": ["So the vibrating air has a mix of lower, middle and higher frequencies, and the mix depends on the vowel.", "To hear speech, the ear has to pick that mix apart and tell how strong each frequency is. That is how a listener tells scene from son."], "end": "So vowels connect the tongue, the physics of mass and stiffness, and hearing in one chain.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p4"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 13", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at a combination of frequencies at the same time. This is because the tongue shape changes where the air in the vocal tract is constricted (narrowed), so different parts of the air have different mass and stiffness. Different mass and stiffness means different frequencies, and they all sound together. The combination is what makes one vowel sound different from another."}, {"source": "Review 14", "q": "What are the 2 basic ways we distinguish vowels?", "a": "Vowels are distinguished by tongue height and by tongue frontness. Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth. For example, the vowel in dean is high and front, the vowel in dune is high and back, the vowel in Dan is low and front, and the vowel in Don is low and back."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 9", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in mean and moon?", "a": "Both are high vowels, so the tongue is high in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in mean is a front vowel, and the vowel in moon is a back vowel. For moon the lips are also rounded."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 16", "q": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "a": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at several frequencies at once, because the tongue shape gives different parts of the air in the vocal tract different mass and stiffness, so they vibrate at different frequencies together."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in Dan and Don?", "a": "Both are low vowels, so the tongue is low in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in Dan is a front vowel and the vowel in Don is a back vowel."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in dean and Dan?", "a": "Both are front vowels, so the raised part of the tongue is toward the front of the mouth for both. The difference is height. The vowel in dean is high, and the vowel in Dan is low."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does one vowel sound different from another, if both are just air vibrating in the vocal tract?", "a": "The two vowels have different combinations of frequencies. This is because the tongue is in a different place for each vowel, so the air space has a different shape. A different shape gives different parts of the air different mass and stiffness, and different mass and stiffness mean different frequencies. So the air vibrates at a different mix of frequencies for each vowel, and the ear hears the difference in the mix."}]}, {"id": "m1-system", "title": "Speech, the octopus, and what a dynamical system is", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 02 and 03", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 03 · p3", "main": "**A dynamical system is a set of elements with relationships between them, and their organized interaction produces behavior over time toward a goal.** To understand a behavior, name the elements, name the relationships among them, and name the goal their coordinated activity serves.", "sub": ["A list of parts on its own does not explain the behavior. The relationships are what make the parts act together."], "end": "So the question to ask about any system is what the elements are, how they are related, and what they are for.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p4 · Lecture 03 · p7", "main": "**An octopus keeping its den is a dynamical system made of eight arms, the ground and the fish it slaps at.** The arms interact with the ground and with the fish, so the environment is part of the system. A nervous system spread through the head and the body coordinates the arms.", "sub": ["A study of 19 arm slaps found that the octopus combines simple movements, rotation (turning an arm) and translation (moving it along), one after another and at the same time, to make the complex slap.", "The simple movements are planned ahead and then adjusted by feedback about where the moving fish is."], "end": "So a complex action is built from a few simple movements organized together, and the organization, not any one arm, explains the behavior.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p5 · Lecture 01 · p2 · Lecture 02 · p3", "main": "**Speech is a dynamical system whose elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat.** The brain coordinates the relationships among these parts so that the shape of the vocal tract changes in the right way. The goal is to convey a thought.", "sub": ["In the MRI videos the tongue reshapes while the openings in the mouth and throat change, and almost none of this can be seen from outside.", "Listing the parts does not explain speaking. Their changing relationships produce the movement, the same way the arms and the ground together produce the octopus slap."], "end": "So speech, like the octopus, is explained by organized interaction, not by a list of parts.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/005.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.mp4", "box": [58.11, 21.24, 35.42, 62.96], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.mp4", "box": [58.11, 21.24, 35.42, 62.96], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/0898ade8d2c896d568bc.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p5 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p6", "main": "**Very different systems can share the same organization, and that is why studying a simple one helps with a complicated one.** The heart, the pancreas, neurons and voluntary movements can all be studied as dynamical systems, with elements that are organs, cells or chemicals.", "sub": ["Seeing the deep similarity between two systems that look different is creative reasoning, as opposed to treating things that merely look alike as the same."], "end": "So the same questions about elements, relationships and goals work for speech, for the body, and for AI.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p3", "main": "**Children learn the extremely complex movements of speech with almost no teaching and no way to see their own speech organs.** By about age two to four they use a large vocabulary and most of the structure of adult language. Reading and writing, by contrast, develop around age six or seven and need a lot of instruction.", "sub": ["**Articulatory phonetics** studies how the structures of the vocal tract move to make speech, and MRI makes those hidden movements visible.", "Voice assistants and other speech technology copy parts of this human skill, which is why speech science and technology belong together."], "end": "So learning to speak is an impressive motor achievement, and speech technology is an attempt to build the same skill.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/003.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/a90271fcfe5e68c46df0.mp4", "box": [71.96, 39.43, 18.0, 31.99], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/a90271fcfe5e68c46df0.jpg"}, {"video": "assets/original/caa27e684380371174b1.mp4", "box": [71.96, 3.86, 18.0, 31.99], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/caa27e684380371174b1.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/a90271fcfe5e68c46df0.mp4", "box": [71.96, 39.43, 18.0, 31.99], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/a90271fcfe5e68c46df0.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p3 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p5", "main": "**Understanding how AI systems work matters because they have psychological, economic, social and environmental consequences, and possible benefits in medicine and engineering.** Knowing the arithmetic a system is built from does not by itself explain the complex behavior that comes out of it.", "sub": ["That gap is the reason scientists, policy makers and lawyers need AI literacy, so that they can make useful decisions about these systems."], "end": "So the aim is to understand the systems well enough to judge their uses and their harms.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 01 · p7", "main": "**Memorization and repetition handle familiar situations, and creative reasoning asks whether very different things share one structure.** Habits and specialized skills can make us treat similar things as identical and small differences as decisive. Creative reasoning instead asks questions and answers them, and looks for the hidden relationship.", "sub": ["Diotima's ladder puts thought, focus, imagination, critical reading and creative writing above memorized answers."], "end": "So the recurring question is what two different systems have in common, and that is the skill to build.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p7 · Lecture 03 · p8", "main": "**Artificial intelligence models psychological abilities, knowing, recognizing, attending, acting and learning, that animals including humans have.** The comparison is about how a skill can be organized, not about whether a machine has the same physical parts as an animal.", "sub": ["Taylor's cloze procedure is an example of measuring knowledge through performance, by how well someone can restore a missing part of a message."], "end": "So each AI mechanism is matched with the human ability it models.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p7"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 25", "q": "There are systems all around us. True or false?", "a": "True. A system is a set of elements with relationships between them that together serve a goal, and that describes speech, an octopus, a thermostat, a population, an economy and a neural network."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What are the elements, the relationships and the goal of the speech system?", "a": "The elements are the parts of the tongue, the lips, the velum, the larynx, the palate and the back wall of the throat. The relationships are the coordinated movements among those parts that change the shape of the vocal tract, which the brain organizes. The goal is to convey a thought."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is a list of the speech organs not a complete explanation of speaking?", "a": "A list of the parts leaves out the relationships between them, and the relationships are what produce the behavior. Speaking happens because the tongue, lips, velum and larynx move together in an organized way toward the goal of conveying a thought. So the explanation has to say how the parts interact, not just what they are."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What makes the octopus arm slap a dynamical system, and what does it have in common with speech?", "a": "The arm slap has elements, the eight arms, the ground and the fish, that interact through relationships coordinated by the nervous system, toward the goal of keeping the den. Speech has the same organization, with the tongue, lips, velum and larynx as elements, coordinated by the brain, toward the goal of conveying a thought. In both, the behavior comes from the organized interaction of the parts, not from any one part on its own."}]}, {"id": "m1-closure", "title": "Closure, the cloze task and how GPT learns", "lectures": "Lectures 02 and 03", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p9", "main": "**Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar pattern that is unfinished.** A broken circle is seen as a whole circle, because familiarity with the shape lets the mind close the gap. Three black disks with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, even though no triangle is drawn.", "sub": ["Cloze is pronounced like close and is named after closure. It takes the same idea from shapes to language. The reader uses the words that remain to fill in the word that is missing."], "end": "So closure means filling in what is missing from what is already known.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p8 · Lecture 02 · p10", "main": "**The cloze procedure deletes words from a message and measures how well people can restore them.** In \"Chickens cackle and ___ quack,\" the missing word is ducks. Each correct restoration counts as one cloze unit.", "sub": ["Filling the gap takes more than knowing one word. It takes the meanings of the words, the way chickens goes with cackle and ducks with quack, and the parallel shape of the sentence.", "Wilson Taylor introduced the procedure in 1953 as a way to measure how readable a text is."], "end": "So knowledge can be tested through the ability to complete a sequence, and that is how a language model shows what it knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p8"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p10", "main": "**GPT learns by doing the cloze task billions of times.** It takes a sentence, hides part of it, and makes the network guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of completions, the weights hold the knowledge of the language.", "sub": ["Completing a sequence is predicting what fits the context, and people do the same thing when they expect what comes next.", "Whether this counts as knowledge is a debate. One view is that the system's knowledge is not so different from ours, and a skeptical view, associated with Yann LeCun, says it is."], "end": "So closure is the principle, the cloze task is the method, and prediction is what the system learns to do.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 7", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, and how it is used in the GPT system.", "a": "Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar but unfinished pattern. A broken circle is still seen as a circle, and three black circles with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, because the mind fills in what is missing. GPT uses the same idea as its way of learning, which is called the cloze task. A sentence is taken, part of it is hidden, and the network has to guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of these completions the network ends up knowing the language."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 11", "q": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, illustrated by the figure of three black circles with wedges cut out, and how it is used in the GPT system as a principle for learning.", "a": "Humans are very good at filling in missing information using what we already know. In the figure no triangle is actually drawn, but we see one anyway because the brain completes the shape. GPT learns the same way, with the cloze task. It takes a sentence, pretends that part of the sentence is missing, and asks the network to complete what is missing. If the guess is wrong, the weights get adjusted. After billions of these completions the system ends up knowing the language.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q11.png", "alt": "Three black circles with wedges cut out that make a triangle appear"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A sentence has a word hidden, and a language model guesses the wrong word. What happens next, and why does this count as learning?", "a": "The wrong guess gets compared with the real missing word, and the difference is the mistake. The weights are then changed a little in the direction that would have made the right word more likely. This counts as learning because the weights hold the knowledge, so changing them changes what the network knows. After billions of these corrections the guesses become right."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a broken circle and the sentence Chickens cackle and ___ quack have in common?", "a": "In both, something is missing and the mind fills it in from what is already there. The circle is completed from familiarity with its shape, and the sentence is completed with ducks from the meanings of the other words and the parallel pattern of the sentence. Both are closure, which is completing a familiar but unfinished pattern."}]}, {"id": "m1-output", "title": "What a neural network outputs: a probability distribution and a decision", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 09 and 10", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10 · Lecture 10 · p3", "main": "**A neural network takes in a signal as a list of numbers and gives out a probability distribution over the possible identities.** A face image can be 48 million pixel numbers, and the question is which of 1000 people it is. A speech wave is a list of pressure numbers, and the question is which of 25 sounds it is.", "sub": ["For five vowels in the order read, rad, rude, Rud, rod, the output [0.2, 0.2, 0.4, 0.2, 0] gives 20 percent to read, 20 percent to rad, 40 percent to rude, 20 percent to Rud and 0 to rod.", "The decision is the category with the largest number, so here the decision is rude."], "end": "So the output is not one answer but a set of probabilities, and the decision is the biggest one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p10", "main": "**A probability distribution is a list where every number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1.** A list that adds up to 0, or to 1.5, is not a distribution, and neither is a list with a negative number in it.", "sub": ["The list only means something together with the agreed order of the categories. The same numbers in a different order would be a different decision.", "For example, [.4, 0, .5, .2] is not a good output for four categories, because the numbers add up to 1.1."], "end": "So to check an output, check that each number is between 0 and 1 and that the total is exactly 1.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p11 · Lecture 10 · p12", "main": "**The true answer is given to the network as a distribution with all of the probability on the correct category.** If the correct vowel is Rud, the fourth of five, the target is [0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. The network's own output might be [0.4, 0.1, 0.2, 0.2, 0.1], which puts the most probability on read.", "sub": ["Comparing the output with the target gives the error, which is also called the loss. Training changes the weights to make that error smaller.", "The input numbers can be positive or negative and are not probabilities. Only the output is a probability distribution."], "end": "So the target is the same kind of list as the output, with a 1 in the right place and 0 everywhere else.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p11 · Lecture 03 · p9", "main": "**For a language model the categories are the possible next words, so the output is a probability for each word.** Each input word is a vector (a list of numbers), for example 1,024 numbers per word. The model uses the whole sequence, chickens, cackle, and, ducks, to predict the next word.", "sub": ["The output is another vector, for example 10,000 numbers, one for each possible next word such as a, cat, quack and Zulu. The number for quack should be the biggest."], "end": "So a language model's decision is the next word with the highest probability.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 11", "q": "What is the output of an LLM or a neural network in general, and how does it express a decision?", "a": "The output is a probability distribution over the possible categories. That is a list with one number per category, where each number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1. The decision is the category with the highest probability. For an LLM the categories are the possible next words, so the output says how likely each word is to come next."}, {"source": "Review 12", "q": "Is this a good output of a neural network, if there are 4 categories, [.4, 0, .5, .2]? Why or why not?", "a": "No, it is not a good output. This is because the output has to be a probability distribution, so the numbers have to add up to 1. These numbers add up to 1.1. Each number is between 0 and 1, so that part is fine, but the total is wrong."}, {"source": "Review 20", "q": "The decision by a neural network is [.1, .1, .1, .2, .5], but the true category is the one in the second position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, as a probability distribution, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is written as [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]. This is because the correct category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and every other category gets 0. The 1 goes in the second position because that is where the true category is. The list still adds up to 1, so it is a probability distribution, and the network can compare its own output with it to find its mistake."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 1", "q": "Which of these four could be the probability distribution decision of a neural network? (a) [0, 0, 0, 0], (b) [0, .4, 0, .6], (c) [-.5, 0, .5, 0], (d) [.3, .3, .4, .5].", "a": "Only (b) is possible. This is because the numbers in a probability distribution have to be between 0 and 1 and have to add up to 1. List (a) adds up to 0. List (c) has a negative number. List (d) adds up to 1.5. List (b) has numbers between 0 and 1 that add up to 1."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 23", "q": "The decision made by a neural network is [.4, .1, .1, .3, .1], but the true category is the one in the last position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, in a probability distribution format, what the true answer is?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 0, 0, 1]. This is because the true category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and the other four categories get 0. The 1 goes in the last position because that is where the true category is."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Is [.2, .3, .5] a good output of a neural network with three categories? Is [.5, .5, .5]?", "a": "The first one is good, because each number is between 0 and 1 and they add up to 1. The second one is not good, because the numbers add up to 1.5. A probability distribution has to add up to exactly 1."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A network's output is [.1, .6, .2, .1] and the true category is the third one. Write the true answer as a probability distribution. Is the network's decision right?", "a": "The true answer is [0, 0, 1, 0], with all the probability on the third category. The network's decision is wrong, because its biggest number, .6, is in the second position, so it decided on the second category. The gap between its output and the target is the error that training uses to fix the weights."}]}, {"id": "m1-weights", "title": "Knowledge in the weights: inner products and forward propagation", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 09 and 10", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p12 · Lecture 02 · p13", "main": "**A network's knowledge is stored in its weights, which are the numbers that determine how the inputs get turned into output probabilities.** The input words are vectors called x's, and the weights are called w's. The computation that combines them uses only adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing, but a very large interconnected system of these operations can do something complex.", "sub": ["Some large AI systems have trillions of weights. The brain stores the same kind of knowledge in its synapses (the connections between neurons), about 150 trillion of them.", "Changing the weights changes which next words get high or low probability for the same input."], "end": "So learning means changing the weights, because that is where the knowledge is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p6", "main": "**Each neuron multiplies every incoming activation by its own weight and adds the products, and that sum is the inner product.** An **activation** is the number coming in from a neuron in the layer before. A **weight** is the number on one connection, and it can be positive or negative.", "sub": ["With inputs of minus 1, 1 and 3 and weights of minus 2, 3 and 1, the products are 2, 3 and 3, and the sum is 8.", "A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight changes it. For example, an input of 3 with a weight of 2 gives 6, and the same input with a weight of minus 2 gives minus 6.", "The inner product is also called the dot product or the scalar product. It is the core calculation of all neural networks."], "end": "So a neuron with 5 inputs has 5 weights, and its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w5 times x5.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p15 · Lecture 09 · p13", "main": "**The inner product is a way of comparing two lists of numbers, so the weights act as templates that the input is matched against.** With an input of [minus 1, 3, minus 2], the template [minus 2, 0, 3] gives 2 plus 0 minus 6, which is minus 4. The template [minus 2, 2, minus 1] gives 2 plus 6 plus 2, which is 10.", "sub": ["A big positive inner product means a strong match, and a value near zero means little match, so the second template matches better.", "In the templatic theory of face perception, the brain compares an incoming face with stored templates, and the most similar template decides who it is. The weights play the part of the templates."], "end": "So before learning the templates are random and the matches are poor, and training makes the templates useful.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p9 · Lecture 10 · p10", "main": "**Forward propagation is the calculation that runs from the input layer to the output layer using the current weights.** A network with 5 inputs, hidden layers of 3, 4 and 3 neurons, and 7 outputs has every neuron connected to every neuron in the next layer, which is 60 connections, each with its own weight.", "sub": ["Each neuron in the first hidden layer takes the inner product of the 5 inputs with its 5 weights. Those 3 activations become the inputs to the next layer, which does the same, and so on through the layers.", "With inputs [2, 1, 3, 4, 2] and weights [2, minus 1, 1, 3, minus 2], the middle hidden neuron gets 4 minus 1 plus 3 plus 12 minus 4, which is 14. With inputs [minus 2, 1, 3, minus 4, 2] and weights [3, minus 2, 1, 2, 5] the sum is minus 6 minus 2 plus 3 minus 8 plus 10, which is minus 3. Hidden activations can be negative.", "The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution, for example [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08], and the fourth category, at 0.30, is the decision."], "end": "So the output depends on the inputs and on every weight along the way, and the signs matter at every step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p14 · Lecture 09 · p16 · Lecture 10 · p5", "main": "**Perception works in stages, and a network copies that by turning each layer's output into the next layer's input.** In the visual system, areas V1, V2, V3 and V4 and the areas after them each transform the information and pass it on, with connections running forward and back.", "sub": ["**Inference** is using the current weights to compute an output for an input. **Learning** is changing the weights. A training step has to run inference first, to get the output it will compare with the correct answer, so inference is part of learning.", "Like Euler's method, a network builds a complex result out of many repeated simple calculations."], "end": "So a hierarchy of template comparisons, layer after layer, is what a neural network is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p14"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p12", "main": "**A speech recognizer that receives a [z] sound wave has to compare it with what it has learned, and two ideas from animal perception organize that comparison.** Template matching compares the input with stored patterns. Hierarchical processing turns the input into a new representation and then transforms that again, stage after stage.", "sub": ["A neural network combines both, by comparing numerical patterns at several levels one after another."], "end": "So recognition is matching plus stages.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p7 · Lecture 10 · p8", "main": "**Neural network models translate ideas about brain activity into arithmetic.** The line runs from McCulloch and Pitts in 1943, through Rosenblatt's perceptrons in 1962, to the Parallel Distributed Processing books of 1986. Each one tries to copy some part of what neurons do with formal operations.", "sub": ["One view says AI and human thinking are fundamentally different, because brain chemistry is not arithmetic. The other view treats AI as a long attempt to use arithmetic to copy parts of brain activity. This comparison is outside the exam."], "end": "So the arithmetic is a model of the brain, not a claim that the two are made of the same stuff.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p7"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 8", "q": "How is knowledge represented in neural networks?", "a": "Knowledge is represented in the weights, which are the numbers on the connections between neurons. The weights determine how the input numbers get turned into the output probabilities. Changing the weights changes what the network knows, so learning means changing the weights. In the brain the same kind of knowledge sits in the synapses (the connections between neurons), and a large AI system can have trillions of weights."}, {"source": "Review 19", "q": "What is the math of forward propagation, from the signal input to a neural network all the way to a decision at the end? Describe it.", "a": "The input signal is a vector, which is a list of numbers, such as pressure values from a sound wave. Each neuron in the first layer multiplies each input number by its own weight and adds the products up. That sum is the inner product, and it is the neuron's activation. The activations of that layer become the inputs to the next layer, where every neuron does the same multiply-and-add with its own weights. This repeats layer by layer until the last layer. The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution over the categories, so the numbers are between 0 and 1 and add up to 1. The decision is the category with the largest probability."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 21", "q": "Imagine a neural network with many neurons in many layers. One neuron, call it y, in layer 4 is connected to 5 neurons from the previous layer. Every neuron is connected to all the neurons in the previous layer. How many weights does y have?", "a": "Neuron y has 5 weights. This is because there is one weight on each connection coming into a neuron, and y has 5 connections coming in, one from each of the 5 neurons in the previous layer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 22", "q": "Continuing from the previous question, y's weights are w1, w2, w3, w4 and w5, and the activations of the 5 neurons feeding it are x1, x2, x3, x4 and x5. How do we write the activation of neuron y?", "a": "The activation of y is w1 times x1, plus w2 times x2, plus w3 times x3, plus w4 times x4, plus w5 times x5. This is because each incoming activation is multiplied by its own weight, and then all five products are added up. That sum is the inner product."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron in layer 3 is connected to 7 neurons in layer 2. How many weights does it have, and how is its activation written?", "a": "It has 7 weights, one on each incoming connection. Its activation is w1 times x1 plus w2 times x2 and so on up to w7 times x7, where the x's are the 7 activations coming in and the w's are the 7 weights. Each input is multiplied by its own weight and the products are added."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A neuron has inputs 2, minus 1 and 3 with weights 1, 4 and minus 2. What is its activation, and what is this calculation called?", "a": "Its activation is minus 8. This is because the products are 2 times 1, which is 2, then minus 1 times 4, which is minus 4, then 3 times minus 2, which is minus 6, and 2 minus 4 minus 6 is minus 8. The calculation is the inner product, which multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between inference and learning in a neural network?", "a": "Inference uses the weights the network has now to compute an output for an input. Learning changes the weights. The two are linked, because a learning step first runs inference to get the current output, then compares it with the correct answer, then changes the weights to shrink the difference. So inference is part of every learning step, but only the weight change is learning."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can an input of 3 end up pushing a neuron's activation down?", "a": "It pushes the activation down when its weight is negative. This is because the input is multiplied by its weight, so 3 times a negative weight such as minus 2 gives minus 6, which lowers the sum. The sign of the weight decides whether an input adds to the activation or subtracts from it."}]}, {"id": "m1-sgd", "title": "Stochastic gradient descent: guess, mistake, correct", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03 and 10", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p16 · Lecture 03 · p12", "main": "**Stochastic gradient descent learns a quantity, such as the average of a list, by starting with a guess and correcting it from one observation at a time.** Adding up three numbers and dividing by three gives their average directly, but speech, language and scenes cannot be learned by adding a few numbers. So a different way of learning the average is used as a model of learning in general.", "sub": ["Start with a guess. Compare it with one value from the list. Move the guess part of the way toward that value. Repeat with another value.", "Each correction happens because a new observation shows a discrepancy (a gap) between the guess and the data."], "end": "So learning here means being corrected by observations, not being told the answer.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p17 · Lecture 03 · p13 · Lecture 03 · p14 · Lecture 03 · p15", "main": "**Each step subtracts a fraction of the mistake from the current estimate, and the fraction is the learning rate.** The data are [78, 67, 98, 85, 76, 68, 98, 72]. The estimate starts at 130, which is an arbitrary guess. A random observation, 98, is drawn. The mistake, or **loss**, is the estimate minus the observation, 130 minus 98, which is 32.", "sub": ["With a learning rate of 10 percent, the estimate moves down by a tenth of 32, so it goes from 130 to 126.8. With a learning rate of 25 percent it moves down by a quarter of 32, to 122. Either way it moves toward 98 without jumping all the way to it.", "**Stochastic** means the observation is picked at random. **Gradient** means the direction that reduces the mistake. **Descent** means the mistake goes down, not that the estimate always goes down."], "end": "So a partial correction lets each observation count without throwing away what the estimate already knows.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/017.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p17"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p23 · Lecture 03 · p27", "main": "**The first minus in the update makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the estimate.** When the estimate is 94.672 and the observation is 98, the difference is minus 3.328. Subtracting a negative number raises the estimate, so it goes up to 95.504.", "sub": ["When the estimate is above the observation, the difference is positive, so subtracting it lowers the estimate. When the estimate is below, the difference is negative, so subtracting it raises the estimate.", "That is why the path of the estimate jitters. It mostly falls from 130 toward 80, but it rises whenever a high observation such as 98 is drawn."], "end": "So one step can move away from the true average, and learning is judged by the whole run, not by one step.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p28 · Lecture 03 · p29", "main": "**The loss goes down whenever the gap between the estimate and the target shrinks, whether the estimate itself is going up or going down.** The loss is half of the squared gap, so it is never negative and a big gap counts much more than a small one.", "sub": ["Starting at 140 with a target of 80, the estimate falls and the loss falls. Starting at minus 100, the estimate rises and the loss still falls, because the gap is shrinking.", "Descent describes the loss, not the estimate."], "end": "So the estimate can rise while the learning is still going downhill.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/028.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p28"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p10 · Lecture 03 · p11", "main": "**Fake it till you make it means the network starts with random weights, so it knows nothing, and gets its knowledge by being corrected.** For chickens cackle and ducks, random weights can give the highest probability to the wrong word. The target puts all the probability on quack, and the gap between them tells training which way to change the weights.", "sub": ["The two principles are to start with a guess and to improve the guess by learning from mistakes.", "Random guessing on its own creates no knowledge. The repeated correction is what turns the guess into knowledge."], "end": "So the system fakes it at the start, and after enough corrections it makes it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 10 · p12 · Lecture 10 · p13", "main": "**Training a network is the same loop run with a labelled input. Compute the output, compare it with the target, and change the weights to shrink the error.** For an input whose correct category is the sixth of seven, the target is [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0]. An output of [0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.15, 0.12, 0.08] puts only 0.12 on the right category, and the gap is the error.", "sub": ["**Backpropagation** carries the output error back through the layers so that every weight can be adjusted with SGD. Repeating this over billions of labelled pairs builds the network's knowledge.", "A mistake in the data gets learned too. In a list of grades with a typo of 400, the estimate is pulled toward 400 and the learned average comes out near 100 instead of near 80. Correct arithmetic does not fix a wrong observation."], "end": "So the loop is guess, measure the mistake, correct a fraction, and repeat, and it learns whatever the data contains, errors included.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture10/012.webp", "caption": "Lecture 10 · p12"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p15", "main": "**Learning from a mistake needs a way to compare the current response with an observation.** The gap between them says which way to change. Averaging is the simple case where that correction can be followed step by step.", "end": "So feedback from data, not theory alone, is what moves the estimate.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p15"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 03 · p16 · Lecture 03 · p17 · Lecture 03 · p18 · Lecture 03 · p20 · Lecture 03 · p21 · Lecture 03 · p22 · Lecture 03 · p24 · Lecture 03 · p25 · Lecture 03 · p26", "main": "**Step after step, the estimate falls whenever the drawn observation is below it, and the size of the step is a quarter of the gap.** From 122 with an observation of 85 the gap is 37, so the estimate drops to 112.75. From there an observation of 76 drops it to about 103.6, then 68 drops it to about 94.7. Later, 72 drops it to about 89.6, 78 to about 86.7 and 67 to about 81.8.", "sub": ["When the observation is close to the estimate, as 85 is to 85.8, the gap is tiny and the path is almost level.", "After twelve corrections the estimate ends near 79.4, just below the true average of 80.25, because the last observation drawn, 68, was below both."], "end": "So each step is the same rule, and the path is the sum of many small corrections in both directions.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture03/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 03 · p16"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 18", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula? Consider the case where the data point is above the current x and the case where it is below.", "a": "The first minus makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the current estimate. If the current estimate is above the data point, the difference in the parentheses is positive, so subtracting it makes the estimate go down, toward the data point. If the current estimate is below the data point, the difference is negative, so subtracting a negative number makes the estimate go up, again toward the data point. For example, with an estimate of 130 and a data point of 98, the difference is 32, so the estimate goes down. With an estimate of 94 and a data point of 98, the difference is minus 4, so the estimate goes up."}, {"source": "Review 10", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I describe it this way.", "a": "The system starts with completely random weights, so at first it does not know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, gets feedback on how wrong the guess was, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this many times it makes it, which means it ends up with the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 7", "q": "Describe how the stochastic gradient descent algorithm works in your own words.", "a": "We want to estimate a quantity from some existing data, like the average of a list of numbers. We start with a random estimate. Then we pick a random data point and compute the loss, which is the difference between our guess and that data point. We subtract a small fraction of the loss from the guess, and that fraction is the learning rate. That gives a new, slightly better guess. We repeat this with new random data points over and over, and the guess gets closer and closer to the real answer."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 15", "q": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I put it this way.", "a": "The system starts out with completely random weights, so it does not actually know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, receives feedback, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this a lot of times, it makes it, which means it has the right knowledge."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 20", "q": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula?", "a": "The minus makes the estimate move toward the data point, whether the data point is above or below the estimate. If the estimate is above the data point, the loss is positive, so subtracting it pushes the estimate down. If the estimate is below the data point, the loss is negative, so subtracting it pushes the estimate up."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "The current estimate is 60 and the next data point is 90, with a learning rate of 10 percent. Which way does the estimate move, and by how much?", "a": "The estimate moves up, to 63. This is because the difference, 60 minus 90, is minus 30. Subtracting 10 percent of minus 30, which is minus 3, raises the estimate by 3. The minus in the formula makes the estimate move toward the data point, which is above it."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Can the SGD estimate move away from the true average on a single step? Why?", "a": "Yes. This is because each step moves the estimate toward the one data point that was drawn, not toward the average. If the estimate is 94 and the data point drawn is 98, the estimate goes up even though the average is 80. Over many steps the moves average out and the estimate settles near 80."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A list of exam grades has one typo, 400, in it. What happens when SGD learns the average from this list?", "a": "The learned average comes out too high, near 100 instead of near 80. This is because the step that draws 400 pulls the estimate strongly toward 400, and SGD has no way to know that the observation is wrong. The arithmetic is correct, but it learns the mistake in the data along with everything else."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the words stochastic, gradient and descent each mean in the name of the algorithm?", "a": "Stochastic means the data point is picked at random each step. Gradient means the direction of change that reduces the mistake. Descent means the mistake goes down over the run. Descent does not mean the estimate itself always goes down, because the estimate rises whenever it is below the data point."}]}, {"id": "m1-rate", "title": "Learning rate and stiffness: the speed and accuracy tradeoff", "lectures": "Lectures 02, 03, 04 and 07", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 02 · p18 · Lecture 03 · p30 · Lecture 07 · p15", "main": "**A bigger learning rate makes the estimate reach the right region faster but keeps it jumping around once it is there.** Three runs start at 130, at 80 and at 10, and all head toward the average near 80. At 1 percent the runs are smooth but slow, and after 300 steps the run from 10 is still below 80. At 10 percent they arrive fairly fast and then wobble a little.", "sub": ["At 50 percent the runs arrive quickly but make big jumps around 80. At 80 percent each observation throws the estimate a long way, so it keeps bouncing over a wide range.", "A big rate gives each observation a lot of influence, so an estimate near the average is easily pulled away by one data point. A small rate gives little jitter but needs many observations to travel far from a bad start."], "end": "So the run that starts near the answer does fine with a small rate, and a run that starts far away needs a bigger rate to get there in time.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p18"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 02 · p19 · Lecture 03 · p31", "main": "**This is the speed and accuracy tradeoff.** Raising the learning rate raises the speed and lowers the accuracy, because the estimate reacts more to each observation and keeps fluctuating. Lowering the learning rate lowers the speed and raises the accuracy, because each correction is small and the fluctuations near the average are small.", "sub": ["Optimization research looks for methods that get both speed and accuracy. The tradeoff describes the problem those methods try to solve."], "end": "So no single learning rate is both the fastest and the most accurate, and the choice is a compromise.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture02/019.webp", "caption": "Lecture 02 · p19"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18 · Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**Stiffness does for a spring what the learning rate does for SGD, because both set how strongly the system corrects a discrepancy.** A viscous spring with no mass follows the rule that the change in position is a negative number times the position, so a positive position falls toward zero and a negative one rises toward zero. A bigger number in that rule, which is more stiffness, means a faster return.", "sub": ["Hot coffee cools toward room temperature and iced coffee warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is big and slows as the gap shrinks, so different starts reach the same equilibrium from opposite sides.", "The SGD estimate does the same, except its path is noisy because each observation is random, while the coffee curve is smooth."], "end": "So a high learning rate and a high stiffness both mean a fast correction, and a low learning rate and a low stiffness both mean a slow one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 2", "q": "What is the role of the learning rate in stochastic gradient descent and the role of stiffness in a viscous springy system? What happens when the learning rate is high versus low, and when stiffness is high versus low?", "a": "The learning rate determines how big a fraction of each mistake gets corrected, and stiffness determines how strongly the spring pulls back toward equilibrium (its resting position). With a high learning rate, each correction is large, so the estimate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, so it is less accurate. With a low learning rate, each correction is small, so the estimate moves slowly but settles steadily, so it is more accurate but slow. With high stiffness, the spring pulls back hard, so it returns to equilibrium fast. With low stiffness, it pulls back weakly, so it returns slowly. In both systems a bigger setting means a faster response."}, {"source": "Review 9", "q": "What is the basic tradeoff that we see in both the shortest path problem and the stochastic gradient descent algorithm?", "a": "The tradeoff is speed against accuracy. In the shortest path problem, the greedy algorithm is fast but can pick the wrong path, and the exhaustive algorithm always finds the right path but is slow. Dynamic programming balances the two. In stochastic gradient descent, a high learning rate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, and a low learning rate settles accurately but slowly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 10", "q": "Imagine a stochastic gradient descent algorithm with a very low learning rate, and a springy system with no mass and a low stiffness. (a) Describe how the SGD estimate and the spring's position evolve with time. (b) How would the evolution in time differ if the learning rate and the stiffness increased? For SGD, ignore accuracy and oscillation.", "a": "Part (a). Both move toward their goal slowly. With a low learning rate, the SGD estimate only takes a small correction toward the average each time, so it takes many steps to get there. With low stiffness, the spring only pulls weakly, so it drifts back to its equilibrium position slowly.\n\nPart (b). Both would get there faster. A higher learning rate makes bigger corrections toward the average, so the estimate arrives in fewer steps. A stiffer spring pulls harder, so it snaps back to equilibrium more quickly."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two SGD runs use the same data. One has a learning rate of 1 percent and the other 80 percent. Describe how each estimate moves over time.", "a": "The 1 percent run moves slowly and smoothly toward the average, and if it starts far away it may not have arrived after hundreds of steps. The 80 percent run reaches the region of the average very fast, but then each observation throws it a long way, so it keeps bouncing around instead of settling. The small rate is accurate but slow, and the big rate is fast but inaccurate."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A spring with no mass is released from a stretched position. Compare a stiff spring with a loose one.", "a": "Both return to equilibrium and stop there without crossing it. The stiff spring returns fast, because it pulls back hard, so the position drops toward zero quickly. The loose spring returns slowly, because it pulls back weakly. Neither one overshoots, because there is no mass to carry the motion past equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a cooling cup of coffee and an SGD estimate have in common?", "a": "Both move toward a goal by correcting a discrepancy, and the correction is big when the gap is big and small when the gap is small. The coffee's temperature moves toward room temperature, and the estimate moves toward the data. The difference is that the coffee curve is smooth, while the estimate's path jitters because each observation is random."}]}, {"id": "m1-paths", "title": "Shortest paths: greedy, exhaustive and dynamic programming", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p3", "main": "**A shortest path problem asks for the cheapest way from the start state to the goal state through a series of stages.** The circles are states, the arrows are the allowed moves, and the number on an arrow is the cost of that move. From A you can go to B for 12 or to C for 7, and the stages continue through D, E and F, then G, H and I, then J.", "sub": ["A route's total is the sum of every arrow it follows. A cheap first arrow does not make a route cheap, because later arrows can undo its advantage."], "end": "So the quantity that matters is the total cost of the whole route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p4", "main": "**The greedy algorithm takes the cheapest arrow out of whatever state it is in, and it can lose.** From A it takes C, because 7 is less than 12. From C it takes E, because 10 is the smallest. From E it takes H, because 7 is the smallest. Then H to J costs 9, and the route totals 33.", "sub": ["Greedy is fast, because it only looks at the next arrow. It is inaccurate, because it ignores the costs that are still ahead, and an expensive first step can lead to a cheaper whole route."], "end": "So greedy is fast but can pick the wrong route.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p6", "main": "**The exhaustive algorithm works out the total of every complete route and picks the cheapest, so it is always right and very slow.** For this problem it finds the true minimum, because it compares every route.", "sub": ["Every added state or stage multiplies the number of complete routes, so the work grows very fast. The routes overlap, so the same additions get done over and over."], "end": "So exhaustive search is accurate but inefficient.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p7 · Lecture 04 · p8", "main": "**Dynamic programming breaks the big problem into smaller problems that it contains, solves the smallest first, and reuses their answers.** Call the best cost of finishing from B the value of B, and the same for C. If those two values are known, the decision at A is the smaller of 12 plus the value of B and 7 plus the value of C.", "sub": ["A **value** is the cost of the whole remaining problem from a state, not the cost of one arrow. The values of B and C are unknown at first, so each one becomes a smaller problem, the cheapest way to finish from that state.", "Breaking problems into smaller problems is Bellman's idea, and it keeps going until the remaining problem is trivial."], "end": "So a hard problem is solved by solving the easy problems inside it and building back up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/007.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p7"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p9 · Lecture 04 · p10 · Lecture 04 · p13 · Lecture 04 · p16", "main": "**The values are worked out backward from the goal, and then the route is followed forward from the start.** The last states have values that are just their final arrow, so G is 5, H is 9 and I is 8. For D, compare 8 plus 5, 7 plus 9 and 10 plus 8, and the smallest, 13, is the value of D. The same rule gives E the value 14 and F the value 15.", "sub": ["Then B is 18 through D and C is 24 through E. At A the choice is 12 plus 18 through B, which is 30, or 7 plus 24 through C, which is 31. So the best route is A to B to D to G to J, costing 30, even though its first arrow is the expensive one.", "The rule at every state is the same. For each possible next state, add the cost of getting there to that state's value, and keep the smallest sum. Remember which next state gave it, so the route can be read off forward afterward."], "end": "So the calculation runs backward from the goal, and the route is lived forward from the start.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p9"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p11 · Lecture 04 · p12", "main": "**Dynamic programming is more efficient than exhaustive search because the routes overlap, and it solves each overlapping part only once.** Exhaustive search redoes the same additions for every route through a state. Dynamic programming finds the best way to finish from each state once and reuses it for every route that passes through.", "sub": ["A costly arrow far ahead, like the 9 from H to J, is carried backward into the values, so the choice at A already knows about it.", "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, exhaustive is accurate but slow, and dynamic programming is the balance."], "end": "So the value of a state summarizes the whole future, and that is what lets a local choice be a good one.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p11"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 5", "q": "Why is the exhaustive algorithm so inefficient for solving the shortest path problem?", "a": "The exhaustive algorithm is inefficient because it works out the total cost of every complete path separately. The paths overlap, so the same additions get done over and over. Every time a stage is added, the number of complete paths multiplies, so the work grows very fast. It does find the true shortest path, but for a big problem it takes far too long."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 4", "q": "Describe how dynamic programming works in terms of the size of problems, and problems containing other problems.", "a": "You break the big problem into smaller subproblems, and keep breaking those down until you reach small problems that are trivial to solve. You solve those smallest problems first. Then you use their answers to solve the larger problems that contain them, and keep going up until you reach the hardest problem, which is the original one."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 5", "q": "Describe why dynamic programming is more efficient than the exhaustive algorithm.", "a": "The paths in a shortest path problem overlap. The exhaustive algorithm solves every path separately, so it redoes the same additions over and over. Dynamic programming solves only the non-overlapping parts. It finds the best solution from each state only once and reuses it for every path through that state, so it never repeats work."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 12", "q": "In the shortest path problem shown, what is the greedy forward solution? Write out the numbers with plus signs.", "a": "The greedy forward solution is 3 plus 3 plus 4 plus 2, which is 12, along the path A to B to E to F to H. This is because greedy takes the cheapest arrow out of each state as it goes. From A the cheapest arrow is 3 to B. From B it is 3 to E. From E it is 4 to F. From F the only arrow is 2 to H. The greedy total is 12, and it is not the best path. The cheapest complete path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 13", "q": "In the same shortest path problem, we call the best cost from any state x to state H V x H. Imagine we have already solved for V D H and V E H. How can I calculate V C H?", "a": "V C H is the smaller of two sums, 2 plus V D H and 1 plus V E H. This is because from C you can only go to D, which costs 2, or to E, which costs 1. Each sum adds the cost of that first step to the best cost of finishing from the state you land in. Taking the minimum is the important part, because the best cost from C is the cheaper of the two complete ways of finishing.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 14", "q": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "a": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In a shortest path problem, what is the difference between the cost of an arrow and the value of a state?", "a": "The cost of an arrow is the price of one move. The value of a state is the cheapest total cost of finishing from that state all the way to the goal, which adds up several arrows. Dynamic programming uses values, because a value already includes every cost that is still ahead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does dynamic programming work backward from the goal?", "a": "It works backward because a state's value can only be computed once the values of the states after it are known. The last states are easy, because their value is just their final arrow. Each earlier state then adds one arrow to a known value and keeps the smallest sum. Once every value is known, the route is read off forward from the start."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, the greedy path from A to H cost 12. What is the cheapest path, and why does greedy miss it?", "a": "The cheapest path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11. Greedy misses it because the first arrow to C costs 5, which is more than the 3 to B, so greedy never goes to C. The cheap arrows after C, 2 and 1, are what make the whole route cheaper, and greedy cannot see them from A.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the Exam 1 graph, we know the values V F H and V G H. How do we calculate V D H?", "a": "V D H is the smaller of 3 plus V F H and 1 plus V G H. This is because from D you can go to F for 3 or to G for 1, and each sum adds that first step to the best cost of finishing from where you land. The minimum of the two is the best cost from D.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow"}]}, {"id": "m1-least-time", "title": "Least distance is not least time", "lectures": "Lecture 04", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 04 · p14", "main": "**The fastest route is not always the shortest route, because the cost of a stretch depends on the conditions on it.** On uniform sand, where speed is the same everywhere, the straight line is both shortest and fastest. A rescuer who runs faster on sand than they swim in water should run farther along the sand before entering the water, so the fastest route bends at the shore.", "sub": ["The extra distance on the fast sand saves more time than it costs, because it shortens the slow swim.", "This is the same idea as paying the expensive 12 to B in the shortest path problem, because the cheaper continuation more than pays it back."], "end": "So the thing to minimize is the total time or total cost of the whole route, not the length of its first stretch.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p14"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p15", "main": "**Light takes the least-time route too, and that is why it bends at the boundary between air and water.** Light moves faster in air than in water, so its path changes direction at the surface, the way the rescuer's path changes direction at the shore. A pencil in a glass of water looks bent because the light reaching the eye followed a bent path.", "sub": ["The pencil itself is straight. The bend is in the path of the light."], "end": "So a locally shorter stretch is not always best when the rest of the route runs under different conditions.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why can the fastest route be longer than the shortest route?", "a": "Because the route crosses regions where the speed is different. Spending extra distance in the fast region shortens the distance in the slow region, and the time saved there is bigger than the time added. So the route with the least total time bends toward the fast region and is longer than the straight line."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do a lifeguard running to a swimmer, a ray of light entering water, and the shortest path problem have in common?", "a": "In all three, the best choice for the whole route is not the cheapest first step. The lifeguard runs farther on sand because swimming is slow. Light bends at the water because it travels slower in water. In the path problem the expensive first arrow leads to the cheaper total. What is minimized is the total time or total cost over the whole route."}]}, {"id": "m1-oscillator", "title": "Position and velocity: why a mass on a spring oscillates", "lectures": "Lectures 05–06, 07 and 08", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p3 · Lecture 07 · p2", "main": "**A harmonic oscillator has two quantities that change each other, position x and velocity v.** Position is where the mass is relative to its resting position. Velocity is how fast the position is changing. A dot over a quantity means its rate of change, so x dot is how position changes and v dot is how velocity changes.", "sub": ["The first rule is that x dot equals v. Velocity determines how position changes, and the sign stays the same, so positive velocity makes position go up.", "The second rule is that v dot equals minus x. Position determines how velocity changes, with the sign reversed, so positive position makes velocity go down and negative position makes velocity go up."], "end": "So position and velocity keep changing each other through a feedback loop.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p4 · Lecture 05–06 · p5", "main": "**The rules say which way a quantity is changing, not whether the quantity is positive right now.** When v is plus 5, position rises. When v is minus 5, position falls. When x is plus 5, velocity goes down. When x is minus 5, velocity goes up.", "sub": ["Velocity going up can mean going from a big negative number toward zero, which is slowing down, not speeding up in the same direction.", "Keeping the quantity and its rate of change separate is what makes the back and forth motion understandable."], "end": "So read each rule as a direction of change.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p6 · Lecture 05–06 · p7 · Lecture 07 · p3", "main": "**Following the two rules around the loop gives repeated motion, not a single return to the middle.** Start with a positive position and zero velocity. Positive x makes velocity become negative. Negative velocity makes x fall. As x nears zero the push on velocity weakens, but the velocity is still negative, so it carries x through zero to the negative side.", "sub": ["Now negative x makes velocity rise. Velocity passes through zero and becomes positive, so x turns around and rises back through zero. Then positive x starts pushing velocity down again, and the cycle repeats.", "Position and velocity are coordinated but do not peak together. Position is at a turning point when velocity is zero, and velocity is biggest when position passes through zero.", "The two arrows, from x to v and from v to x, close the loop, so one change leads to another and back again. That is what oscillation is."], "end": "So two simple rules, each the cause of the other, produce vibration.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p4 · Lecture 07 · p7", "main": "**A spring and a mass make vibration because the spring wants to return to equilibrium and the moving mass wants to keep moving.** The spring's goal is to get back to its resting position, and **stiffness** is how strongly it pulls back. A mass in motion stays in motion unless something changes it, which is **inertia**. The spring can pull the mass back to the middle, but the mass arrives moving, so it goes through, and the spring has to slow it and turn it around from the other side.", "sub": ["The same back and forth shows up in a car after a bump, in music, in boom and bust, in the SGD estimate swinging around the average, in the air in the vocal tract and in the vocal folds."], "end": "So the goal of the spring plus the inertia of the mass is the whole explanation of oscillation.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/004.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p4"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p8 · Lecture 08 · p3", "main": "**A spring on its own returns to equilibrium and stops, and a spring with a mass overshoots and keeps going.** Pull a spring alone to plus 1 and let go, and it comes back toward 0 and flattens out without crossing. Push it to minus 1 and it rises toward 0 without crossing. Attach a mass, release it from plus 1, and it moves to 0 and keeps going, because it is already moving. The spring is then compressed, pulls back, and turns the mass around, and the mass carries it through the next crossing too.", "sub": ["The trace of the mass on the spring crosses zero again and again, with each swing a little smaller when there is light damping.", "The resting position is the same in both cases. What differs is whether the motion stops there or passes through."], "end": "So the mass is the difference between settling and oscillating.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 16", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If it starts with x negative and v equal to 0, will it oscillate? Say in a couple of sentences what will happen.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium (x equal to 0). The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it goes past 0 and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. So the position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Review 15", "q": "Some people say that everything is a harmonic oscillator. Give 5 examples of oscillatory systems we discussed in class or in the Meadows book.", "a": "A mass on a spring swinging back and forth. The air in the vocal tract vibrating during speech, with its particles moving closer together and farther apart. The vocal folds opening and closing hundreds of times a second. The estimate in stochastic gradient descent swinging around the average when the learning rate is high. The economy going through boom and bust around its growth trend. A car bouncing after it goes over a bump. In the Meadows book, a room temperature swinging above and below the setting when the thermostat is far from the heater, and a car dealership's inventory swinging up and down because orders arrive after a delay. Any five of these work."}, {"source": "Review 4", "q": "Two temperature control systems are set to 70. In the first, the thermostat is right next to the heating and cooling unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, the temperature drops to 70, and the thermostat detects that and turns the unit off. Below 70, the heat turns on until 70 and then stops. In the second, the thermostat is far from the unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, but the thermostat does not detect 70, so the temperature keeps dropping to 60. Then the heat turns on, the temperature rises past 70 to 80 before the thermostat notices, and so on. Which system is like a spring by itself, and which is like a mass-spring system?", "a": "The first system, with the thermostat next to the unit, is like a spring by itself. This is because it moves toward its goal of 70 and stops there, the way a spring alone returns to equilibrium and stays. The second system, with the far thermostat, is like a mass-spring system. This is because it keeps going past 70, to 60 and then to 80, and swings back and forth around the goal. A moving mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so a mass-spring system overshoots and oscillates in the same way. The delay in detecting the temperature plays the part of the mass."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 17", "q": "What is the goal of a spring, if it is extended or squeezed? What is the primary physical property of a mass that is already moving?", "a": "The goal of a spring is to go back to equilibrium, its resting position. The primary property of a mass that is already moving is that it keeps on moving. Together these two facts are why a mass on a spring oscillates, because the spring keeps pulling the mass back and the mass keeps going past the middle."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 18", "q": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If x starts as negative and v is 0, will the system oscillate? How will the position of the mass evolve with time?", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts out negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium at x equal to 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A mass on a spring starts with x positive and v equal to 0. Will it oscillate? Describe what happens.", "a": "Yes, it oscillates. The position starts positive, so the spring is stretched and pulls the mass back toward equilibrium at 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots to the negative side and compresses the spring. The spring then pushes it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth as a cosine-shaped wave, starting from its high point."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "In the oscillator rules, x dot equals v and v dot equals minus x. If x is plus 5 right now, is the mass moving up or down?", "a": "You cannot tell from x alone. The rules only say that a positive x makes the velocity go down. Whether the mass is moving up or down right now depends on the current velocity. If v is still positive the mass is moving up while slowing, and if v is already negative it is moving down."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a mass on a spring keep passing through the middle instead of stopping there?", "a": "Because at the middle the spring is at its resting position and pulls with no force, but the mass is moving as fast as it ever does. A moving mass keeps moving unless something changes its motion, so it goes through. Only after it is past the middle does the spring start pulling it back."}]}, {"id": "m1-balancing", "title": "A spring alone, a cooling cup and balancing feedback", "lectures": "Lectures 04, 05–06, 07 and 09", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p6", "main": "**A viscous spring with no mass obeys one rule, the change in x is minus one third of x, so it returns to equilibrium and stops.** If x is positive, its change is negative, so x falls. If x is negative, its change is positive, so x rises. Both lead to the same resting position at 0.", "sub": ["Starting from 1, the first return is big, because one third of 1 is a big step. As x gets smaller, its rate of change gets smaller too, so the curve drops steeply at first and then flattens. From below zero it rises and flattens.", "This is **balancing feedback** (negative feedback), because the system acts against the discrepancy from equilibrium. The motion reduces the very displacement that causes it, and when x is 0 nothing drives it any more.", "A bigger number in the rule, such as minus 0.9 instead of minus 0.1, means a faster return. A smaller number means a slower return."], "end": "So without a mass there is no overshoot, only a return that slows as it gets close.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/006.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p6"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 09 · p3 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**A differential equation says how a system changes from the state it is in now, and together with a starting state it predicts the whole future.** The rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, with x starting at 100, gives a change of about minus 33, so the next value is about 67. The same rule on 67 gives a smaller step, and the value heads toward 0 while the curve flattens.", "sub": ["The same rule describes a spring, a cup of coffee and a learning system, because all three correct a gap, and the correction shrinks as the gap shrinks.", "Repeating the arithmetic step by step is Euler's method."], "end": "So knowing the rule of change and the starting point is enough to predict the time course.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture09/003.webp", "caption": "Lecture 09 · p3"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 04 · p18", "main": "**Learning and a cooling cup of coffee share the same shape of approach to equilibrium.** Hot coffee at 60, 80 or 100 degrees cools toward room temperature at 18, and iced coffee at 0, 5 or 10 degrees warms toward it. The change is fast when the gap is large and slows as the gap shrinks, so every cup ends at the same temperature from either side.", "sub": ["The SGD estimate behaves the same way from 130 above or 10 below, except that its path is noisy, because each observation is random."], "end": "So a correction that opposes a discrepancy is the shared organization of both.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture04/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 04 · p18"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p16", "main": "**An economy oscillates too, with output rising to a peak, falling into a recession, reaching a trough and recovering.** The swings happen around a trend line that slopes upward, so repeated ups and downs can go together with overall growth.", "sub": ["Oscillation does not need the long-term level to stay flat."], "end": "So boom and bust is one more example of back and forth change.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/016.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p16"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p18 · Lecture 05–06 · p19", "main": "**Music and even atoms are described with the same vibration ideas.** A musical tone is a vibration. In the quantum description of a magnesium atom, the electrons occupy wave modes rather than tracing little orbits, and the filled modes add up to the electron density.", "sub": ["An atom is not a harmonic oscillator, but it is described with wave modes, which is the same mathematics."], "end": "So the same ideas reach from a spring to a musical note to an atom.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/018.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p18"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 17", "q": "If x dot equals minus 0.1 times x describes a system, and x starts negative, what will happen? What happens if the number minus 0.1 becomes minus 0.9?", "a": "The position x rises toward 0 and stops there. This is because x is negative, so minus 0.1 times x is positive, so x goes up. As x gets closer to 0 the change gets smaller, so it rises fast at first and then more and more slowly, and it never crosses 0. With minus 0.9 the same thing happens much faster, because each step changes x by 90 percent of the gap instead of 10 percent. There is no mass in this system, so there is no overshoot and no oscillation."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A system follows the rule that the change in x is minus 0.1 times x, and x starts positive. What happens? What if it starts at exactly 0?", "a": "If x starts positive it falls toward 0 and stops there. This is because minus 0.1 times a positive x is negative, so x goes down, and the step gets smaller as x gets smaller, so it never crosses 0.\n\nIf x starts at exactly 0, nothing happens, because minus 0.1 times 0 is 0, so there is no change. The system is already at equilibrium."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why does a spring with no mass never overshoot its resting position?", "a": "Because the only thing driving the motion is the discrepancy from equilibrium. As the spring gets close to its resting position the discrepancy shrinks, so the motion shrinks, and at the resting position the motion is zero. There is no mass to carry the motion past that point. With a mass, the mass arrives moving and keeps moving, and that is what makes the overshoot."}]}, {"id": "m1-mass-stiffness", "title": "Mass, stiffness and frequency, from springs to vocal folds", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 07 · p13 · Lecture 08 · p4", "main": "**More mass lowers the frequency of a mass-spring system, and more stiffness raises it.** **Perturbation theory** asks how the behavior changes when one setting of the system is changed. When the mass gets heavier, the same spring has more to slow down and turn around, so each cycle takes longer and fewer cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes down.", "sub": ["When the spring gets stiffer, it pulls the mass back more strongly for the same displacement, so the turns happen sooner and more cycles fit into a second, so the frequency goes up.", "Mass and stiffness therefore have opposite effects on frequency."], "end": "So to predict a frequency change, ask whether mass or stiffness changed, and in which direction.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/013.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p13"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p8", "main": "**The vocal folds are small muscles, about 1 centimeter long and 1 millimeter high, and their mass and stiffness set the pitch of the voice.** About ten other muscles act on them through the surrounding cartilages. Stretching the folds makes them stiffer, so they vibrate faster and the pitch goes up. Bunching them makes them effectively heavier, so they vibrate more slowly and the pitch goes down.", "sub": ["The rate of vibration is called **F0**, the fundamental frequency. Changing it is how we sing, ask a question, show surprise or doubt, and in tone languages tell one word from another.", "The folds vibrate because air pressure from the lungs builds up below the closed folds and pushes them apart, the fast air between them sucks them back together (the Bernoulli force, like a shower curtain pulled inward), pressure builds again, and this repeats hundreds of times a second."], "end": "So the vocal folds are a mass-spring system made of tissue, and the air above them is a separate vibrating system.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/008.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p8"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Exam 1 · 24", "q": "If I raise the stiffness of a mass-spring system, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall? And if I raise the mass, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall?", "a": "Raising the stiffness makes the frequency rise, because a stiffer spring pulls the mass back faster, so each cycle takes less time. Raising the mass makes the frequency fall, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around, so each cycle takes more time."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "A singer stretches the vocal folds tighter. What happens to the pitch, and why?", "a": "The pitch goes up. This is because stretching the folds makes them stiffer, and a stiffer system springs back faster, so it vibrates more times per second. More vibrations per second is a higher fundamental frequency, which is heard as a higher pitch."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "If the mass of a mass-spring system is doubled and the stiffness is also doubled, what can you say about the frequency?", "a": "The two changes work against each other. Doubling the mass lowers the frequency, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around. Doubling the stiffness raises it, because a stiffer spring pulls back harder. For a single mass on a single spring the two effects cancel, so the frequency stays the same."}]}, {"id": "m1-air", "title": "Air vibration: compression, rarefaction and frequency", "lectures": "Lectures 01, 05–06 and 07", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 01 · p4", "main": "**The air between the tongue and the walls of the vocal tract can vibrate, and that vibration is the sound of speech.** The curved vocal tract can be straightened into a tube to understand the motion of its air. In the tube the rows of air particles move back and forth, so the air is more crowded in some places and less crowded in others.", "sub": ["Moving the tongue changes the shape of the air space, which is **articulatory phonetics**. The sound the vibrating air makes is **acoustic phonetics**."], "end": "So the visible space above the tongue and the physical vibration that carries sound are the same thing seen two ways.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture01/004.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/1b8ee778658fd124057c.mp4", "box": [33.28, 42.3, 67.11, 22.08], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/1b8ee778658fd124057c.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/1b8ee778658fd124057c.mp4", "box": [33.28, 42.3, 67.11, 22.08], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/1b8ee778658fd124057c.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 01 · p4 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p10 · Lecture 07 · p5", "main": "**A vibration in air is a repeated change between compression and rarefaction.** A slice of air keeps the same particles the whole time. In **compression** the particles move closer together and the pressure is high. In **rarefaction** they move farther apart and the pressure is low. The particles do not change size or number, only their spacing.", "sub": ["At equilibrium the spacing is 100 percent. In the compressed phase it drops to about 60 percent, and in the spread-out phase it rises to about 140 percent, exaggerated so it can be seen.", "The slice contracts, expands back through equilibrium, reaches rarefaction, and contracts again, over and over."], "end": "So sound in air is particles swinging closer and farther apart around their resting spacing, like a mass on a spring.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/010.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/e8fcd6445835f8428280.mp4", "box": [22.55, 24.65, 50.92, 67.89], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e8fcd6445835f8428280.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/e8fcd6445835f8428280.mp4", "box": [22.55, 24.65, 50.92, 67.89], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/e8fcd6445835f8428280.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p10 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p11 · Lecture 07 · p9", "main": "**The expansion of the air slice follows a cosine, and one full cycle is one round trip from spread out to compressed and back.** At the start the value is plus 1 and the particles are farthest apart. The value falls through 0, the equilibrium spacing, to minus 1, the closest spacing, and rises back to plus 1. Three cycles fit in the graph.", "sub": ["The moving point on the curve shows the spacing at that moment. The graph is a record of expansion over time, not a path that the particles travel along."], "end": "So positive values mean rarefaction, negative values mean compression, and the curve repeats.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/011.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.mp4", "box": [16.19, 21.29, 63.45, 63.45], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.mp4", "box": [16.19, 21.29, 63.45, 63.45], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/26ab41923fa45409a2ac.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p11", "main": "**Frequency is how many complete cycles fit into a fixed amount of time, measured in cycles per second, or hertz.** Three air slices swing by the same amount. One completes 3 cycles in 12 seconds, one completes 6, and one completes 9. The one with 9 cycles has three times the frequency of the one with 3.", "sub": ["Equal swings do not mean equal frequencies. The particles can spread and compress by the same amount at very different rates."], "end": "So frequency is about how fast the cycle repeats, not how big the swing is.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/011.webp", "videos": [{"video": "assets/original/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.mp4", "box": [7.62, 9.19, 87.2, 87.2], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.jpg"}], "video": "assets/original/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.mp4", "box": [7.62, 9.19, 87.2, 87.2], "poster": "assets/exam2/frames/4aec6bc2f3b2d0a8f721.jpg", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p11 · with the video played on the slide"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 05–06 · p15", "main": "**Speaking involves two different vibrating things, the vocal folds and the air in the vocal tract.** The folds are moving tissue. The air is changing compression and rarefaction. An explanation of speech has to account for both.", "end": "So the voice has a source, the folds, and a vibrating air column above it.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture05-06/015.webp", "caption": "Lecture 05–06 · p15"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What is the difference between compression and rarefaction, and what stays the same during both?", "a": "In compression the particles of air move closer together and the pressure goes up. In rarefaction they move farther apart and the pressure goes down. What stays the same is the particles themselves. The slice keeps the same particles, with the same size and number, and only their spacing changes."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two vibrations have the same amplitude, but one completes 4 cycles in 12 seconds and the other completes 8. Which has the higher frequency, and by how much?", "a": "The one with 8 cycles has the higher frequency, twice as high. This is because frequency is the number of cycles in a fixed time, and 8 cycles in 12 seconds is twice 4 cycles in 12 seconds. The amplitude, how far the particles spread and compress, does not change the frequency."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Why is the cosine graph of the air slice not a drawing of where the particles go?", "a": "The graph shows how spread out the slice is over time. The height of the curve is the spacing at that moment, plus 1 for farthest apart and minus 1 for closest together. The particles themselves only move back and forth along the tube. They do not travel along a wavy path."}]}, {"id": "m1-hearing", "title": "Hearing: the cochlea and the basilar membrane", "lectures": "Lectures 07 and 08", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p5 · Lecture 08 · p6", "main": "**Sound travels from the air into the ear and reaches the cochlea, the coiled structure of the inner ear that sends frequency information to the brain.** The vibration moves the eardrum (the tympanic membrane), passes through three small bones, the malleus, incus and stapes, and enters the cochlea.", "sub": ["Inside the cochlea runs the **basilar membrane**, a long strip from the entrance to the far end at the helicotrema. Thinking of it uncoiled makes it easier to see how different places along one strip can respond differently.", "The word to memorize is cochlea. The other labels on the diagram show where things are and do not need to be memorized."], "end": "So the path is air, eardrum, three bones, cochlea, basilar membrane.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/005.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p5"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 07 · p14 · Lecture 08 · p7", "main": "**The basilar membrane sorts a sound into its frequencies because its mass and stiffness change along its length.** The wide end is heavy and loose, so it has a lot of mass and little stiffness, and both of those favor low frequencies. The narrow end is light and stiff, and both of those favor high frequencies. Places in between respond to frequencies in between.", "sub": ["Extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. Extra stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. That is the same rule as for a mass on a spring.", "Nerve fibers leave every place along the membrane, so which fibers are active tells the brain where the vibration is strongest, and that tells the brain which frequencies are present. Georg von Békésy won the 1961 Nobel Prize for this work."], "end": "So a mixture of frequencies makes several places vibrate at once, and the brain reads the mixture off the pattern of active nerves.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture07/014.webp", "caption": "Lecture 07 · p14"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Review 3", "q": "What is the physical principle behind how your auditory system hears different frequencies? Describe the basic physics of the basilar membrane to show how the system works.", "a": "The principle is that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The basilar membrane (the long strip inside the cochlea) is heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other end. The heavy, loose end vibrates most for low frequencies, because extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. The light, stiff end vibrates most for high frequencies, because stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. Each place along the membrane is connected to its own neurons. So which neurons fire tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells the brain which frequencies are in the sound."}, {"source": "Exam 1 · 3", "q": "One side of the basilar membrane is heavy and not stiff, while the other side is light and very stiff. How do these facts enable hearing?", "a": "Frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. So the heavy, non-stiff end of the membrane vibrates at low frequencies, and the light, stiff end vibrates at high frequencies. Each part of the membrane is hooked to neurons. Those neurons tell the brain which frequencies were heard, based on which location is vibrating."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Which end of the basilar membrane responds to a high-pitched sound, and why?", "a": "The light, stiff end responds to a high-pitched sound. This is because a high pitch is a high frequency, and a part with little mass is easy to move while a stiff part springs back fast, so that part vibrates quickly. The heavy, loose end vibrates slowly, so it responds to low frequencies instead."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "How does the brain know which frequencies are in a sound, if all it gets is nerve signals?", "a": "Each place along the basilar membrane is connected to its own nerve fibers, and each place vibrates most for one frequency. So the set of nerve fibers that is active tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells it which frequencies are present. The brain reads frequency from location."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "What do the basilar membrane and a mass-spring system have in common?", "a": "Both follow the rule that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The membrane is like a row of mass-spring systems, heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other, so each place has its own natural frequency. That is what lets it separate a sound into its frequencies."}]}, {"id": "m1-euler", "title": "Euler's method: arithmetic that approximates continuous change", "lectures": "Lectures 08 and 09", "blocks": [{"ref": "Lecture 08 · p11 · Lecture 09 · p4", "main": "**Euler's method turns a rule about rates of change into a sequence of numbers by taking small time steps.** The new value is the old value plus the rate of change times the size of the time step. Repeating that arithmetic gives an approximate record of how the system moves through time.", "sub": ["The result is approximate, so the size of the time step and the order of the updates both affect what comes out.", "For the rule that the change in x is minus one third of x, starting at 100 gives about 67, then a smaller step, and so on toward 0."], "end": "So a computer simulates a dynamical system by adding up many small changes.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/011.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p11"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p10", "main": "**Explicit Euler updates position and velocity from the old pair, and with a big time step its swings grow.** With a time step of 0.5, starting at x equal to 1 and v equal to 0, the new position is the old velocity times 0.5 plus the old position, and the new velocity is minus the old position times 0.5 plus the old velocity. The first step gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second gives x of 0.75 and v of minus 1.", "sub": ["The values keep changing sign, but each swing gets bigger, reaching past 2 and past 3 within twelve steps.", "That growth comes from the approximation with this time step, not from the real oscillator gaining energy."], "end": "So the same physics simulated two ways can look different, and this way looks like it is blowing up.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/010.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p10"}}, {"ref": "Lecture 08 · p9", "main": "**Position-first Euler updates the position first and then uses the new position to update the velocity, and its swings stay the same size.** The first step again gives x of 1 and v of minus 0.5. The second step gives x of 0.75, and then the velocity uses that new 0.75, so v is minus 0.875 instead of minus 1.", "sub": ["The sequence stays between about minus 1 and plus 1, crossing zero again and again, which is what the real oscillator does.", "The order matters because the second calculation receives information from the first."], "end": "So the way the arithmetic is arranged changes the answer, and the position-first order keeps the oscillation bounded.", "media": {"image": "assets/slides/lecture08/009.webp", "caption": "Lecture 08 · p9"}}], "questions": [{"source": "Predicted", "q": "What does Euler's method do, and why is its answer only approximate?", "a": "Euler's method takes a rule for how fast something changes and turns it into a list of values, by adding the rate of change times a small time step to the current value, over and over. It is only approximate because the rate is treated as constant during each step, when really it keeps changing. A smaller time step makes the approximation closer."}, {"source": "Predicted", "q": "Two simulations of the same mass-spring system use the same time step, but one keeps growing and the other stays bounded. What is the difference?", "a": "The difference is the order of the updates. The simulation that grows is explicit Euler, which computes the new position and the new velocity both from the old pair. The one that stays bounded updates the position first and then uses that new position to update the velocity. The physics is the same, so the growth is a mistake of the arithmetic, not a property of the oscillator."}]}]}, "review": {"title": "Exam 1 review guide", "lead": "The 20 questions from the Exam 1 review sheet and the 25 questions from Exam 1 itself, each with its answer written in plain steps, the answer first and then the reason.", "note": "The review sheet and the Exam 1 key are the professor's. The answers here say what the key says, in full sentences, with the middle steps filled in from the slides.", "sets": [{"id": "review", "title": "Exam 1 review sheet · 20 questions", "about": "Handed out before Exam 1 and answered in class on September 29. About 15 of the 25 exam questions came from this sheet with one thing changed.", "prefix": "Review", "items": [{"number": 1, "question": "What is the similarity between m in mad and b in bad, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "answer": "Both m and b are voiced, and both are made at the lips, so they share voicing and place of articulation. The difference is manner (how the air is let out). For m the velum (the soft back part of the roof of the mouth) is lowered, so the air comes out through the nose, and m is a nasal stop. For b the velum is raised, so the air is blocked completely at the lips and then released through the mouth, and b is an oral stop (a plosive).", "source": "Review 1"}, {"number": 2, "question": "What is the role of the learning rate in stochastic gradient descent and the role of stiffness in a viscous springy system? What happens when the learning rate is high versus low, and when stiffness is high versus low?", "answer": "The learning rate determines how big a fraction of each mistake gets corrected, and stiffness determines how strongly the spring pulls back toward equilibrium (its resting position). With a high learning rate, each correction is large, so the estimate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, so it is less accurate. With a low learning rate, each correction is small, so the estimate moves slowly but settles steadily, so it is more accurate but slow. With high stiffness, the spring pulls back hard, so it returns to equilibrium fast. With low stiffness, it pulls back weakly, so it returns slowly. In both systems a bigger setting means a faster response.", "source": "Review 2"}, {"number": 3, "question": "What is the physical principle behind how your auditory system hears different frequencies? Describe the basic physics of the basilar membrane to show how the system works.", "answer": "The principle is that frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. The basilar membrane (the long strip inside the cochlea) is heavy and loose at one end and light and stiff at the other end. The heavy, loose end vibrates most for low frequencies, because extra mass makes a part harder to move, so it vibrates more slowly. The light, stiff end vibrates most for high frequencies, because stiffness makes a part spring back faster, so it vibrates more quickly. Each place along the membrane is connected to its own neurons. So which neurons fire tells the brain which places are vibrating, and that tells the brain which frequencies are in the sound.", "source": "Review 3"}, {"number": 4, "question": "Two temperature control systems are set to 70. In the first, the thermostat is right next to the heating and cooling unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, the temperature drops to 70, and the thermostat detects that and turns the unit off. Below 70, the heat turns on until 70 and then stops. In the second, the thermostat is far from the unit. When the temperature is above 70, the air conditioning turns on, but the thermostat does not detect 70, so the temperature keeps dropping to 60. Then the heat turns on, the temperature rises past 70 to 80 before the thermostat notices, and so on. Which system is like a spring by itself, and which is like a mass-spring system?", "answer": "The first system, with the thermostat next to the unit, is like a spring by itself. This is because it moves toward its goal of 70 and stops there, the way a spring alone returns to equilibrium and stays. The second system, with the far thermostat, is like a mass-spring system. This is because it keeps going past 70, to 60 and then to 80, and swings back and forth around the goal. A moving mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so a mass-spring system overshoots and oscillates in the same way. The delay in detecting the temperature plays the part of the mass.", "source": "Review 4"}, {"number": 5, "question": "Why is the exhaustive algorithm so inefficient for solving the shortest path problem?", "answer": "The exhaustive algorithm is inefficient because it works out the total cost of every complete path separately. The paths overlap, so the same additions get done over and over. Every time a stage is added, the number of complete paths multiplies, so the work grows very fast. It does find the true shortest path, but for a big problem it takes far too long.", "source": "Review 5"}, {"number": 6, "question": "What are the different ways we distinguish between the consonants at the beginning of the words Pam, Bam, Dam, Tam, Kam and Gam?", "answer": "All six are oral stops, so they are distinguished by place of articulation and by voicing. Place is where the air is blocked. The p and b are made at the lips (labial), the t and d at the alveolar ridge behind the upper teeth (alveolar), and the k and g with the back of the tongue at the velum (velar). Voicing is whether the vocal folds vibrate. The p, t and k are voiceless, and the b, d and g are voiced. So each sound is one place combined with one voicing.", "source": "Review 6"}, {"number": 7, "question": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, and how it is used in the GPT system.", "answer": "Closure is the tendency to complete a familiar but unfinished pattern. A broken circle is still seen as a circle, and three black circles with wedges cut out are seen as a triangle, because the mind fills in what is missing. GPT uses the same idea as its way of learning, which is called the cloze task. A sentence is taken, part of it is hidden, and the network has to guess the missing word. If the guess is wrong, the weights are changed a little. After billions of these completions the network ends up knowing the language.", "source": "Review 7"}, {"number": 8, "question": "How is knowledge represented in neural networks?", "answer": "Knowledge is represented in the weights, which are the numbers on the connections between neurons. The weights determine how the input numbers get turned into the output probabilities. Changing the weights changes what the network knows, so learning means changing the weights. In the brain the same kind of knowledge sits in the synapses (the connections between neurons), and a large AI system can have trillions of weights.", "source": "Review 8"}, {"number": 9, "question": "What is the basic tradeoff that we see in both the shortest path problem and the stochastic gradient descent algorithm?", "answer": "The tradeoff is speed against accuracy. In the shortest path problem, the greedy algorithm is fast but can pick the wrong path, and the exhaustive algorithm always finds the right path but is slow. Dynamic programming balances the two. In stochastic gradient descent, a high learning rate reaches the right region fast but keeps jumping around it, and a low learning rate settles accurately but slowly.", "source": "Review 9"}, {"number": 10, "question": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I describe it this way.", "answer": "The system starts with completely random weights, so at first it does not know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, gets feedback on how wrong the guess was, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this many times it makes it, which means it ends up with the right knowledge.", "source": "Review 10"}, {"number": 11, "question": "What is the output of an LLM or a neural network in general, and how does it express a decision?", "answer": "The output is a probability distribution over the possible categories. That is a list with one number per category, where each number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to 1. The decision is the category with the highest probability. For an LLM the categories are the possible next words, so the output says how likely each word is to come next.", "source": "Review 11"}, {"number": 12, "question": "Is this a good output of a neural network, if there are 4 categories, [.4, 0, .5, .2]? Why or why not?", "answer": "No, it is not a good output. This is because the output has to be a probability distribution, so the numbers have to add up to 1. These numbers add up to 1.1. Each number is between 0 and 1, so that part is fine, but the total is wrong.", "source": "Review 12"}, {"number": 13, "question": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "answer": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at a combination of frequencies at the same time. This is because the tongue shape changes where the air in the vocal tract is constricted (narrowed), so different parts of the air have different mass and stiffness. Different mass and stiffness means different frequencies, and they all sound together. The combination is what makes one vowel sound different from another.", "source": "Review 13"}, {"number": 14, "question": "What are the 2 basic ways we distinguish vowels?", "answer": "Vowels are distinguished by tongue height and by tongue frontness. Height is whether the tongue is high or low in the mouth. Frontness is whether the raised part of the tongue is toward the front or the back of the mouth. For example, the vowel in dean is high and front, the vowel in dune is high and back, the vowel in Dan is low and front, and the vowel in Don is low and back.", "source": "Review 14"}, {"number": 15, "question": "Some people say that everything is a harmonic oscillator. Give 5 examples of oscillatory systems we discussed in class or in the Meadows book.", "answer": "A mass on a spring swinging back and forth. The air in the vocal tract vibrating during speech, with its particles moving closer together and farther apart. The vocal folds opening and closing hundreds of times a second. The estimate in stochastic gradient descent swinging around the average when the learning rate is high. The economy going through boom and bust around its growth trend. A car bouncing after it goes over a bump. In the Meadows book, a room temperature swinging above and below the setting when the thermostat is far from the heater, and a car dealership's inventory swinging up and down because orders arrive after a delay. Any five of these work.", "source": "Review 15"}, {"number": 16, "question": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If it starts with x negative and v equal to 0, will it oscillate? Say in a couple of sentences what will happen.", "answer": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium (x equal to 0). The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it goes past 0 and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. So the position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave.", "source": "Review 16"}, {"number": 17, "question": "If x dot equals minus 0.1 times x describes a system, and x starts negative, what will happen? What happens if the number minus 0.1 becomes minus 0.9?", "answer": "The position x rises toward 0 and stops there. This is because x is negative, so minus 0.1 times x is positive, so x goes up. As x gets closer to 0 the change gets smaller, so it rises fast at first and then more and more slowly, and it never crosses 0. With minus 0.9 the same thing happens much faster, because each step changes x by 90 percent of the gap instead of 10 percent. There is no mass in this system, so there is no overshoot and no oscillation.", "source": "Review 17"}, {"number": 18, "question": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula? Consider the case where the data point is above the current x and the case where it is below.", "answer": "The first minus makes the estimate move toward the data point whether the data point is above or below the current estimate. If the current estimate is above the data point, the difference in the parentheses is positive, so subtracting it makes the estimate go down, toward the data point. If the current estimate is below the data point, the difference is negative, so subtracting a negative number makes the estimate go up, again toward the data point. For example, with an estimate of 130 and a data point of 98, the difference is 32, so the estimate goes down. With an estimate of 94 and a data point of 98, the difference is minus 4, so the estimate goes up.", "source": "Review 18"}, {"number": 19, "question": "What is the math of forward propagation, from the signal input to a neural network all the way to a decision at the end? Describe it.", "answer": "The input signal is a vector, which is a list of numbers, such as pressure values from a sound wave. Each neuron in the first layer multiplies each input number by its own weight and adds the products up. That sum is the inner product, and it is the neuron's activation. The activations of that layer become the inputs to the next layer, where every neuron does the same multiply-and-add with its own weights. This repeats layer by layer until the last layer. The last layer turns its numbers into a probability distribution over the categories, so the numbers are between 0 and 1 and add up to 1. The decision is the category with the largest probability.", "source": "Review 19"}, {"number": 20, "question": "The decision by a neural network is [.1, .1, .1, .2, .5], but the true category is the one in the second position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, as a probability distribution, what the true answer is?", "answer": "The true answer is written as [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]. This is because the correct category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and every other category gets 0. The 1 goes in the second position because that is where the true category is. The list still adds up to 1, so it is a probability distribution, and the network can compare its own output with it to find its mistake.", "source": "Review 20"}]}, {"id": "exam", "title": "Exam 1 · the 25 questions and the key", "about": "Given on October 1, 2026. Every question was worth the same number of points, and the space after each question showed how many sentences to write.", "prefix": "Exam 1 ·", "items": [{"number": 1, "question": "Which of these four could be the probability distribution decision of a neural network? (a) [0, 0, 0, 0], (b) [0, .4, 0, .6], (c) [-.5, 0, .5, 0], (d) [.3, .3, .4, .5].", "answer": "Only (b) is possible. This is because the numbers in a probability distribution have to be between 0 and 1 and have to add up to 1. List (a) adds up to 0. List (c) has a negative number. List (d) adds up to 1.5. List (b) has numbers between 0 and 1 that add up to 1.", "source": "Exam 1 · 1"}, {"number": 2, "question": "Name three ways in which we can distinguish consonants from each other.", "answer": "Any three of these four. Voicing, which is whether the vocal folds vibrate. Place of articulation, which is where in the mouth the sound is made, such as labial, dental, alveolar, postalveolar or velar. Manner of articulation, which is how the air is constricted, such as fricative, oral stop or nasal stop. Nasality, which is whether the velum is lowered so that the air escapes through the nose.", "source": "Exam 1 · 2"}, {"number": 3, "question": "One side of the basilar membrane is heavy and not stiff, while the other side is light and very stiff. How do these facts enable hearing?", "answer": "Frequency goes up with stiffness and down with mass. So the heavy, non-stiff end of the membrane vibrates at low frequencies, and the light, stiff end vibrates at high frequencies. Each part of the membrane is hooked to neurons. Those neurons tell the brain which frequencies were heard, based on which location is vibrating.", "source": "Exam 1 · 3"}, {"number": 4, "question": "Describe how dynamic programming works in terms of the size of problems, and problems containing other problems.", "answer": "You break the big problem into smaller subproblems, and keep breaking those down until you reach small problems that are trivial to solve. You solve those smallest problems first. Then you use their answers to solve the larger problems that contain them, and keep going up until you reach the hardest problem, which is the original one.", "source": "Exam 1 · 4"}, {"number": 5, "question": "Describe why dynamic programming is more efficient than the exhaustive algorithm.", "answer": "The paths in a shortest path problem overlap. The exhaustive algorithm solves every path separately, so it redoes the same additions over and over. Dynamic programming solves only the non-overlapping parts. It finds the best solution from each state only once and reuses it for every path through that state, so it never repeats work.", "source": "Exam 1 · 5"}, {"number": 6, "question": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the tongue tip is up at the teeth. In (b) the lower lip is against the upper teeth. Which of these is f as in family, and which is th as in thing?", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q06.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)", "answer": "The f is (b), and the th is (a). This is because f is a labial sound, made with the lower lip against the upper teeth, which is what (b) shows. The th is a dental sound, made with the tongue tip up at the teeth, which is what (a) shows.", "source": "Exam 1 · 6"}, {"number": 7, "question": "Describe how the stochastic gradient descent algorithm works in your own words.", "answer": "We want to estimate a quantity from some existing data, like the average of a list of numbers. We start with a random estimate. Then we pick a random data point and compute the loss, which is the difference between our guess and that data point. We subtract a small fraction of the loss from the guess, and that fraction is the learning rate. That gives a new, slightly better guess. We repeat this with new random data points over and over, and the guess gets closer and closer to the real answer.", "source": "Exam 1 · 7"}, {"number": 8, "question": "What is the similarity between n in neck and d in deck, and what is the difference between those sounds?", "answer": "Both are voiced alveolar stops, so they share voicing, place and a complete closure. The difference is that n is a nasal stop, so the velum is lowered and the air comes out through the nose. The d is an oral stop, so the air is blocked completely and then released through the mouth.", "source": "Exam 1 · 8"}, {"number": 9, "question": "What is the similarity and the difference between the vowels in mean and moon?", "answer": "Both are high vowels, so the tongue is high in the mouth for both. The difference is that the vowel in mean is a front vowel, and the vowel in moon is a back vowel. For moon the lips are also rounded.", "source": "Exam 1 · 9"}, {"number": 10, "question": "Imagine a stochastic gradient descent algorithm with a very low learning rate, and a springy system with no mass and a low stiffness. (a) Describe how the SGD estimate and the spring's position evolve with time. (b) How would the evolution in time differ if the learning rate and the stiffness increased? For SGD, ignore accuracy and oscillation.", "answer": "Part (a). Both move toward their goal slowly. With a low learning rate, the SGD estimate only takes a small correction toward the average each time, so it takes many steps to get there. With low stiffness, the spring only pulls weakly, so it drifts back to its equilibrium position slowly.\n\nPart (b). Both would get there faster. A higher learning rate makes bigger corrections toward the average, so the estimate arrives in fewer steps. A stiffer spring pulls harder, so it snaps back to equilibrium more quickly.", "source": "Exam 1 · 10"}, {"number": 11, "question": "Describe the psychological principle of closure, illustrated by the figure of three black circles with wedges cut out, and how it is used in the GPT system as a principle for learning.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q11.png", "alt": "Three black circles with wedges cut out that make a triangle appear", "answer": "Humans are very good at filling in missing information using what we already know. In the figure no triangle is actually drawn, but we see one anyway because the brain completes the shape. GPT learns the same way, with the cloze task. It takes a sentence, pretends that part of the sentence is missing, and asks the network to complete what is missing. If the guess is wrong, the weights get adjusted. After billions of these completions the system ends up knowing the language.", "source": "Exam 1 · 11"}, {"number": 12, "question": "In the shortest path problem shown, what is the greedy forward solution? Write out the numbers with plus signs.", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow", "answer": "The greedy forward solution is 3 plus 3 plus 4 plus 2, which is 12, along the path A to B to E to F to H. This is because greedy takes the cheapest arrow out of each state as it goes. From A the cheapest arrow is 3 to B. From B it is 3 to E. From E it is 4 to F. From F the only arrow is 2 to H. The greedy total is 12, and it is not the best path. The cheapest complete path is A to C to D to G to H, which costs 5 plus 2 plus 1 plus 3, which is 11.", "source": "Exam 1 · 12"}, {"number": 13, "question": "In the same shortest path problem, we call the best cost from any state x to state H V x H. Imagine we have already solved for V D H and V E H. How can I calculate V C H?", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q12.png", "alt": "A five-stage shortest path graph from A to H with costs on every arrow", "answer": "V C H is the smaller of two sums, 2 plus V D H and 1 plus V E H. This is because from C you can only go to D, which costs 2, or to E, which costs 1. Each sum adds the cost of that first step to the best cost of finishing from the state you land in. Taking the minimum is the important part, because the best cost from C is the cheaper of the two complete ways of finishing.", "source": "Exam 1 · 13"}, {"number": 14, "question": "In terms of accuracy and speed in solving shortest path problems, how do the greedy and exhaustive algorithms compare? And what algorithm do we use if we want to balance those two factors?", "answer": "Greedy is fast but inaccurate, because it only looks at the next arrow and can miss a cheaper complete path. Exhaustive is accurate but slow, because it works out every complete path. To balance the two we use dynamic programming.", "source": "Exam 1 · 14"}, {"number": 15, "question": "What is my favorite popular way of describing how learning in AI works? Fake it till you make it. Describe why I put it this way.", "answer": "The system starts out with completely random weights, so it does not actually know anything. It is just faking it. It makes a guess, receives feedback, and changes its weights a little so that it does better next time. After repeating this a lot of times, it makes it, which means it has the right knowledge.", "source": "Exam 1 · 15"}, {"number": 16, "question": "In each vowel, does the air vibrate at a single frequency? Yes or no?", "answer": "No. The air in a vowel vibrates at several frequencies at once, because the tongue shape gives different parts of the air in the vocal tract different mass and stiffness, so they vibrate at different frequencies together.", "source": "Exam 1 · 16"}, {"number": 17, "question": "What is the goal of a spring, if it is extended or squeezed? What is the primary physical property of a mass that is already moving?", "answer": "The goal of a spring is to go back to equilibrium, its resting position. The primary property of a mass that is already moving is that it keeps on moving. Together these two facts are why a mass on a spring oscillates, because the spring keeps pulling the mass back and the mass keeps going past the middle.", "source": "Exam 1 · 17"}, {"number": 18, "question": "A system is described by x dot equals v and v dot equals minus k over m times x. If x starts as negative and v is 0, will the system oscillate? How will the position of the mass evolve with time?", "answer": "Yes, it oscillates. The position x starts out negative, so the spring is squeezed and pushes the mass toward equilibrium at x equal to 0. The mass speeds up and reaches 0, but a moving mass keeps moving, so it overshoots and stretches the spring on the positive side. The spring then pulls it back, it overshoots again, and this repeats. The position swings back and forth, tracing out a cosine-shaped wave.", "source": "Exam 1 · 18"}, {"number": 19, "question": "Two vocal tract images are shown. In (a) the back of the tongue is raised. In (b) the lips are closed and the velum is lowered. Which of these is k as in Ken, and which is m as in men?", "img": "assets/module1/exam1-q19.png", "alt": "Two vocal tract images labelled (a) and (b)", "answer": "The k is (a), and the m is (b). This is because k is a velar sound, made with the back of the tongue raised up to the velum, which is what (a) shows. The m is a labial nasal, made with the lips closed and the velum lowered so that air goes through the nose, which is what (b) shows.", "source": "Exam 1 · 19"}, {"number": 20, "question": "In SGD for averaging a list, the formula for the new estimate of the average x is: new x equals current x minus the learning rate times (current x minus the data point d). What is the purpose of the first minus in the formula?", "answer": "The minus makes the estimate move toward the data point, whether the data point is above or below the estimate. If the estimate is above the data point, the loss is positive, so subtracting it pushes the estimate down. If the estimate is below the data point, the loss is negative, so subtracting it pushes the estimate up.", "source": "Exam 1 · 20"}, {"number": 21, "question": "Imagine a neural network with many neurons in many layers. One neuron, call it y, in layer 4 is connected to 5 neurons from the previous layer. Every neuron is connected to all the neurons in the previous layer. How many weights does y have?", "answer": "Neuron y has 5 weights. This is because there is one weight on each connection coming into a neuron, and y has 5 connections coming in, one from each of the 5 neurons in the previous layer.", "source": "Exam 1 · 21"}, {"number": 22, "question": "Continuing from the previous question, y's weights are w1, w2, w3, w4 and w5, and the activations of the 5 neurons feeding it are x1, x2, x3, x4 and x5. How do we write the activation of neuron y?", "answer": "The activation of y is w1 times x1, plus w2 times x2, plus w3 times x3, plus w4 times x4, plus w5 times x5. This is because each incoming activation is multiplied by its own weight, and then all five products are added up. That sum is the inner product.", "source": "Exam 1 · 22"}, {"number": 23, "question": "The decision made by a neural network is [.4, .1, .1, .3, .1], but the true category is the one in the last position. Using Python list notation, how do we express to the network, in a probability distribution format, what the true answer is?", "answer": "The true answer is [0, 0, 0, 0, 1]. This is because the true category gets all of the probability, which is 1, and the other four categories get 0. The 1 goes in the last position because that is where the true category is.", "source": "Exam 1 · 23"}, {"number": 24, "question": "If I raise the stiffness of a mass-spring system, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall? And if I raise the mass, does the frequency of vibration rise or fall?", "answer": "Raising the stiffness makes the frequency rise, because a stiffer spring pulls the mass back faster, so each cycle takes less time. Raising the mass makes the frequency fall, because a heavier mass is harder to move and turn around, so each cycle takes more time.", "source": "Exam 1 · 24"}, {"number": 25, "question": "There are systems all around us. True or false?", "answer": "True. A system is a set of elements with relationships between them that together serve a goal, and that describes speech, an octopus, a thermostat, a population, an economy and a neural network.", "source": "Exam 1 · 25"}]}]}};
